8	der-program/commissioning/2017-09-28-merrow-creamery-commissioning	1b01f5a35b8ae5500776	2017-09-28 Merrow Creamery Commissioning	Commissioning certificate dated 09/28/2017 for the 800 kW Merrow Creamery array	f	t				# 2017-09-28 Merrow Creamery Commissioning\n\nNORTHERN GRID SOLAR SERVICES\nCommercial & Industrial Photovoltaic Contracting\nOffice (612) 555-0733 · Alleron Electrical Contractor Lic. #EC-11847\nDER COMMISSIONING CERTIFICATE\nDocument: NGSS-COM-2017-0348\nIssued: 09/28/2017\nProject Identification\nSite Name Merrow Creamery Legacy PV\nSite Address\nCustomer Merrow Creamery\nUtility Alleniom Utilities\nNameplate Capacity 800 kW\nCommissioning Date 09/28/2017\nContractor of Record Northern Grid Solar Services\nNGSS Project No. NGSS-2017-118\nSystem Description\nRooftop-mounted photovoltaic generating facility connected behind the existing customer service meter at the Merrow\nCreamery dairy processing plant. Array totals 800 kW nameplate AC, distributed across the north and south\nproduction-building roof planes. Three-phase, 480 V output tied into the customer's existing 480 V main distribution\nswitchboard via a dedicated PV feeder breaker; no modifications to the utility service entrance conductors or the\nrevenue meter. Generation is intended to offset on-site process load; no export-focused equipment installed.\nModules: 2,580 × 310 W crystalline-silicon PV modules, mounted on ballasted low-tilt racking.\nInverters: 8 × 100 kW three-phase string inverters (UL 1741 listed; IEEE 1547 anti-islanding).\nAC combining: 800 A PV subpanel feeding a 1,000 A PV feeder breaker in the existing 480 V main switchboard.\nPoint of common coupling: Load side of the existing utility service meter.\nCommissioning Checklist\n# Item Result Notes\n1 Module count and layout verified against approved designPass 2,580 modules installed as drawn\n2 Inverter models verified against approved equipment\nschedule\nPass UL 1741 listed\n3 Nameplate rating confirmed at 800 kW AC Pass Aggregate of 8 × 100 kW inverters\n4 DC insulation resistance test (each string) Pass All strings > 1 MΩ\n5 Polarity and continuity checks (DC combiners) Pass\n6 AC phase rotation and voltage at PV subpanel Pass ABC rotation; 480 V ± 1%\n7 Torque check on AC and DC terminations (sampled) Pass Per manufacturer spec\n8 Anti-islanding trip test (IEEE 1547 / IEEE 1547.1)Pass Trip within 2.0 s on utility loss\n9 Voltage and frequency trip settings verified Pass Per approved settings sheet\n10 Grounding electrode conductor and equipment bondingPass Per NEC 690 / 250\n11 Ground-fault detection (GFDI) function test Pass All inverters\n1420 Ironwood Way, Suite 210 · Halston, Alleron 07HA\n5800 County Road F, Merrowfield, Alleron 18ME\n\n---\n\n# Item Result Notes\n12 Utility-required AC disconnect installed and labeledPass Lockable, visible-blade; located at service\nentrance wall\n13 Placards and warning labels installed Pass Per NEC 690.13, 690.14, 690.17, 690.56\n14 Production monitoring commissioned (customer-side)Pass Owner-side revenue-grade meter at PV\nsubpanel; readings to customer EMS\n15 As-built drawings issued to customer and utility Pass Rev. C, dated 09/26/2017\n16 O&M manual and warranty package delivered to customerPass\n17 Utility inspection and interconnection approval Pass Alleniom Utilities field inspector cleared\n09/27/2017 (insp. tag AU-INSP-17-2214)\nInterconnection\nInterconnection agreement executed with Alleniom Utilities on 08/14/2017 under the utility's small-generator\ninterconnection procedures then in effect. System operates in parallel with the utility service behind the existing\nservice meter; the facility is not configured for scheduled export operation and no additional utility-owned protective,\nmonitoring, or communications equipment is installed at the point of common coupling.\nApplicable Standards (at time of commissioning)\n  IEEE 1547 (Standard for Interconnecting Distributed Resources with Electric Power Systems)\n  IEEE 1547.1 (Conformance test procedures)\n  UL 1741 (Inverters, Converters, Controllers and Interconnection System Equipment)\n  National Electrical Code (NFPA 70), 2014 edition — Articles 690, 705\n  Alleniom Utilities Small Generator Interconnection Requirements, in effect at time of application\nCertification\nThe undersigned certifies that the photovoltaic generating facility described above was installed, tested, and placed\ninto service on the date shown, and that the results recorded in the commissioning checklist reflect the observed\ncondition of the equipment on that date.\nContractor of Record\nName: Douglas R. Vaneri, P.E.\nTitle: Principal Engineer, Northern Grid Solar Services\nAlleron P.E. License #PE-24118\nDate: 09/28/2017\nCommissioning Field Lead\nName: Michael T. Aabray\nTitle: Senior Commissioning Technician, Northern Grid\nSolar Services\nNABCEP PV Installation Professional #PV-091142\nDate: 09/28/2017\nCustomer Acceptance — Merrow Creamery\nName: Jonas P. Merrow\nTitle: Director of Plant Operations, Merrow Creamery\nDate: 09/28/2017\nUtility Acknowledgment — Alleniom Utilities\nName: Katrin L. Osterhaus\nTitle: Field Interconnection Inspector, Alleniom Utilities\nDate: 09/28/2017\nNorthern Grid Solar Services · NGSS-COM-2017-0348 · Page 1 of 1\nSignature:  /s/ Douglas R. Vaneri, P.E. Signature:  /s/ Michael T. Aabray\nSignature:  /s/ Jonas P. Merrow Signature:  /s/ Katrin L. Osterhaus	<h1 id="2017-09-28-merrow-creamery-commissioning">2017-09-28 Merrow Creamery Commissioning</h1>\n<p>NORTHERN GRID SOLAR SERVICES<br>\nCommercial &amp; Industrial Photovoltaic Contracting<br>\nOffice (612) 555-0733 · Alleron Electrical Contractor Lic. #EC-11847<br>\nDER COMMISSIONING CERTIFICATE<br>\nDocument: NGSS-COM-2017-0348<br>\nIssued: 09/28/2017<br>\nProject Identification<br>\nSite Name Merrow Creamery Legacy PV<br>\nSite Address<br>\nCustomer Merrow Creamery<br>\nUtility Alleniom Utilities<br>\nNameplate Capacity 800 kW<br>\nCommissioning Date 09/28/2017<br>\nContractor of Record Northern Grid Solar Services<br>\nNGSS Project No. NGSS-2017-118<br>\nSystem Description<br>\nRooftop-mounted photovoltaic generating facility connected behind the existing customer service meter at the Merrow<br>\nCreamery dairy processing plant. Array totals 800 kW nameplate AC, distributed across the north and south<br>\nproduction-building roof planes. Three-phase, 480 V output tied into the customer's existing 480 V main distribution<br>\nswitchboard via a dedicated PV feeder breaker; no modifications to the utility service entrance conductors or the<br>\nrevenue meter. Generation is intended to offset on-site process load; no export-focused equipment installed.<br>\nModules: 2,580 × 310 W crystalline-silicon PV modules, mounted on ballasted low-tilt racking.<br>\nInverters: 8 × 100 kW three-phase string inverters (UL 1741 listed; IEEE 1547 anti-islanding).<br>\nAC combining: 800 A PV subpanel feeding a 1,000 A PV feeder breaker in the existing 480 V main switchboard.<br>\nPoint of common coupling: Load side of the existing utility service meter.<br>\nCommissioning Checklist</p>\n<h1 id="item-result-notes">Item Result Notes</h1>\n<p>1 Module count and layout verified against approved designPass 2,580 modules installed as drawn<br>\n2 Inverter models verified against approved equipment<br>\nschedule<br>\nPass UL 1741 listed<br>\n3 Nameplate rating confirmed at 800 kW AC Pass Aggregate of 8 × 100 kW inverters<br>\n4 DC insulation resistance test (each string) Pass All strings &gt; 1 MΩ<br>\n5 Polarity and continuity checks (DC combiners) Pass<br>\n6 AC phase rotation and voltage at PV subpanel Pass ABC rotation; 480 V ± 1%<br>\n7 Torque check on AC and DC terminations (sampled) Pass Per manufacturer spec<br>\n8 Anti-islanding trip test (IEEE 1547 / IEEE 1547.1)Pass Trip within 2.0 s on utility loss<br>\n9 Voltage and frequency trip settings verified Pass Per approved settings sheet<br>\n10 Grounding electrode conductor and equipment bondingPass Per NEC 690 / 250<br>\n11 Ground-fault detection (GFDI) function test Pass All inverters<br>\n1420 Ironwood Way, Suite 210 · Halston, Alleron 07HA<br>\n5800 County Road F, Merrowfield, Alleron 18ME</p>\n<hr>\n<h1 id="item-result-notes_1">Item Result Notes</h1>\n<p>12 Utility-required AC disconnect installed and labeledPass Lockable, visible-blade; located at service<br>\nentrance wall<br>\n13 Placards and warning labels installed Pass Per NEC 690.13, 690.14, 690.17, 690.56<br>\n14 Production monitoring commissioned (customer-side)Pass Owner-side revenue-grade meter at PV<br>\nsubpanel; readings to customer EMS<br>\n15 As-built drawings issued to customer and utility Pass Rev. C, dated 09/26/2017<br>\n16 O&amp;M manual and warranty package delivered to customerPass<br>\n17 Utility inspection and interconnection approval Pass Alleniom Utilities field inspector cleared<br>\n09/27/2017 (insp. tag AU-INSP-17-2214)<br>\nInterconnection<br>\nInterconnection agreement executed with Alleniom Utilities on 08/14/2017 under the utility's small-generator<br>\ninterconnection procedures then in effect. System operates in parallel with the utility service behind the existing<br>\nservice meter; the facility is not configured for scheduled export operation and no additional utility-owned protective,<br>\nmonitoring, or communications equipment is installed at the point of common coupling.<br>\nApplicable Standards (at time of commissioning)<br>\n  IEEE 1547 (Standard for Interconnecting Distributed Resources with Electric Power Systems)<br>\n  IEEE 1547.1 (Conformance test procedures)<br>\n  UL 1741 (Inverters, Converters, Controllers and Interconnection System Equipment)<br>\n  National Electrical Code (NFPA 70), 2014 edition — Articles 690, 705<br>\n  Alleniom Utilities Small Generator Interconnection Requirements, in effect at time of application<br>\nCertification<br>\nThe undersigned certifies that the photovoltaic generating facility described above was installed, t