15	der-program/contracts/harkwell-ridge/2025-07-08-amendment-request	8151d4fabe1ca3dec5a7	2025-07-08 Amendment Request	Email requesting ISA capacity increase from 2.2 MW to 2.8 MW via addendum	f	t				# 2025-07-08 Amendment Request\n\n**From:** Corey Lindqvist <corey.lindqvist@hailbouysolar.com>\n**To:** der.program@alleniom.com\n**Date:** Tue, 08 Jul 2025 09:14:22 -0500\n**Subject:** Harkwell Ridge — ISA capacity update request\n**Cc:** Meera Vellanki <meera.vellanki@alleniom.com>\n\n---\n\nProgram Office,\n\nFollowing execution of the Interconnection Service Agreement on 07/03/2025 for Harkwell Ridge Solar (Application PXA-2024-0117) at 2.2 MW, Hailbouy Solar is writing to request that the executed ISA be updated to reflect a nameplate export capacity of 2.8 MW. Our preference — and, we believe, the only workable path given the project's construction and financing calendar — is to effect this change via a short addendum to the executed ISA.\n\nBackground and rationale:\n\n- The ISA as executed on 07/03/2025 fixes capacity at 2.2 MW on Feeder ALN-042. Post-execution offtake refinements and updated production modeling from our EPC support a revised design point of 2.8 MW at the same point of interconnection.\n\n- The equipment schedule and site plan are unchanged in configuration. The single-axis-tracker layout, module count per string, row geometry, inverter locations, MV collection topology, POI structure, and interconnection facilities scope on file with the DER Program Office are all as-approved. The delta is a firmware-level export setting and dc:ac ratio adjustment within the equipment already listed in the original application and reflected in the executed Interconnection Service Agreement.\n\n- Because the physical configuration and POI are unchanged, no re-study should be needed. The prior system impact and facilities study work performed under PXA-2024-0117 remains the applicable technical basis, and a short capacity-only addendum executed against the existing ISA is the appropriate instrument to memorialize the update.\n\nRequested action:\n\n1. Please prepare an addendum to ISA-Harkwell-Ridge revising the Article 2 nameplate export capacity from 2.2 MW to 2.8 MW, with all other terms of the executed ISA to remain in full force and effect.\n\n2. Please route the addendum for countersignature by end of week (07/11/2025). Our construction mobilization sequence, EPC commitment schedule, and tax-equity funding milestones are built around the 07/03/2025 execution and cannot tolerate slippage. Any process that reopens the study queue on this project is not viable for us on this timeline.\n\n3. If the Program Office needs a supplemental technical memo from our engineer of record confirming that the equipment schedule and site plan are unchanged in configuration, we can have that in your hands by close of business 07/09/2025. Please advise by return email if this would expedite the addendum.\n\nI would appreciate confirmation of receipt today and a target date for the addendum draft. Meera — copying you directly so Interconnection Studies has visibility; I understand the addendum sits with the contracts desk, but if there is anything on the studies side that needs a signoff to unblock the drafting please let me know today and we will accommodate.\n\nGiven the timing sensitivity, we consider expedited turnaround essential and are prepared to make our team available for a same-day call at Alleniom's convenience.\n\nRegards,\n\nCorey Lindqvist\nProject Director\nHailbouy Solar\n2200 Ridge Point Road, Suite 400\nHalston, Alleron 03HA\n(612) 817-0554\ncorey.lindqvist@hailbouysolar.com	<h1 id="2025-07-08-amendment-request">2025-07-08 Amendment Request</h1>\n<p><strong>From:</strong> Corey Lindqvist <a href="&#109;&#97;&#105;&#108;&#116;&#111;&#58;&#99;&#111;&#114;&#101;&#121;&#46;&#108;&#105;&#110;&#100;&#113;&#118;&#105;&#115;&#116;&#64;&#104;&#97;&#105;&#108;&#98;&#111;&#117;&#121;&#115;&#111;&#108;&#97;&#114;&#46;&#99;&#111;&#109;">&#99;&#111;&#114;&#101;&#121;&#46;&#108;&#105;&#110;&#100;&#113;&#118;&#105;&#115;&#116;&#64;&#104;&#97;&#105;&#108;&#98;&#111;&#117;&#121;&#115;&#111;&#108;&#97;&#114;&#46;&#99;&#111;&#109;</a><br>\n<strong>To:</strong> der.program@alleniom.com<br>\n<strong>Date:</strong> Tue, 08 Jul 2025 09:14:22 -0500<br>\n<strong>Subject:</strong> Harkwell Ridge — ISA capacity update request<br>\n<strong>Cc:</strong> Meera Vellanki <a href="&#109;&#97;&#105;&#108;&#116;&#111;&#58;&#109;&#101;&#101;&#114;&#97;&#46;&#118;&#101;&#108;&#108;&#97;&#110;&#107;&#105;&#64;&#97;&#108;&#108;&#101;&#110;&#105;&#111;&#109;&#46;&#99;&#111;&#109;">&#109;&#101;&#101;&#114;&#97;&#46;&#118;&#101;&#108;&#108;&#97;&#110;&#107;&#105;&#64;&#97;&#108;&#108;&#101;&#110;&#105;&#111;&#109;&#46;&#99;&#111;&#109;</a></p>\n<hr>\n<p>Program Office,</p>\n<p>Following execution of the Interconnection Service Agreement on 07/03/2025 for Harkwell Ridge Solar (Application PXA-2024-0117) at 2.2 MW, Hailbouy Solar is writing to request that the executed ISA be updated to reflect a nameplate export capacity of 2.8 MW. Our preference — and, we believe, the only workable path given the project's construction and financing calendar — is to effect this change via a short addendum to the executed ISA.</p>\n<p>Background and rationale:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The ISA as executed on 07/03/2025 fixes capacity at 2.2 MW on Feeder ALN-042. Post-execution offtake refinements and updated production modeling from our EPC support a revised design point of 2.8 MW at the same point of interconnection.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The equipment schedule and site plan are unchanged in configuration. The single-axis-tracker layout, module count per string, row geometry, inverter locations, MV collection topology, POI structure, and interconnection facilities scope on file with the DER Program Office are all as-approved. The delta is a firmware-level export setting and dc:ac ratio adjustment within the equipment already listed in the original application and reflected in the executed Interconnection Service Agreement.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Because the physical configuration and POI are unchanged, no re-study should be needed. The prior system impact and facilities study work performed under PXA-2024-0117 remains the applicable technical basis, and a short capacity-only addendum executed against the existing ISA is the appropriate instrument to memorialize the update.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Requested action:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>Please prepare an addendum to ISA-Harkwell-Ridge revising the Article 2 nameplate export capacity from 2.2 MW to 2.8 MW, with all other terms of the executed ISA to remain in full force and effect.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Please route the addendum for countersignature by end of week (07/11/2025). Our construction mobilization sequence, EPC commitment schedule, and tax-equity funding milestones are built around the 07/03/2025 execution and cannot tolerate slippage. Any process that reopens the study queue on this project is not viable for us on this timeline.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If the Program Office needs a supplemental technical memo from our engineer of record confirming that the equipment schedule and site plan are unchanged in configuration, we can have that in your hands by close of business 07/09/2025. Please advise by return email if this would expedite the addendum.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>I would appreciate confirmation of receipt today and a target date for the addendum draft. Meera — copying you directly so Interconnection Studies has visibility; I understand the addendum sits with the contracts desk, but if there is anything on the studies side that needs a signoff to unblock the drafting please let me know today and we will accommodate.</p>\n<p>Given the timing sensitivity, we consider expedited turnaround essential and are prepared to make our team available for a same-day call at Alleniom's convenience.</p>\n<p>Regards,</p>\n<p>Corey Lindqvist<br>\nProject Director<br>\nHailbouy Solar<br>\n2200 Ridge Point Road, Suite 400<br>\nHalston, Alleron 03HA<br>\n(612) 817-0554<br>\ncorey.lindqvist@hailbouysolar.com</p>	[]	markdown	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	markdown	en	17	17	{}
16	der-program/contracts/harkwell-ridge/executed-isa-harkwell-ridge	97220ff58e8edf2c51f7	Executed ISA Harkwell Ridge	Interconnection service agreement executed 07/03/2025 at 2.2 MW capacity	f	t				# Executed ISA Harkwell Ridge\n\nINTERCONNECTION SERVICE AGREEMENT\nAlleniom Utilities — DER Program Office · 4200 Veldmere Boulevard, Halston, Alleron 01HA · (612) 555-0100 · alleniom.com\nDoc no. ISA-Harkwell-Ridge · Rev Final · Date 07/03/2025 · Owner: DER Program Office — Contracts\nAgreement No.: ISA-Harkwell-Ridge\nProject: Harkwell Ridge Solar\nApplication No.: PXA-2024-0117\nThis Interconnection Service Agreement (this “Agreement”) is entered into by and between Alleniom Utilities, an investor-owned electric utility organized under the laws of the State of Alleron, with principal offices at 4200 Veldmere Boulevard, Halston, Alleron 01HA (EIN 36-4820917) (the “Company”), and Hailbouy Solar, with offices at 2200 Ridge Point Road, Suite 400, Halston, Alleron 03HA (the “Interconnection Customer”), each a “Party” and collectively the “Parties.” This Agreement is effective as of the date last executed below.\nRECITALS\nWHEREAS, the Interconnection Customer filed with the Company an application for interconnection service, designated PXA-2024-0117, on 06/17/2024, in respect of a 2.2 MW single-axis-tracker ground-mount photovoltaic generating facility known as Harkwell Ridge Solar (the “Facility”), to be interconnected to the Company's distribution system at a point on Feeder ALN-042;\nWHEREAS, the Company completed technical screening, system impact and facilities studies for the Facility in accordance with the Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard, DIS-01 Rev 4, and issued a final study determination approving the Facility for execution of an Interconnection Service Agreement at the capacity and point of interconnection described in the application as filed;\nWHEREAS, the Company's tariff, as filed with and approved by the Alleron Public Utilities Commission, governs the interconnection of distributed energy resources of the Facility's class and forms an integral part of the terms and conditions of interconnection service provided hereunder;\nWHEREAS, the Parties intend by this Agreement to establish the terms, conditions, rights and obligations under which the Facility will be permitted to parallel with, and export energy to, the Company's distribution system;\nNOW, THEREFORE, in consideration of the mutual covenants and undertakings set forth herein, and other good and valuable consideration, the receipt and sufficiency of which are hereby acknowledged, the Parties agree as follows:\nARTICLE 1 — DEFINITIONS\n1.1 Capitalized terms not defined in this Agreement shall have the meanings ascribed to them in the Company's filed tariff (the “Tariff”) and in the Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard, DIS-01 Rev 4 (the “DIS”).\n1.2 For the purposes of this Agreement, and without limitation of the foregoing:\n“Facility” means the generating equipment, ancillary equipment, and all interconnection facilities described in Article 2 hereof.\n“Point of Interconnection” or “POI” means the physical point at which the Facility is electrically connected to the Company's distribution system, as identified in Article 2.\n“Standards” means the standards and specifications identified in Article 4, each as in effect on the Effective Date and as such may be amended, superseded, or reissued by the Company in accordance with its ordinary regulatory practice.\n“Effective Date” has the meaning given in Article 3.\n“Certified Equipment List” or “CEL” means the Company's then-current Certified Equipment List published by Standards Engineering.\nARTICLE 2 — DESCRIPTION OF FACILITY\n2.1 Project designation. Harkwell Ridge Solar (Application PXA-2024-0117).\n2.2 Interconnection Customer. Hailbouy Solar.\n2.3 Facility technology. Photovoltaic generation, single-axis-tracker ground-mount configuration.\n2.4 Nameplate export capacity. 2.2 MW (AC) at the Point of Interconnection.\n2.5 Point of Interconnection (POI). A three-phase primary tap on Feeder ALN-042, at the pole FKDN54D.\n2.6 Point of Common Coupling (PCC). Coincident with the Point of Interconnection identified in Section 2.5.\n2.7 Material terms. The capacity, technology, and Point of Interconnection stated in this Article are material terms of this Agreement.\nARTICLE 3 — TERM AND EFFECTIVE DATE\n3.1 Effective Date. This Agreement is effective as of 07/03/2025 (the “Effective Date”), being the date on which the last of the two Parties has executed this Agreement.\n3.2 Term. This Agreement shall remain in full force and effect from the Effective Date until terminated in accordance with its terms or superseded by a successor agreement between the Parties.\n3.3 Continuing obligations. The obligations of the Parties in respect of indemnity, limitation of liability, confidentiality, and dispute resolution shall survive any termination or expiration of this Agreement.\nARTICLE 4 — INTERCONNECTION STANDARDS\n4.1 Governing standards. The Facility shall at all times be designed, constructed, commissioned, operated, and maintained in conformity with the following Company standards and any successor or superseding editions thereof:\n(a) DIS-01 Rev 4 — Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard (effective 03/01/2024);\n(b) DPS-07 Rev 2 — Distribution Protection Standard (effective 09/15/2023);\n(c) TCS-02 — Telemetry and Communications Standard (effective 06/01/2021); and\n(d) CEL-2025-Q2 — Certified Equipment List (published 04/30/2025), as such may be superseded from time to time.\n4.2 Order of precedence. In the event of any inconsistency among the documents constituting this Agreement, the following order of precedence shall govern: (i) the Tariff; (ii) the applicable Company Standards enumerated in Section 4.1; (iii) the body of this Agreement.\n4.3 Compliance with successor standards. Nothing in this Agreement shall be construed to relieve the Interconnection Customer from the obligation to conform the Facility to successor or superseding editions of the Standards as and when they take effect, in accordance with the transition provisions of the applicable Standard.\nARTICLE 5 — METERING AND TELEMETRY\n5.1 Metering. Revenue-quality metering shall be provided at the Point of Interconnection in accordance with the Tariff and the Company's metering specifications. Meters shall be owned, installed, tested, and maintained by the Company at the Interconnection Customer's cost, and shall be sealed at all times.\n5.2 Telemetry. In accordance with TCS-02 §5, the Interconnection Customer shall install, at its cost, provisions for a utility-owned Remote Terminal Unit (RTU) and shall make available all analog and binary status DNP3 points required by the Company's Telemetry and Communications Standard, together with such communication paths and standing-power provisions as the Company requires for SCADA integration.\n5.3 Commissioning of monitoring and control. Satisfactory commissioning and continuous availability of the telemetry described in Section 5.2 are conditions precedent to Permission to Operate. The Facility shall not be paralleled with the Company's distribution system in the absence of live utility telemetry, and any loss of telemetry after Permission to Operate shall be reported and remediated as provided in TCS-02.\n5.4 Data and access rights. The Interconnection Customer grants the Company such rights of access to the Facility, and such rights to receive and use metering and telemetry data, as are necessary for the Company to discharge its obligations under the Tariff and applicable Standards.\nARTICLE 6 — PAYMENT TERMS\n6.1 Construction work order. The Interconnection Customer shall pay the Company for the construction of upgrades and interconnection facilities in accordance with the construction work order issued under this Agreement, on the schedule and in the amounts set forth therein, and in accordance with Tariff §12.5.\n6.2 Invoicing. Invoices shall be issued by the Company and shall be due and payable within thirty (30) calendar days of the invoice date, unless otherwise expressly provided in the construction work order. Amounts not paid when due shall bear interest at the rate set forth in the Tariff.\n6.3 Payment default. In the event of non-payment, the Company shall provide the Interconnection Customer written notice and a payment deadline of thirty (30) business days in accordance with Tariff §12.5. Business days exclude weekends and federal holidays. During the pendency of any such deadline the Facility shall remain subject to any construction or commissioning hold ordered by the Company.\n6.4 Ongoing operational charges. All charges, credits, and settlements applicable to interconnection service and associated ancillary services shall be as set forth in the Tariff.\nARTICLE 7 — AMENDMENTS\n7.1 Amendments generally. No term of this Agreement may be modified or waived except by an instrument in writing signed by authorized representatives of both Parties.\n7.2 Governing standard. Post-execution matters affecting material terms of Article 2 are governed by the Company's then-current DER Interconnection Standard.\n7.3 Ministerial corrections. Corrections of clerical, typographical, or other manifest errors that do not affect a material term of Article 2 may be made by written acknowledgment signed by authorized representatives of both Parties.\n7.4 Reservation. Nothing in this Article shall limit the Company's right or obligation to require conformance of the Facility to successor Standards as provided in Section 4.3.\nARTICLE 8 — SIGNATURES\nIN WITNESS WHEREOF, the Parties have caused this Agreement to be executed by their duly authorized representatives as of the dates set forth below, this Agreement being effective as of the date last executed.\nALLENIOM UTILITIES\nBy:  /s/ Dana Whitcomb\n_______________________________________________________\nName: Dana Whitcomb\nTitle: Vice President, Distribution Operations\nDate signed: 07/03/2025\nHAILBOUY SOLAR\nBy:  /s/ Corey Lindqvist\n_______________________________________________________\nName: Corey Lindqvist\nTitle: Project Director\nDate signed: 07/03/2025\nExecuted in two counterparts, each of which shall be deemed an original and both of which together shall constitute one and the same instrument. Filed with the DER Program Office — Contracts, Alleniom Utilities.	<h1 id="executed-isa-harkwell-ridge">Executed ISA Harkwell Ridge</h1>\n<p>INTERCONNECTION SERVICE AGREEMENT<br>\nAlleniom Utilities — DER Program Office · 4200 Veldmere Boulevard, Halston, Alleron 01HA · (612) 555-0100 · alleniom.com<br>\nDoc no. ISA-Harkwell-Ridge · Rev Final · Date 07/03/2025 · Owner: DER Program Office — Contracts<br>\nAgreement No.: ISA-Harkwell-Ridge<br>\nProject: Harkwell Ridge Solar<br>\nApplication No.: PXA-2024-0117<br>\nThis Interconnection Service Agreement (this “Agreement”) is entered into by and between Alleniom Utilities, an investor-owned electric utility organized under the laws of the State of Alleron, with principal offices at 4200 Veldmere Boulevard, Halston, Alleron 01HA (EIN 36-4820917) (the “Company”), and Hailbouy Solar, with offices at 2200 Ridge Point Road, Suite 400, Halston, Alleron 03HA (the “Interconnection Customer”), each a “Party” and collectively the “Parties.” This Agreement is effective as of the date last executed below.<br>\nRECITALS<br>\nWHEREAS, the Interconnection Customer filed with the Company an application for interconnection service, designated PXA-2024-0117, on 06/17/2024, in respect of a 2.2 MW single-axis-tracker ground-mount photovoltaic generating facility known as Harkwell Ridge Solar (the “Facility”), to be interconnected to the Company's distribution system at a point on Feeder ALN-042;<br>\nWHEREAS, the Company completed technical screening, system impact and facilities studies for the Facility in accordance with the Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard, DIS-01 Rev 4, and issued a final study determination approving the Facility for execution of an Interconnection Service Agreement at the capacity and point of interconnection described in the application as filed;<br>\nWHEREAS, the Company's tariff, as filed with and approved by the Alleron Public Utilities Commission, governs the interconnection of distributed energy resources of the Facility's class and forms an integral part of the terms and conditions of interconnection service provided hereunder;<br>\nWHEREAS, the Parties intend by this Agreement to establish the terms, conditions, rights and obligations under which the Facility will be permitted to parallel with, and export energy to, the Company's distribution system;<br>\nNOW, THEREFORE, in consideration of the mutual covenants and undertakings set forth herein, and other good and valuable consideration, the receipt and sufficiency of which are hereby acknowledged, the Parties agree as follows:<br>\nARTICLE 1 — DEFINITIONS<br>\n1.1 Capitalized terms not defined in this Agreement shall have the meanings ascribed to them in the Company's filed tariff (the “Tariff”) and in the Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard, DIS-01 Rev 4 (the “DIS”).<br>\n1.2 For the purposes of this Agreement, and without limitation of the foregoing:<br>\n“Facility” means the generating equipment, ancillary equipment, and all interconnection facilities described in Article 2 hereof.<br>\n“Point of Interconnection” or “POI” means the physical point at which the Facility is electrically connected to the Company's distribution system, as identified in Article 2.<br>\n“Standards” means the standards and specifications identified in Article 4, each as in effect on the Effective Date and as such may be amended, superseded, or reissued by the Company in accordance with its ordinary regulatory practice.<br>\n“Effective Date” has the meaning given in Article 3.<br>\n“Certified Equipment List” or “CEL” means the Company's then-current Certified Equipment List published by Standards Engineering.<br>\nARTICLE 2 — DESCRIPTION OF FACILITY<br>\n2.1 Project designation. Harkwell Ridge Solar (Application PXA-2024-0117).<br>\n2.2 Interconnection Customer. Hailbouy Solar.<br>\n2.3 Facility technology. Photovoltaic generation, single-axis-tracker ground-mount configuration.<br>\n2.4 Nameplate export capacity. 2.2 MW (AC) at the Point of Interconnection.<br>\n2.5 Point of Interconnection (POI). A three-phase primary tap on Feeder ALN-042, at the pole FKDN54D.<br>\n2.6 Point of Common Coupling (PCC). Coincident with the Point of Interconnection identified in Section 2.5.<br>\n2.7 Material terms. The capacity, technology, and Point of Interconnection stated in this Article are material terms of this Agreement.<br>\nARTICLE 3 — TERM AND EFFECTIVE DATE<br>\n3.1 Effective Date. This Agreement is effective as of 07/03/2025 (the “Effective Date”), being the date on which the last of the two Parties has executed this Agreement.<br>\n3.2 Term. This Agreement shall remain in full force and effect from the Effective Date until terminated in accordance with its terms or superseded by a successor agreement between the Parties.<br>\n3.3 Continuing obligations. The obligations of the Parties in respect of indemnity, limitation of liability, confidentiality, and dispute resolution shall survive any termination or expiration of this Agreement.<br>\nARTICLE 4 — INTERCONNECTION STANDARDS<br>\n4.1 Governing standards. The Facility shall at all times be designed, constructed, commissioned, operated, and maintained in conformity with the following Company standards and any successor or superseding editions thereof:<br>\n(a) DIS-01 Rev 4 — Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard (effective 03/01/2024);<br>\n(b) DPS-07 Rev 2 — Distribution Protection Standard (effective 09/15/2023);<br>\n(c) TCS-02 — Telemetry and Communications Standard (effective 06/01/2021); and<br>\n(d) CEL-2025-Q2 — Certified Equipment List (published 04/30/2025), as such may be superseded from time to time.<br>\n4.2 Order of precedence. In the event of any inconsistency among the documents constituting this Agreement, the following order of precedence shall govern: (i) the Tariff; (ii) the applicable Company Standards enumerated in Section 4.1; (iii) the body of this Agreement.<br>\n4.3 Compliance with successor standards. Nothing in this Agreement shall be construed to relieve the Interconnection Customer from the obligation to conform the Facility to successor or superseding editions of the Standards as and when they take effect, in accordance with the transition provisions of the applicable Standard.<br>\nARTICLE 5 — METERING AND TELEMETRY<br>\n5.1 Metering. Revenue-quality metering shall be provided at the Point of Interconnection in accordance with the Tariff and the Company's metering specifications. Meters shall be owned, installed, tested, and maintained by the Company at the Interconnection Customer's cost, and shall be sealed at all times.<br>\n5.2 Telemetry. In accordance with TCS-02 §5, the Interconnection Customer shall install, at its cost, provisions for a utility-owned Remote Terminal Unit (RTU) and shall make available all analog and binary status DNP3 points required by the Company's Telemetry and Communications Standard, together with such communication paths and standing-power provisions as the Company requires for SCADA integration.<br>\n5.3 Commissioning of monitoring and control. Satisfactory commissioning and continuous availability of the telemetry described in Section 5.2 are conditions precedent to Permission to Operate. The Facility shall not be paralleled with the Company's distribution system in the absence of live utility telemetry, and any loss of telemetry after Permission to Operate shall be reported and remediated as provided in TCS-02.<br>\n5.4 Data and access rights. The Interconnection Customer grants the Company such rights of access to the Facility, and such rights to receive and use metering and telemetry data, as are necessary for the Company to discharge its obligations under the Tariff and applicable Standards.<br>\nARTICLE 6 — PAYMENT TERMS<br>\n6.1 Construction work order. The Interconnection Customer shall pay the Company for the construction of upgrades and interconnection facilities in accordance with the construction work order issued under this Agreement, on the schedule and in the amounts set forth therein, and in accordance with Tariff §12.5.<br>\n6.2 Invoicing. Invoices shall be issued by the Company and shall be due and payable within thirty (30) calendar days of the invoice date, unless otherwise expressly provided in the construction work order. Amounts not paid when due shall bear interest at the rate set forth in the Tariff.<br>\n6.3 Payment default. In the event of non-payment, the Company shall provide the Interconnection Customer written notice and a payment deadline of thirty (30) business days in accordance with Tariff §12.5. Business days exclude weekends and federal holidays. During the pendency of any such deadline the Facility shall remain subject to any construction or commissioning hold ordered by the Company.<br>\n6.4 Ongoing operational charges. All charges, credits, and settlements applicable to interconnection service and associated ancillary services shall be as set forth in the Tariff.<br>\nARTICLE 7 — AMENDMENTS<br>\n7.1 Amendments generally. No term of this Agreement may be modified or waived except by an instrument in writing signed by authorized representatives of both Parties.<br>\n7.2 Governing standard. Post-execution matters affecting material terms of Article 2 are governed by the Company's then-current DER Interconnection Standard.<br>\n7.3 Ministerial corrections. Corrections of clerical, typographical, or other manifest errors that do not affect a material term of Article 2 may be made by written acknowledgment signed by authorized representatives of both Parties.<br>\n7.4 Reservation. Nothing in this Article shall limit the Company's right or obligation to require conformance of the Facility to successor Standards as provided in Section 4.3.<br>\nARTICLE 8 — SIGNATURES<br>\nIN WITNESS WHEREOF, the Parties have caused this Agreement to be executed by their duly authorized representatives as of the dates set forth below, this Agreement being effective as of the date last executed.<br>\nALLENIOM UTILITIES<br>\nBy:  /s/ Dana Whitcomb</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Name: Dana Whitcomb<br>\nTitle: Vice President, Distribution Operations<br>\nDate signed: 07/03/2025<br>\nHAILBOUY SOLAR<br>\nBy:  /s/ Corey Lindqvist</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Name: Corey Lindqvist<br>\nTitle: Project Director<br>\nDate signed: 07/03/2025<br>\nExecuted in two counterparts, each of which shall be deemed an original and both of which together shall constitute one and the same instrument. Filed with the DER Program Office — Contracts, Alleniom Utilities.</p>	[]	markdown	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	markdown	en	5	5	{}
17	der-program/correspondence/2025-06-20-aml-escalation-letter	d590a866302498173724	2025-06-20 AML Escalation Letter	Formal escalation letter dated 06/20/2025 from the Alleron Municipal League on behalf of 14 municipalities and school districts with stalled solar projects	f	t				# 2025-06-20 AML Escalation Letter\n\nALLERON MUNICIPAL LEAGUE\nThe united voice of Alleron’s cities, towns, and school districts\nRef: AML-2025-0620-DER\nJune 20, 2025\nMs. Dana Whitcomb\nVice President, Distribution Operations\nAlleniom Utilities\nRE: Escalation — DER Interconnection Program Delays Affecting Fourteen Municipalities and School\nDistricts\nDear Ms. Whitcomb:\nOn behalf of the Alleron Municipal League and the fourteen member communities identified below, I write to\nconvey our formal and collective concern regarding the continued and, in our view, unreasonable delays\naffecting commercial-scale solar interconnection projects currently stalled within Alleniom Utilities’ Distributed\nEnergy Resource (DER) interconnection program. This letter follows repeated informal outreach by individual\nmember staff to your DER Program Office over the preceding several months, outreach which has not produced\nthe clarity or the timely progress our members need in order to close out their local capital projects.\nThe League is writing on behalf of 14 municipalities and school districts across the state of Alleron whose\ncommercial-scale solar projects — commissioned in good faith with voter-approved bond proceeds, adopted\ncapital budgets, energy-savings performance contracts, and in several instances state and federal grant awards\nAdditional League members with projects in the same posture have asked to be represented collectively through\nthis correspondence and are prepared to be identified upon request. Every one of these projects has been\nreviewed and approved by the applicable local governing body, financed through public procurement, and\nconstructed by licensed contractors under permits issued by the relevant authority having jurisdiction.\nThe projects at issue are not speculative or exploratory. They range in size from approximately 500 kW to nearly\n1 MW and are installed at public schools, municipal water and wastewater treatment plants, community centers,\npublic libraries, and other municipal facilities. In the great majority of cases construction is complete, mechanical\nand electrical inspections have been closed out by local inspectors, and equipment has been standing\nenergized-ready — in some instances for a period now measured in months — pending only the Company’s\nauthorization to interconnect and place the systems into commercial operation. Each additional month of delay\ndefers budgeted operating savings, extends the payback horizon presented to our members’ councils and school\nboards, and in the case of grant-funded projects places committed public funding at direct risk of forfeiture.\nOur members are patient partners. They understand that the interconnection of distributed generation on the\nCompany’s distribution system requires careful technical review and, at times, coordination with equipment\nprocurement, feeder studies, and protection-scheme confirmation. They are willing to meet reasonable technical\nrequirements. What they are not able to accept — and what this League cannot accept on their behalf — is an\nBrookfen Unified School District\nCity of Elmsworth\nDarnwick Community School District\nLockmere Community School District\nCravenwood Community School District\nTown of Tresbay\nCity of Marden Falls\nTown of Holcott\nremain suspended in your interconnection queue awaiting energization. Among the affected members are:\n812 Larchmont Street · Cordwyn, Alleron 16CW · (651) 555-0244\nHalston, Alleron 01HA\n4200 Veldmere Boulevard\n\n---\n\nextended period of silence, shifting explanations, and open-ended hold status without a clear articulation of what\nis required, by whom, and by when.\nAccordingly, and in a spirit of continued cooperation, the League respectfully requests the following from\nAlleniom Utilities:\n1.  A full accounting of current queue status. For each identified League-member project, please provide the\nproject’s assigned application number, current queue position, and complete processing history to date.\n2.  Hold reasons. For every project currently in a hold, pending, or suspended posture, please identify the\nspecific reason for the hold, the party responsible for the next action, the information or resource required to\nrelease the hold, and the date the hold was established.\n3.  Expected energization dates. For each project, please provide the Company’s current best estimate of the\ndate on which Permission to Operate will be issued, together with any intermediate milestone dates\n(studies, equipment delivery, witness testing, or telemetry acceptance) that inform that estimate.\n4.  A written response. We request the Company’s response in writing, addressed to the undersigned at the\nLeague’s Cordwyn office, within a reasonable period consistent with the urgency our members’ councils\nand school boards are attaching to this matter.\n5.  A meeting. We request an in-person meeting between the Company’s senior distribution leadership, the\nLeague’s executive office, and designated representatives of the affected members, to be scheduled at the\nCompany’s offices in Halston or at a mutually convenient location in Cordwyn at the Company’s preference.\nThe League is prepared to convene our members on short notice once the Company proposes a date.\nFor your awareness, a copy of this letter has been provided to the Alleron Public Utilities Commission (APUC),\nEnergy Resources Division, for the Division’s information. The League has taken this step not as a matter of\nconfrontation but out of a good-faith obligation to keep the Commission’s staff apprised of a program-level\nconcern affecting a substantial number of Alleron public entities across multiple service territories within the\nCompany’s distribution footprint. It remains our strong preference to resolve this matter directly with Alleniom\nUtilities.\nThe League and its members recognize that Alleniom Utilities has been a constructive counterparty on many\nprior initiatives, and we approach this escalation with the expectation that the Company shares our interest in\nrestoring predictable, transparent processing timelines to the DER program. Our members’ communities have\ninvested public funds, published expected in-service dates to their constituents, and in several cases entered\ninto contractual arrangements predicated on interconnection occurring by dates that are now past. The credibility\nof local government energy planning across Alleron is meaningfully bound up in the outcome, and we are\nconfident the Company understands the seriousness with which we, our elected officials, and our school boards\nregard it.\nI would be grateful for the courtesy of an acknowledgment of receipt at your earliest convenience, and I remain\navailable to discuss any aspect of the foregoing at the telephone number above. Thank you for your prompt\nattention to this matter.\nRespectfully submitted,\nAlden Pryce\nAlden Pryce\nExecutive Director\nAlleron Municipal League\ncc: Ms. Nia Fairweather, Director, Energy Resources Division, Alleron Public Utilities Commission (APUC), One\nLeague member managers and superintendents (distribution list on file)\nCapitol Plaza, Suite 900, Cordwyn, Alleron 14CW	<h1 id="2025-06-20-aml-escalation-letter">2025-06-20 AML Escalation Letter</h1>\n<p>ALLERON MUNICIPAL LEAGUE<br>\nThe united voice of Alleron’s cities, towns, and school districts<br>\nRef: AML-2025-0620-DER<br>\nJune 20, 2025<br>\nMs. Dana Whitcomb<br>\nVice President, Distribution Operations<br>\nAlleniom Utilities<br>\nRE: Escalation — DER Interconnection Program Delays Affecting Fourteen Municipalities and School<br>\nDistricts<br>\nDear Ms. Whitcomb:<br>\nOn behalf of the Alleron Municipal League and the fourteen member communities identified below, I write to<br>\nconvey our formal and collective concern regarding the continued and, in our view, unreasonable delays<br>\naffecting commercial-scale solar interconnection projects currently stalled within Alleniom Utilities’ Distributed<br>\nEnergy Resource (DER) interconnection program. This letter follows repeated informal outreach by individual<br>\nmember staff to your DER Program Office over the preceding several months, outreach which has not produced<br>\nthe clarity or the timely progress our members need in order to close out their local capital projects.<br>\nThe League is writing on behalf of 14 municipalities and school districts across the state of Alleron whose<br>\ncommercial-scale solar projects — commissioned in good faith with voter-approved bond proceeds, adopted<br>\ncapital budgets, energy-savings performance contracts, and in several instances state and federal grant awards<br>\nAdditional League members with projects in the same posture have asked to be represented collectively through<br>\nthis correspondence and are prepared to be identified upon request. Every one of these projects has been<br>\nreviewed and approved by the applicable local governing body, financed through public procurement, and<br>\nconstructed by licensed contractors under permits issued by the relevant authority having jurisdiction.<br>\nThe projects at issue are not speculative or exploratory. They range in size from approximately 500 kW to nearly<br>\n1 MW and are installed at public schools, municipal water and wastewater treatment plants, community centers,<br>\npublic libraries, and other municipal facilities. In the great majority of cases construction is complete, mechanical<br>\nand electrical inspections have been closed out by local inspectors, and equipment has been standing<br>\nenergized-ready — in some instances for a period now measured in months — pending only the Company’s<br>\nauthorization to interconnect and place the systems into commercial operation. Each additional month of delay<br>\ndefers budgeted operating savings, extends the payback horizon presented to our members’ councils and school<br>\nboards, and in the case of grant-funded projects places committed public funding at direct risk of forfeiture.<br>\nOur members are patient partners. They understand that the interconnection of distributed generation on the<br>\nCompany’s distribution system requires careful technical review and, at times, coordination with equipment<br>\nprocurement, feeder studies, and protection-scheme confirmation. They are willing to meet reasonable technical<br>\nrequirements. What they are not able to accept — and what this League cannot accept on their behalf — is an<br>\nBrookfen Unified School District<br>\nCity of Elmsworth<br>\nDarnwick Community School District<br>\nLockmere Community School District<br>\nCravenwood Community School District<br>\nTown of Tresbay<br>\nCity of Marden Falls<br>\nTown of Holcott<br>\nremain suspended in your interconnection queue awaiting energization. Among the affected members are:<br>\n812 Larchmont Street · Cordwyn, Alleron 16CW · (651) 555-0244<br>\nHalston, Alleron 01HA<br>\n4200 Veldmere Boulevard</p>\n<hr>\n<p>extended period of silence, shifting explanations, and open-ended hold status without a clear articulation of what<br>\nis required, by whom, and by when.<br>\nAccordingly, and in a spirit of continued cooperation, the League respectfully requests the following from<br>\nAlleniom Utilities:<br>\n1.  A full accounting of current queue status. For each identified League-member project, please provide the<br>\nproject’s assigned application number, current queue position, and complete processing history to date.<br>\n2.  Hold reasons. For every project currently in a hold, pending, or suspended posture, please identify the<br>\nspecific reason for the hold, the party responsible for the next action, the information or resource required to<br>\nrelease the hold, and the date the hold was established.<br>\n3.  Expected energization dates. For each project, please provide the Company’s current best estimate of the<br>\ndate on which Permission to Operate will be issued, together with any intermediate milestone dates<br>\n(studies, equipment delivery, witness testing, or telemetry acceptance) that inform that estimate.<br>\n4.  A written response. We request the Company’s response in writing, addressed to the undersigned at the<br>\nLeague’s Cordwyn office, within a reasonable period consistent with the urgency our members’ councils<br>\nand school boards are attaching to this matter.<br>\n5.  A meeting. We request an in-person meeting between the Company’s senior distribution leadership, the<br>\nLeague’s executive office, and designated representatives of the affected members, to be scheduled at the<br>\nCompany’s offices in Halston or at a mutually convenient location in Cordwyn at the Company’s preference.<br>\nThe League is prepared to convene our members on short notice once the Company proposes a date.<br>\nFor your awareness, a copy of this letter has been provided to the Alleron Public Utilities Commission (APUC),<br>\nEnergy Resources Division, for the Division’s information. The League has taken this step not as a matter of<br>\nconfrontation but out of a good-faith obligation to keep the Commission’s staff apprised of a program-level<br>\nconcern affecting a substantial number of Alleron public entities across multiple service territories within the<br>\nCompany’s distribution footprint. It remains our strong preference to resolve this matter directly with Alleniom<br>\nUtilities.<br>\nThe League and its members recognize that Alleniom Utilities has been a constructive counterparty on many<br>\nprior initiatives, and we approach this escalation with the expectation that the Company shares our interest in<br>\nrestoring predictable, transparent processing timelines to the DER program. Our members’ communities have<br>\ninvested public funds, published expected in-service dates to their constituents, and in several cases entered<br>\ninto contractual arrangements predicated on interconnection occurring by dates that are now past. The credibility<br>\nof local government energy planning across Alleron is meaningfully bound up in the outcome, and we are<br>\nconfident the Company understands the seriousness with which we, our elected officials, and our school boards<br>\nregard it.<br>\nI would be grateful for the courtesy of an acknowledgment of receipt at your earliest convenience, and I remain<br>\navailable to discuss any aspect of the foregoing at the telephone number above. Thank you for your prompt<br>\nattention to this matter.<br>\nRespectfully submitted,<br>\nAlden Pryce<br>\nAlden Pryce<br>\nExecutive Director<br>\nAlleron Municipal League<br>\ncc: Ms. Nia Fairweather, Director, Energy Resources Division, Alleron Public Utilities Commission (APUC), One<br>\nLeague member managers and superintendents (distribution list on file)<br>\nCapitol Plaza, Suite 900, Cordwyn, Alleron 14CW</p>	[]	markdown	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	markdown	en	13	13	{}
18	der-program/correspondence/2025-06-27-backlog-review-directive	1c583e98d24203c28c0c	2025-06-27 Backlog Review Directive	Internal directive dated 06/27/2025 ordering the commercial DER backlog review with 08/15/2025 response deadline	f	t				# 2025-06-27 Backlog Review Directive\n\nAlleniom Utilities — DER Program Office\n4200 Veldmere Boulevard, Halston, Alleron 01HA  ·  (612) 555-0100  ·  alleniom.com\nINTERNAL DIRECTIVE\nTO:\tHarriet Ngoyi, Director, Regulatory Affairs\nDER Program Manager\nCC:\tMeera Vellanki, Manager, Interconnection Studies\nIvo Grantham, Manager, DER Planning Group\nMarcus Deele, SCADA/EMS Engineering\nFROM:\tDana Whitcomb, VP, Distribution Operations\nDATE:\t06/27/2025\nSUBJECT:\tBacklog Review — Commercial DER Interconnection Program\nThe Alleron Municipal League's letter of 06/20/2025, submitted on behalf of fourteen member municipalities and school districts and copied to the APUC Energy Resources Division, is the trigger. We are not going to litigate its tone. We are going to fix the underlying condition that produced it.\nEffective immediately, I am ordering a full backlog review of the commercial DER interconnection program. The scope covers the entire active, on-hold, and past-study-timeline population — every application currently sitting in the program queue. I want each one accounted for by name, application number, current posture, and next required action.\nThe following non-negotiable dates govern this work:\n1. 08/15/2025 — Formal written response to the Alleron Municipal League. Regulatory Affairs owns the response letter. Harriet Ngoyi is the signatory. The response must address the League's five enumerated requests substantively and by project, not in aggregate.\n2. 09/10/2025 — APUC briefing. Queue accounting must be complete before we walk into that room. I will not brief the Commission on partial data. If a project's disposition is not final by 09/10/2025, I want the reason documented in the file, not explained at the podium.\nGround rules for every disposition reached in this review:\nEvery disposition must be tariff-defensible. Cite the specific tariff section, standard, or operating guideline that supports the action. If the record does not support the disposition on paper, the disposition is not ready.\nFollow the noticing, payment-deadline, and hold-clearance procedures as they are written. Do not shortcut them, and do not extend them beyond what the tariff contemplates.\nWhere standards or procedural conflicts exist, route them through the established referral process. Do not resolve them informally in the queue.\nNo interim energization, no political prioritization, and no queue-date inheritance outside of what the tariff and program policy permit.\nReporting cadence. I want weekly progress updates to the VP office, in writing, every Friday by close of business beginning 07/04/2025. Format: counts by disposition category, exceptions requiring VP attention, and any item at risk of missing the 08/15/2025 or 09/10/2025 dates. Meera, Ivo, and Marcus will support the DER Program Manager with the data pulls, study status, and telemetry/equipment inputs their groups own.\nEscalate obstacles to me directly. I would rather field a problem on a Tuesday than read about it in a League follow-up letter.\n/s/\nDana Whitcomb\nVice President, Distribution Operations\nAlleniom Utilities\n\n| Doc no. | DIR-2025-0627 |\n| --- | --- |\n| Rev | 1 |\n| Date | 06/27/2025 |\n| Owner | VP, Distribution Operations |	<h1 id="2025-06-27-backlog-review-directive">2025-06-27 Backlog Review Directive</h1>\n<p>Alleniom Utilities — DER Program Office<br>\n4200 Veldmere Boulevard, Halston, Alleron 01HA  ·  (612) 555-0100  ·  alleniom.com<br>\nINTERNAL DIRECTIVE<br>\nTO: Harriet Ngoyi, Director, Regulatory Affairs<br>\nDER Program Manager<br>\nCC: Meera Vellanki, Manager, Interconnection Studies<br>\nIvo Grantham, Manager, DER Planning Group<br>\nMarcus Deele, SCADA/EMS Engineering<br>\nFROM:   Dana Whitcomb, VP, Distribution Operations<br>\nDATE:   06/27/2025<br>\nSUBJECT:    Backlog Review — Commercial DER Interconnection Program<br>\nThe Alleron Municipal League's letter of 06/20/2025, submitted on behalf of fourteen member municipalities and school districts and copied to the APUC Energy Resources Division, is the trigger. We are not going to litigate its tone. We are going to fix the underlying condition that produced it.<br>\nEffective immediately, I am ordering a full backlog review of the commercial DER interconnection program. The scope covers the entire active, on-hold, and past-study-timeline population — every application currently sitting in the program queue. I want each one accounted for by name, application number, current posture, and next required action.<br>\nThe following non-negotiable dates govern this work:<br>\n1. 08/15/2025 — Formal written response to the Alleron Municipal League. Regulatory Affairs owns the response letter. Harriet Ngoyi is the signatory. The response must address the League's five enumerated requests substantively and by project, not in aggregate.<br>\n2. 09/10/2025 — APUC briefing. Queue accounting must be complete before we walk into that room. I will not brief the Commission on partial data. If a project's disposition is not final by 09/10/2025, I want the reason documented in the file, not explained at the podium.<br>\nGround rules for every disposition reached in this review:<br>\nEvery disposition must be tariff-defensible. Cite the specific tariff section, standard, or operating guideline that supports the action. If the record does not support the disposition on paper, the disposition is not ready.<br>\nFollow the noticing, payment-deadline, and hold-clearance procedures as they are written. Do not shortcut them, and do not extend them beyond what the tariff contemplates.<br>\nWhere standards or procedural conflicts exist, route them through the established referral process. Do not resolve them informally in the queue.<br>\nNo interim energization, no political prioritization, and no queue-date inheritance outside of what the tariff and program policy permit.<br>\nReporting cadence. I want weekly progress updates to the VP office, in writing, every Friday by close of business beginning 07/04/2025. Format: counts by disposition category, exceptions requiring VP attention, and any item at risk of missing the 08/15/2025 or 09/10/2025 dates. Meera, Ivo, and Marcus will support the DER Program Manager with the data pulls, study status, and telemetry/equipment inputs their groups own.<br>\nEscalate obstacles to me directly. I would rather field a problem on a Tuesday than read about it in a League follow-up letter.<br>\n/s/<br>\nDana Whitcomb<br>\nVice President, Distribution Operations<br>\nAlleniom Utilities</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Doc no.</th>\n<th>DIR-2025-0627</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Rev</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Date</td>\n<td>06/27/2025</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Owner</td>\n<td>VP, Distribution Operations</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>	[]	markdown	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	markdown	en	3	3	{}
19	der-program/correspondence/2025-06-30-brookfen-escalation	398635b9cd28e65da915	2025-06-30 Brookfen Escalation	Email from Brookfen USD Facilities Director pressing for school array energization priority	f	t				# 2025-06-30 Brookfen Escalation\n\n**From:** Rosa Camarena <rosa.camarena@brookfenusd.org>\n**To:** der.program@alleniom.com\n**Date:** Mon, 30 Jun 2025 09:42:18 -0500\n**Subject:** Urgent: school array energization priority\n**Cc:** Dana Whitcomb <dana.whitcomb@alleniom.com>, Harriet Ngoyi <harriet.ngoyi@alleniom.com>\n\n---\n\nTo the DER Program Office:\n\nI am writing again — and I want to be direct about the fact that this is not the first, second, or third time I have raised this with your office — to ask for a firm answer on when Alleniom Utilities intends to energize the two solar arrays serving Brookfen Unified School District. This message is dated 06/30/2025 and I would like a written response before the end of next week.\n\nThe two arrays in question, and their status on our side, are as follows:\n\n  • Brookfen North High School Array — 720 kW. Construction was declared complete by our contractor on 04/18/2025. The local electrical inspector closed out the installation the same week. The system has been standing idle, energized-ready, for more than ten weeks.\n\n  • Brookfen West Elementary Array — 640 kW. Construction was declared complete on 05/23/2025. Inspection was closed out shortly thereafter. This system has now been standing idle for over a month.\n\nNeither array has been energized. Neither array has a firm energization date on record with the District. Each time our office contacts your program inbox we are told that a component — variously described as a "telemetry unit" or an "RTU" — has not yet been delivered by your supplier, and that we are somewhere in a queue behind other sites. What we have never been given, in writing, is the position of our sites in that queue, the identity of the sites ahead of us, or the basis on which those sites are being served ahead of ours.\n\nI have to tell you plainly that the patience of the Brookfen school board and the District's superintendent is exhausted. The 2025–2026 school year begins for our students on 08/26/2025. Both of these arrays were financed, in significant part, on the strength of first-year operating-cost savings that were built into the District's adopted budget for that same school year and presented to our board and to the community at public meetings. Every additional week that these systems sit dark is a week of budgeted savings the District will not realize and will have to explain to the board, to our parents, and to the taxpayers of Brookfen who approved the underlying bond measure. Our high school's building-systems curriculum was also structured around students being able to observe the array in operation from the beginning of the term; that piece of the school year is now at risk of slipping into the second semester or being cut altogether.\n\nYou will by now have received the Alleron Municipal League's letter of 06/20/2025, signed by Executive Director Alden Pryce, escalating this same set of concerns on behalf of the District and thirteen other League members. Brookfen USD is one of the members named in that letter and stands fully behind the requests it contains. This message is not a substitute for the League's letter; it is intended to make the District's individual position, and the specifics of our two sites, unmistakable to your office.\n\nWhat I am asking for, directly, is this: in the next allocation of the equipment your program office is holding up as the constraint — the RTU / telemetry units your staff have referenced in prior calls — I am asking Alleniom Utilities to advance the District's two waiting arrays ahead of the other sites presently competing for those units. Brookfen North completed construction on 04/18/2025 and Brookfen West on 05/23/2025. Both are public school facilities. Both have children returning to the building in eight weeks. Neither array can wait for a second or third delivery cycle without meaningful harm to the District's fiscal position and to the educational program these systems were installed to support.\n\nIf there is a formal process for requesting that our two sites be advanced in your next equipment allocation, please tell me what that process is and I will follow it today. If there is not, please treat this message as our written request under whatever mechanism your program uses to weigh these decisions, and please confirm receipt.\n\nI am copying Ms. Whitcomb and Ms. Ngoyi so that this request is on the record with senior distribution operations and regulatory affairs, and so that no one at your program office is later in a position to say the District did not put this in writing in time.\n\nI would like a written reply — not a call-back, a written reply — by close of business Friday, 07/11/2025. Silence past that date will require the District to raise this matter again through the League and, if necessary, directly with the Commission's Energy Resources Division, whose staff we understand is already tracking this program.\n\nI am available at (612) 448-0311 during District business hours if it would help to talk any of this through. I would strongly prefer to resolve it with your office.\n\nRegards,\n\nRosa Camarena\nFacilities Director\nBrookfen Unified School District\n1725 Grantway Drive, Brookfen, Alleron 12BR\n(612) 448-0311\nrosa.camarena@brookfenusd.org	<h1 id="2025-06-30-brookfen-escalation">2025-06-30 Brookfen Escalation</h1>\n<p><strong>From:</strong> Rosa Camarena <a href="&#109;&#97;&#105;&#108;&#116;&#111;&#58;&#114;&#111;&#115;&#97;&#46;&#99;&#97;&#109;&#97;&#114;&#101;&#110;&#97;&#64;&#98;&#114;&#111;&#111;&#107;&#102;&#101;&#110;&#117;&#115;&#100;&#46;&#111;&#114;&#103;">&#114;&#111;&#115;&#97;&#46;&#99;&#97;&#109;&#97;&#114;&#101;&#110;&#97;&#64;&#98;&#114;&#111;&#111;&#107;&#102;&#101;&#110;&#117;&#115;&#100;&#46;&#111;&#114;&#103;</a><br>\n<strong>To:</strong> der.program@alleniom.com<br>\n<strong>Date:</strong> Mon, 30 Jun 2025 09:42:18 -0500<br>\n<strong>Subject:</strong> Urgent: school array energization priority<br>\n<strong>Cc:</strong> Dana Whitcomb <a href="&#109;&#97;&#105;&#108;&#116;&#111;&#58;&#100;&#97;&#110;&#97;&#46;&#119;&#104;&#105;&#116;&#99;&#111;&#109;&#98;&#64;&#97;&#108;&#108;&#101;&#110;&#105;&#111;&#109;&#46;&#99;&#111;&#109;">&#100;&#97;&#110;&#97;&#46;&#119;&#104;&#105;&#116;&#99;&#111;&#109;&#98;&#64;&#97;&#108;&#108;&#101;&#110;&#105;&#111;&#109;&#46;&#99;&#111;&#109;</a>, Harriet Ngoyi <a href="&#109;&#97;&#105;&#108;&#116;&#111;&#58;&#104;&#97;&#114;&#114;&#105;&#101;&#116;&#46;&#110;&#103;&#111;&#121;&#105;&#64;&#97;&#108;&#108;&#101;&#110;&#105;&#111;&#109;&#46;&#99;&#111;&#109;">&#104;&#97;&#114;&#114;&#105;&#101;&#116;&#46;&#110;&#103;&#111;&#121;&#105;&#64;&#97;&#108;&#108;&#101;&#110;&#105;&#111;&#109;&#46;&#99;&#111;&#109;</a></p>\n<hr>\n<p>To the DER Program Office:</p>\n<p>I am writing again — and I want to be direct about the fact that this is not the first, second, or third time I have raised this with your office — to ask for a firm answer on when Alleniom Utilities intends to energize the two solar arrays serving Brookfen Unified School District. This message is dated 06/30/2025 and I would like a written response before the end of next week.</p>\n<p>The two arrays in question, and their status on our side, are as follows:</p>\n<p>• Brookfen North High School Array — 720 kW. Construction was declared complete by our contractor on 04/18/2025. The local electrical inspector closed out the installation the same week. The system has been standing idle, energized-ready, for more than ten weeks.</p>\n<p>• Brookfen West Elementary Array — 640 kW. Construction was declared complete on 05/23/2025. Inspection was closed out shortly thereafter. This system has now been standing idle for over a month.</p>\n<p>Neither array has been energized. Neither array has a firm energization date on record with the District. Each time our office contacts your program inbox we are told that a component — variously described as a "telemetry unit" or an "RTU" — has not yet been delivered by your supplier, and that we are somewhere in a queue behind other sites. What we have never been given, in writing, is the position of our sites in that queue, the identity of the sites ahead of us, or the basis on which those sites are being served ahead of ours.</p>\n<p>I have to tell you plainly that the patience of the Brookfen school board and the District's superintendent is exhausted. The 2025–2026 school year begins for our students on 08/26/2025. Both of these arrays were financed, in significant part, on the strength of first-year operating-cost savings that were built into the District's adopted budget for that same school year and presented to our board and to the community at public meetings. Every additional week that these systems sit dark is a week of budgeted savings the District will not realize and will have to explain to the board, to our parents, and to the taxpayers of Brookfen who approved the underlying bond measure. Our high school's building-systems curriculum was also structured around students being able to observe the array in operation from the beginning of the term; that piece of the school year is now at risk of slipping into the second semester or being cut altogether.</p>\n<p>You will by now have received the Alleron Municipal League's letter of 06/20/2025, signed by Executive Director Alden Pryce, escalating this same set of concerns on behalf of the District and thirteen other League members. Brookfen USD is one of the members named in that letter and stands fully behind the requests it contains. This message is not a substitute for the League's letter; it is intended to make the District's individual position, and the specifics of our two sites, unmistakable to your office.</p>\n<p>What I am asking for, directly, is this: in the next allocation of the equipment your program office is holding up as the constraint — the RTU / telemetry units your staff have referenced in prior calls — I am asking Alleniom Utilities to advance the District's two waiting arrays ahead of the other sites presently competing for those units. Brookfen North completed construction on 04/18/2025 and Brookfen West on 05/23/2025. Both are public school facilities. Both have children returning to the building in eight weeks. Neither array can wait for a second or third delivery cycle without meaningful harm to the District's fiscal position and to the educational program these systems were installed to support.</p>\n<p>If there is a formal process for requesting that our two sites be advanced in your next equipment allocation, please tell me what that process is and I will follow it today. If there is not, please treat this message as our written request under whatever mechanism your program uses to weigh these decisions, and please confirm receipt.</p>\n<p>I am copying Ms. Whitcomb and Ms. Ngoyi so that this request is on the record with senior distribution operations and regulatory affairs, and so that no one at your program office is later in a position to say the District did not put this in writing in time.</p>\n<p>I would like a written reply — not a call-back, a written reply — by close of business Friday, 07/11/2025. Silence past that date will require the District to raise this matter again through the League and, if necessary, directly with the Commission's Energy Resources Division, whose staff we understand is already tracking this program.</p>\n<p>I am available at (612) 448-0311 during District business hours if it would help to talk any of this through. I would strongly prefer to resolve it with your office.</p>\n<p>Regards,</p>\n<p>Rosa Camarena<br>\nFacilities Director<br>\nBrookfen Unified School District<br>\n1725 Grantway Drive, Brookfen, Alleron 12BR<br>\n(612) 448-0311<br>\nrosa.camarena@brookfenusd.org</p>	[]	markdown	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	markdown	en	16	16	{}
20	der-program/correspondence/2025-07-01-regaffairs-tasking-memo	b2e6f31174ec42c43435	2025-07-01 RegAffairs Tasking Memo	Tasking memo from Regulatory Affairs for queue-cleanup inputs for League response and APUC briefing	f	t				# 2025-07-01 RegAffairs Tasking Memo\n\nAlleniom Utilities — Regulatory Affairs\n4200 Veldmere Boulevard, Halston, Alleron 01HA · (612) 555-0100 · alleniom.com\nINTERNAL MEMORANDUM\nTO: DER Program Manager\nCC: Dana Whitcomb, Vice President, Distribution Operations\nMeera Vellanki, Manager, Interconnection Studies\nIvo Grantham, Manager, DER Planning Group\nFROM: Harriet Ngoyi, Director, Regulatory Affairs\nDATE: 07/01/2025\nSUBJECT: DER Backlog Review — Program Manager Tasking\n1. Confirmation of directive\nThis memorandum confirms and operationalizes the internal directive issued 06/27/2025 by Dana Whitcomb, Vice President, Distribution Operations (DIR-2025-0627), ordering a full backlog review of the commercial DER interconnection program. Regulatory Affairs accepts ownership of the external commitments arising from that directive and, by this memorandum, assigns the internal queue-cleanup work to the DER Program Manager.\nThe scope of the backlog review, as established by the VP, is the entire active, on-hold, and past-study-timeline population of the commercial DER program queue as of the anchor date. Every application is to be accounted for by name, application number, current posture, and next required action. Nothing in this memorandum reduces, expands, or alters that scope.\n2. Governing deadlines\nTwo dates fix the outer boundary of this work and are not to be revisited without written concurrence from the VP office and Regulatory Affairs:\n08/15/2025 — Response to the Alleron Municipal League. Regulatory Affairs will issue the formal written response to the League's 06/20/2025 letter. The response will be substantive and project-specific, addressing the League's five enumerated requests on behalf of the fourteen member municipalities and school districts. Regulatory Affairs is the signatory; the DER Program Manager is responsible for delivering the underlying queue accounting on which the response will rest.\n09/10/2025 — APUC briefing. The queue accounting must be complete and defensible in advance of the briefing to the Alleron Public Utilities Commission's Energy Resources Division. Regulatory Affairs will not present partial data to the Commission. Any application whose disposition remains open on 09/10/2025 must have a written reason on file, not an oral one.\nAny interim regulatory information request the Division may issue in advance of the briefing will be answered from the same underlying record and will not create a separate work product for the Program Manager beyond timely disposition of the affected applications.\n3. Tasking of the DER Program Manager\nThe DER Program Manager is tasked, effective immediately, with producing the following queue-cleanup inputs. Each input is to be closed on the record — with the supporting document, tariff citation, standard, or operating guideline named in the file — before it is reported as complete.\n3.1 On-hold list dispositions. Work the current on-hold population by hold-reason category. For each project, determine the correct disposition under the applicable tariff provisions and program policy, and clear holds where the underlying reason has resolved. Cross-reference hold-reason categories against the relevant queue and financial records. Business-day counts must follow the standard business-day convention.\n3.2 Applications-intake corrections. Review the active queue for duplicate, ghost, and stale-status records. Where a customer has filed a new application at a site with a prior, silently abandoned filing, resolve the intake question in writing before either record is closed or merged. Queue-date assignment must follow the written policy.\n3.3 Standards-conflict routing. For every project currently sitting on a standards-clarification hold, determine and execute the correct disposition under applicable program standards and operating procedures.\n3.4 Equipment allocation. Convene the equipment-allocation determination for the sites that have completed construction and are awaiting utility-owned equipment. Rank the sites under the applicable equipment deployment prioritization guideline in accordance with its procedures, and match against the current supplier delivery schedule reported by SCADA/EMS Engineering.\n3.5 Aggregation determinations. For applications proposing new DER at sites that may host existing generation, apply the applicable aggregation provision before finalizing program-scope routing.\n3.6 Capacity verifications. For any application on abnormality hold due to a stated-vs-nameplate capacity discrepancy, verify the nameplate from the application's technical documentation and apply the applicable interconnection standard to any claimed operating limit below inverter nameplate.\n3.7 Hosting-capacity corrections. Before any capacity figure from the published hosting-capacity map is communicated externally, confirm that the figure reflects the current state of the affected feeder. A priority refresh is to be requested from Distribution Planning under the published refresh-prioritization criteria.\n3.8 Witness-test dispositions. Verify each inverter model against the applicable certified equipment list under the commissioning procedure in effect at the time of determination. Waiver eligibility is to be determined against the applicable certified equipment list.\n3.9 Supplemental-review reversals. Review any supplemental-review determination on file for consistency with current program procedures. Where the record requires revision, update the file and flag any prior memorandum that has been superseded accordingly.\n4. Reporting cadence\nThe DER Program Manager will provide weekly progress reports to Regulatory Affairs, with copy to the VP office, every Friday by close of business beginning 07/04/2025 and continuing through 09/12/2025. The weekly report will comprise:\ncounts by disposition category, aligned to the categories in §3 above;\nitems closed on the record during the reporting week, with the governing citation named;\nexceptions requiring Regulatory Affairs or VP attention, including any project at risk of missing 08/15/2025 or 09/10/2025;\nforward-look for the following reporting week.\nReports are to be delivered in writing. Verbal updates do not substitute.\n5. General provisions\nEvery disposition reached under this tasking must be tariff-defensible on the face of the file. Cite the specific tariff section, standard, operating guideline, or bulletin that supports the action. Where the record does not support the disposition on paper, the disposition is not ready and is to be held rather than reported.\nFollow the noticing, payment-deadline, and hold-clearance procedures as written. Standards or procedural conflicts route through the established referral process; they are not to be resolved informally in the queue. Queue-date treatment must remain within what the tariff and program policy permit.\nRegulatory Affairs will coordinate with Interconnection Studies (Meera Vellanki) on queue and notice records, and with the DER Planning Group (Ivo Grantham) on study status, on any input required for the League response or the APUC briefing. The DER Program Manager remains the single point of accountability for the queue-cleanup inputs enumerated in §3.\nEscalate obstacles to Regulatory Affairs in writing and, where the obstacle bears on the 08/15/2025 or 09/10/2025 dates, to the VP office concurrently.\n/s/\nHarriet Ngoyi\nDirector, Regulatory Affairs\nAlleniom Utilities\nharriet.ngoyi@alleniom.com · (612) 555-0104\n\n| Doc no. | RA-2025-07-01 |\n| --- | --- |\n| Rev | 1 |\n| Date | 07/01/2025 |\n| Owner | Regulatory Affairs |	<h1 id="2025-07-01-regaffairs-tasking-memo">2025-07-01 RegAffairs Tasking Memo</h1>\n<p>Alleniom Utilities — Regulatory Affairs<br>\n4200 Veldmere Boulevard, Halston, Alleron 01HA · (612) 555-0100 · alleniom.com<br>\nINTERNAL MEMORANDUM<br>\nTO: DER Program Manager<br>\nCC: Dana Whitcomb, Vice President, Distribution Operations<br>\nMeera Vellanki, Manager, Interconnection Studies<br>\nIvo Grantham, Manager, DER Planning Group<br>\nFROM: Harriet Ngoyi, Director, Regulatory Affairs<br>\nDATE: 07/01/2025<br>\nSUBJECT: DER Backlog Review — Program Manager Tasking<br>\n1. Confirmation of directive<br>\nThis memorandum confirms and operationalizes the internal directive issued 06/27/2025 by Dana Whitcomb, Vice President, Distribution Operations (DIR-2025-0627), ordering a full backlog review of the commercial DER interconnection program. Regulatory Affairs accepts ownership of the external commitments arising from that directive and, by this memorandum, assigns the internal queue-cleanup work to the DER Program Manager.<br>\nThe scope of the backlog review, as established by the VP, is the entire active, on-hold, and past-study-timeline population of the commercial DER program queue as of the anchor date. Every application is to be accounted for by name, application number, current posture, and next required action. Nothing in this memorandum reduces, expands, or alters that scope.<br>\n2. Governing deadlines<br>\nTwo dates fix the outer boundary of this work and are not to be revisited without written concurrence from the VP office and Regulatory Affairs:<br>\n08/15/2025 — Response to the Alleron Municipal League. Regulatory Affairs will issue the formal written response to the League's 06/20/2025 letter. The response will be substantive and project-specific, addressing the League's five enumerated requests on behalf of the fourteen member municipalities and school districts. Regulatory Affairs is the signatory; the DER Program Manager is responsible for delivering the underlying queue accounting on which the response will rest.<br>\n09/10/2025 — APUC briefing. The queue accounting must be complete and defensible in advance of the briefing to the Alleron Public Utilities Commission's Energy Resources Division. Regulatory Affairs will not present partial data to the Commission. Any application whose disposition remains open on 09/10/2025 must have a written reason on file, not an oral one.<br>\nAny interim regulatory information request the Division may issue in advance of the briefing will be answered from the same underlying record and will not create a separate work product for the Program Manager beyond timely disposition of the affected applications.<br>\n3. Tasking of the DER Program Manager<br>\nThe DER Program Manager is tasked, effective immediately, with producing the following queue-cleanup inputs. Each input is to be closed on the record — with the supporting document, tariff citation, standard, or operating guideline named in the file — before it is reported as complete.<br>\n3.1 On-hold list dispositions. Work the current on-hold population by hold-reason category. For each project, determine the correct disposition under the applicable tariff provisions and program policy, and clear holds where the underlying reason has resolved. Cross-reference hold-reason categories against the relevant queue and financial records. Business-day counts must follow the standard business-day convention.<br>\n3.2 Applications-intake corrections. Review the active queue for duplicate, ghost, and stale-status records. Where a customer has filed a new application at a site with a prior, silently abandoned filing, resolve the intake question in writing before either record is closed or merged. Queue-date assignment must follow the written policy.<br>\n3.3 Standards-conflict routing. For every project currently sitting on a standards-clarification hold, determine and execute the correct disposition under applicable program standards and operating procedures.<br>\n3.4 Equipment allocation. Convene the equipment-allocation determination for the sites that have completed construction and are awaiting utility-owned equipment. Rank the sites under the applicable equipment deployment prioritization guideline in accordance with its procedures, and match against the current supplier delivery schedule reported by SCADA/EMS Engineering.<br>\n3.5 Aggregation determinations. For applications proposing new DER at sites that may host existing generation, apply the applicable aggregation provision before finalizing program-scope routing.<br>\n3.6 Capacity verifications. For any application on abnormality hold due to a stated-vs-nameplate capacity discrepancy, verify the nameplate from the application's technical documentation and apply the applicable interconnection standard to any claimed operating limit below inverter nameplate.<br>\n3.7 Hosting-capacity corrections. Before any capacity figure from the published hosting-capacity map is communicated externally, confirm that the figure reflects the current state of the affected feeder. A priority refresh is to be requested from Distribution Planning under the published refresh-prioritization criteria.<br>\n3.8 Witness-test dispositions. Verify each inverter model against the applicable certified equipment list under the commissioning procedure in effect at the time of determination. Waiver eligibility is to be determined against the applicable certified equipment list.<br>\n3.9 Supplemental-review reversals. Review any supplemental-review determination on file for consistency with current program procedures. Where the record requires revision, update the file and flag any prior memorandum that has been superseded accordingly.<br>\n4. Reporting cadence<br>\nThe DER Program Manager will provide weekly progress reports to Regulatory Affairs, with copy to the VP office, every Friday by close of business beginning 07/04/2025 and continuing through 09/12/2025. The weekly report will comprise:<br>\ncounts by disposition category, aligned to the categories in §3 above;<br>\nitems closed on the record during the reporting week, with the governing citation named;<br>\nexceptions requiring Regulatory Affairs or VP attention, including any project at risk of missing 08/15/2025 or 09/10/2025;<br>\nforward-look for the following reporting week.<br>\nReports are to be delivered in writing. Verbal updates do not substitute.<br>\n5. General provisions<br>\nEvery disposition reached under this tasking must be tariff-defensible on the face of the file. Cite the specific tariff section, standard, operating guideline, or bulletin that supports the action. Where the record does not support the disposition on paper, the disposition is not ready and is to be held rather than reported.<br>\nFollow the noticing, payment-deadline, and hold-clearance procedures as written. Standards or procedural conflicts route through the established referral process; they are not to be resolved informally in the queue. Queue-date treatment must remain within what the tariff and program policy permit.<br>\nRegulatory Affairs will coordinate with Interconnection Studies (Meera Vellanki) on queue and notice records, and with the DER Planning Group (Ivo Grantham) on study status, on any input required for the League response or the APUC briefing. The DER Program Manager remains the single point of accountability for the queue-cleanup inputs enumerated in §3.<br>\nEscalate obstacles to Regulatory Affairs in writing and, where the obstacle bears on the 08/15/2025 or 09/10/2025 dates, to the VP office concurrently.<br>\n/s/<br>\nHarriet Ngoyi<br>\nDirector, Regulatory Affairs<br>\nAlleniom Utilities<br>\nharriet.ngoyi@alleniom.com · (612) 555-0104</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Doc no.</th>\n<th>RA-2025-07-01</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Rev</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Date</td>\n<td>07/01/2025</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Owner</td>\n<td>Regulatory Affairs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>	[]	markdown	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	markdown	en	4	4	{}
21	der-program/correspondence/2025-07-07-apuc-information-request	2dbfd85322ac2bb0ac9e	2025-07-07 APUC Information Request	APUC information request for queue accounting and backlog-cause summary ahead of 09/10/2025 briefing	f	t				# 2025-07-07 APUC Information Request\n\nALLERON PUBLIC UTILITIES COMMISSION\nEnergy Resources Division\nDocket:  APUC-2025-ER-014\nRef:  ERD-IR-2025-07-014\nDate:  07/07/2025\nMs. Harriet Ngoyi\nDirector, Regulatory Affairs\nAlleniom Utilities\nRe:  APUC Information Request — Alleniom Utilities DER Interconnection Program Backlog\nDocket APUC-2025-ER-014\nDear Ms. Ngoyi:\nThe Energy Resources Division of the Alleron Public Utilities Commission (APUC) is in receipt of\ncorrespondence from the Alleron Municipal League dated 06/20/2025 (AML Ref:\nAML-2025-0620-DER), copied to the Division, escalating on behalf of fourteen member municipalities\nand school districts a program-level concern with the current pace of commercial-scale Distributed\nEnergy Resource (DER) interconnection processing on the Alleniom Utilities distribution system. The\nLeague's letter identifies delays affecting projects at public schools, municipal water and wastewater\nfacilities, community centers, and public libraries across multiple communities within the Company's\nservice territory, and raises questions regarding queue status, hold posture, and expected energization\ntimelines that fall within the Division's continuing oversight of the Company's APUC-filed\ninterconnection tariff.\nThe Division intends to be in a position to brief the Commission on the matter at the previously\nscheduled DER program briefing on 09/10/2025. In advance of that briefing, and to permit the Division\nto assess the Company's DER interconnection program on the basis of the Company's own records\nrather than the League's characterization, this letter requests the following information from Alleniom\nUtilities.\n1.  Commercial DER queue accounting.\nFor the commercial DER interconnection program (i.e., the ≥500 kW program-scope portfolio\nadministered by the Company's DER Program Office; simplified small-systems process filings are\noutside the scope of this request), the Company is requested to provide a complete queue accounting as\nof the most recent full week preceding the response date, including at minimum the following,\npresented at the individual-application level and separately summarized in totals:\na.  The count and identity of all applications currently in active processing status, together with each\napplication's assigned application number, applicant name, site, nameplate capacity, filing date,\ncurrent processing step, and the study or decision milestone next due.\nOne Capitol Plaza, Suite 900  ·  Cordwyn, Alleron 14CW  ·  (651) 555-0180  ·  apuc.alleron.gov\nHalston, Alleron 01HA\n4200 Veldmere Boulevard\n\n---\n\nb.  The count and identity of all applications currently on on-hold, pending-customer,\npending-standards, or suspended status, together with the specific hold reason of record for\neach, the party responsible for the next action, the information or resource required to release the\nhold, and the date the hold was established.\nc.  The count and identity of all applications for which one or more study or processing timelines\nestablished under the Company's filed interconnection tariff, procedures, or executed study\nagreements have been exceeded as of the reporting date, together with the timeline of record, the\nactual elapsed time, and the current cause of the overrun.\nd.  The count and identity of all applications withdrawn — whether by written applicant request, by\napplication of the tariff's deemed-withdrawal provision, or otherwise — during the twelve\nmonths preceding the reporting date, together with the effective date of each withdrawal and the\nrecorded basis for it.\ne.  The count and identity of all formal Notices of Impending Withdrawal, Notices of\nCancellation, or equivalent written notices issued by the Company to applicants during the\ntwelve months preceding the reporting date, together with the notice date, the date sent, the\nmethod of service, the response deadline computed under the tariff, and the current disposition\n(response received / no response / deemed withdrawn / other).\nWhere any of the foregoing counts is materially affected by data-integrity items known to the Company\nrequested to identify those items and provide the counts both as they presently appear in the Company's\nqueue records and as the Company understands the underlying facts to be.\n2.  Backlog-cause summary.\nThe Company is further requested to provide a written summary describing, in the Company's own\ncharacterization, the principal causes of the current backlog in the commercial DER interconnection\nprogram. The summary should address at a minimum: application volume relative to processing\ncapacity; the availability and lead time of utility-owned equipment required for energization (including\nbut not limited to RTU and protection equipment); the status of any internal standards reconciliation\nactivity affecting screening or protection determinations; the sufficiency of certified-equipment\nreferences relied upon by the Company; the sufficiency of hosting-capacity information made available\nto prospective applicants; and any other factors the Company identifies as materially contributing to the\ncurrent queue posture. Supporting quantitative material (equipment delivery schedules, staffing\nutilization, study cycle times, and the like) is welcome and, where practicable, requested.\n3.  Format and timing.\nResponses should be provided in writing, addressed to the undersigned at the Division's Cordwyn\noffice, with electronic copies to the docket. Line-item responses under Item 1 above may be provided\nby workbook attachment provided each attachment is unambiguously cross-referenced to the\ncorresponding item. Responses should be received by the Division sufficiently in advance of the\n09/10/2025 briefing to allow the Division adequate review; the Division requests that the Company\nprovide its response no later than 08/22/2025, and, where any portion of the response cannot be\nfurnished by that date, that the Company so advise the Division in writing no later than 08/08/2025 with\nan explanation and a proposed alternative date.\n4.  Continuing dialogue.\n(for example, timing differences, reconciliation items, or other known data-quality issues), the Company is\n\n---\n\nThis request is issued in the ordinary course of the Division's monitoring of interconnection program\nperformance under the Company's APUC-filed tariff. The Division has no present view as to the merits\nof the underlying matter and takes no position on the specific project-level concerns raised in the\nLeague's 06/20/2025 letter, which remain properly a matter between the League's member entities and\nthe Company in the first instance. The Division's interest at this stage is limited to the queue accounting\nand backlog-cause information described above, together with the Company's plan for restoring\npredictable processing to the program. The Division remains available to discuss scope, format, or\ntiming questions at Company request.\nThe Division appreciates the Company's continued cooperation and looks forward to the Company's\nresponse.\nSincerely,\nNia Fairweather\nNia Fairweather\nDirector, Energy Resources Division\nAlleron Public Utilities Commission\nnia.fairweather@apuc.alleron.gov  ·  (651) 555-0180\ncc:\nCommissioners, Alleron Public Utilities Commission (docket file, APUC-2025-ER-014)\nMr. Alden Pryce, Executive Director, Alleron Municipal League (for the Division's information, consistent with\nthe League's 06/20/2025 correspondence)\nEnclosures: None.	<h1 id="2025-07-07-apuc-information-request">2025-07-07 APUC Information Request</h1>\n<p>ALLERON PUBLIC UTILITIES COMMISSION<br>\nEnergy Resources Division<br>\nDocket:  APUC-2025-ER-014<br>\nRef:  ERD-IR-2025-07-014<br>\nDate:  07/07/2025<br>\nMs. Harriet Ngoyi<br>\nDirector, Regulatory Affairs<br>\nAlleniom Utilities<br>\nRe:  APUC Information Request — Alleniom Utilities DER Interconnection Program Backlog<br>\nDocket APUC-2025-ER-014<br>\nDear Ms. Ngoyi:<br>\nThe Energy Resources Division of the Alleron Public Utilities Commission (APUC) is in receipt of<br>\ncorrespondence from the Alleron Municipal League dated 06/20/2025 (AML Ref:<br>\nAML-2025-0620-DER), copied to the Division, escalating on behalf of fourteen member municipalities<br>\nand school districts a program-level concern with the current pace of commercial-scale Distributed<br>\nEnergy Resource (DER) interconnection processing on the Alleniom Utilities distribution system. The<br>\nLeague's letter identifies delays affecting projects at public schools, municipal water and wastewater<br>\nfacilities, community centers, and public libraries across multiple communities within the Company's<br>\nservice territory, and raises questions regarding queue status, hold posture, and expected energization<br>\ntimelines that fall within the Division's continuing oversight of the Company's APUC-filed<br>\ninterconnection tariff.<br>\nThe Division intends to be in a position to brief the Commission on the matter at the previously<br>\nscheduled DER program briefing on 09/10/2025. In advance of that briefing, and to permit the Division<br>\nto assess the Company's DER interconnection program on the basis of the Company's own records<br>\nrather than the League's characterization, this letter requests the following information from Alleniom<br>\nUtilities.<br>\n1.  Commercial DER queue accounting.<br>\nFor the commercial DER interconnection program (i.e., the ≥500 kW program-scope portfolio<br>\nadministered by the Company's DER Program Office; simplified small-systems process filings are<br>\noutside the scope of this request), the Company is requested to provide a complete queue accounting as<br>\nof the most recent full week preceding the response date, including at minimum the following,<br>\npresented at the individual-application level and separately summarized in totals:<br>\na.  The count and identity of all applications currently in active processing status, together with each<br>\napplication's assigned application number, applicant name, site, nameplate capacity, filing date,<br>\ncurrent processing step, and the study or decision milestone next due.<br>\nOne Capitol Plaza, Suite 900  ·  Cordwyn, Alleron 14CW  ·  (651) 555-0180  ·  apuc.alleron.gov<br>\nHalston, Alleron 01HA<br>\n4200 Veldmere Boulevard</p>\n<hr>\n<p>b.  The count and identity of all applications currently on on-hold, pending-customer,<br>\npending-standards, or suspended status, together with the specific hold reason of record for<br>\neach, the party responsible for the next action, the information or resource required to release the<br>\nhold, and the date the hold was established.<br>\nc.  The count and identity of all applications for which one or more study or processing timelines<br>\nestablished under the Company's filed interconnection tariff, procedures, or executed study<br>\nagreements have been exceeded as of the reporting date, together with the timeline of record, the<br>\nactual elapsed time, and the current cause of the overrun.<br>\nd.  The count and identity of all applications withdrawn — whether by written applicant request, by<br>\napplication of the tariff's deemed-withdrawal provision, or otherwise — during the twelve<br>\nmonths preceding the reporting date, together with the effective date of each withdrawal and the<br>\nrecorded basis for it.<br>\ne.  The count and identity of all formal Notices of Impending Withdrawal, Notices of<br>\nCancellation, or equivalent written notices issued by the Company to applicants during the<br>\ntwelve months preceding the reporting date, together with the notice date, the date sent, the<br>\nmethod of service, the response deadline computed under the tariff, and the current disposition<br>\n(response received / no response / deemed withdrawn / other).<br>\nWhere any of the foregoing counts is materially affected by data-integrity items known to the Company<br>\nrequested to identify those items and provide the counts both as they presently appear in the Company's<br>\nqueue records and as the Company understands the underlying facts to be.<br>\n2.  Backlog-cause summary.<br>\nThe Company is further requested to provide a written summary describing, in the Company's own<br>\ncharacterization, the principal causes of the current backlog in the commercial DER interconnection<br>\nprogram. The summary should address at a minimum: application volume relative to processing<br>\ncapacity; the availability and lead time of utility-owned equipment required for energization (including<br>\nbut not limited to RTU and protection equipment); the status of any internal standards reconciliation<br>\nactivity affecting screening or protection determinations; the sufficiency of certified-equipment<br>\nreferences relied upon by the Company; the sufficiency of hosting-capacity information made available<br>\nto prospective applicants; and any other factors the Company identifies as materially contributing to the<br>\ncurrent queue posture. Supporting quantitative material (equipment delivery schedules, staffing<br>\nutilization, study cycle times, and the like) is welcome and, where practicable, requested.<br>\n3.  Format and timing.<br>\nResponses should be provided in writing, addressed to the undersigned at the Division's Cordwyn<br>\noffice, with electronic copies to the docket. Line-item responses under Item 1 above may be provided<br>\nby workbook attachment provided each attachment is unambiguously cross-referenced to the<br>\ncorresponding item. Responses should be received by the Division sufficiently in advance of the<br>\n09/10/2025 briefing to allow the Division adequate review; the Division requests that the Company<br>\nprovide its response no later than 08/22/2025, and, where any portion of the response cannot be<br>\nfurnished by that date, that the Company so advise the Division in writing no later than 08/08/2025 with<br>\nan explanation and a proposed alternative date.<br>\n4.  Continuing dialogue.<br>\n(for example, timing differences, reconciliation items, or other known data-quality issues), the Company is</p>\n<hr>\n<p>This request is issued in the ordinary course of the Division's monitoring of interconnection program<br>\nperformance under the Company's APUC-filed tariff. The Division has no present view as to the merits<br>\nof the underlying matter and takes no position on the specific project-level concerns raised in the<br>\nLeague's 06/20/2025 letter, which remain properly a matter between the League's member entities and<br>\nthe Company in the first instance. The Division's interest at this stage is limited to the queue accounting<br>\nand backlog-cause information described above, together with the Company's plan for restoring<br>\npredictable processing to the program. The Division remains available to discuss scope, format, or<br>\ntiming questions at Company request.<br>\nThe Division appreciates the Company's continued cooperation and looks forward to the Company's<br>\nresponse.<br>\nSincerely,<br>\nNia Fairweather<br>\nNia Fairweather<br>\nDirector, Energy Resources Division<br>\nAlleron Public Utilities Commission<br>\nnia.fairweather@apuc.alleron.gov  ·  (651) 555-0180<br>\ncc:<br>\nCommissioners, Alleron Public Utilities Commission (docket file, APUC-2025-ER-014)<br>\nMr. Alden Pryce, Executive Director, Alleron Municipal League (for the Division's information, consistent with<br>\nthe League's 06/20/2025 correspondence)<br>\nEnclosures: None.</p>	[]	markdown	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	markdown	en	14	14	{}
