a</td>\n<td>Refresh prioritization criteria: application date, capacity requested, DER category (net metering vs pure generation). Feeders with the earliest pending pre-application or application date receive priority for re-study, followed by feeders where the requested capacity is largest relative to the currently published available figure, followed by DER category weighting per the net metering vs pure generation distinction (pure-generation queue entries are weighted more heavily against feeder headroom than net-metering behind-the-meter entries at equivalent nameplate).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Basis</td>\n<td>Feeder-level planning study using recorded peak and minimum load, existing interconnected DER of record at the time of the underlying study, and standard thermal / voltage / protection screens per Alleniom Distribution Planning methodology.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DER Category legend</td>\n<td>"Net metering" — behind-the-meter DER netted against customer load at the point of common coupling. "Pure generation" — export-only front-of-the-meter DER. "Mixed" — feeder hosts a combination of net-metering and pure-generation interconnections.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Voltage class note</td>\n<td>Nominal voltages given as feeder primary line-to-line kV.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scope</td>\n<td>Alleniom distribution system, all substations serving in-scope DER territory. Sub-transmission and transmission-connected resources excluded.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Applicability</td>\n<td>Values are the published available capacity figures as of the publication date shown above. Applicants and pre-application requesters should consult Distribution Planning for confirmation before finalizing project sizing decisions on any feeder shown. Published values do not reflect interconnection queue activity recorded after the publication date; the active-queue register maintained by the DER Program Office is the record of that activity. A pre-application capacity response is prepared agai