>\n<td>Models delisted between editions are removed from the current tabs without further notation; historical certification records are retained by Standards Engineering.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>	[]	markdown	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	markdown	en	9	9	{}
41	der-program/standards/dis-01-rev4-der-interconnection-standard	0c23df5fee64903e8f6d	DIS-01 Rev4 DER Interconnection Standard	Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard Rev 4 effective 03/01/2024 based on IEEE 1547-2018	f	t				# DIS-01 Rev4 DER Interconnection Standard\n\nDIS-01 Rev 4 — Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard\nPage 1\nALLENIOM UTILITIES\nDER INTERCONNECTION STANDARD\nDoc no.\nRev\nEffective date\nOwner\nDIS-01\nRev 4\n03/01/2024\nAlleniom Standards Engineering — Evan Solberg\nDocument control\nItem\nValue\nDocument number\nDIS-01\nRevision\nRev 4\nEffective date\n03/01/2024\nSupersedes\nDIS-01 Rev 3 (12/01/2021)\nBasis\nIEEE 1547-2018\nOwner\nAlleniom Standards Engineering (Evan Solberg)\nApproved by\nManager, Standards Engineering\nDistribution\nAlleniom DER Program Office; Interconnection Studies; DER Planning Group;\nSCADA/EMS Engineering; Regulatory Affairs; contracted developers on request\nNext scheduled review\n03/01/2027\nRevision history\nRev\nDate\nSummary\nRev 1\n06/01/2016\nInitial issue.\nRev 2\n09/01/2018\nAlignment with IEEE 1547-2018. Introduced Category 1/2/3 protective-equipment table.\nRev 3\n12/01/2021\nProgram-scope threshold set at 500 kW; added §3.4 aggregation.\nRev 4\n03/01/2024\nAdded §3.5 shared-POI eligibility; added §3.6 controlled-capacity recognition (UL 1741 / UL\n3141 PCS + Control Narrative); §7 restated to require withdrawal and reapplication for\npost-execution capacity or POI changes; Table 5-1 rewritten by category.\n§1 Purpose and Scope\nThis standard establishes the technical and procedural requirements for interconnection of distributed energy\nresources (DER) to the Alleniom Utilities distribution system. It is based on IEEE 1547-2018 and applicable state\ninterconnection rules filed with the Alleron Public Utilities Commission (APUC), and it applies to all DER classes\ndefined in §3.\nDIS-01 Rev 4 governs:\n classification of DER facilities by aggregate nameplate capacity at the point of common coupling (§3);\n eligibility to share an existing point of interconnection where legacy equipment is on site (§3.5);\n recognition of a controlled operating limit below nameplate (§3.6);\n protective and control equipment requirements by class and category (§5);\n\n\n---\n\nDIS-01 Rev 4 — Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard Page 2\nDIS-01 Rev 4 · Effective 03/01/2024\n treatment of post-execution changes to executed interconnection service agreements (§7).\nThis standard is read together with the APUC-filed interconnection tariff (§12). Where a topic is addressed by both\nthis standard and the tariff, the tariff controls. Requirements for real-time monitoring and communications integration\nare established under TCS-02 and are not restated here except by cross-reference.\n§2 References\nThe following documents are referenced within this standard. Unless otherwise noted, the latest published revision\napplies.\n IEEE 1547-2018 — IEEE Standard for Interconnection and Interoperability of Distributed Energy Resources\nwith Associated Electric Power Systems Interfaces.\n DPS-07 Rev 2 — Alleniom Distribution Protection Standard (effective 09/15/2023). Establishes distribution\nprotection requirements including protective devices at points of interconnection with export-capable in-scope\nfacilities.\n TCS-02 — Alleniom Telemetry & Communications Standard (effective 06/01/2021). Governs utility-owned RTU\ninstallation and SCADA telemetry for DER at and above the program-scope threshold; establishes the condition\nfor permission to operate.\n CEL-2025-Q2 — Alleniom Certified Equipment List (published 04/30/2025). Listing basis: UL 1741-SB and UL\n3141. The CEL front matter restates §3.6 of this standard.\n ERP-9 — Alleniom Engineering Referral Procedure (Form ER-11). §3 routes any standards \n§3 DER Classification\nDER facilities are classified by aggregate nameplate capacity at the point of common coupling, in accordance with\nIEEE 1547-2018. Class thresholds, applicable review path, and equipment references are stated in Table 3-1.\nCommercial small-scale 500 kW–1 MW; medium-scale 1 MW–5 MW; program scope ≥500 kW; sub-500 kW DER\n(commercial and residential) is handled under the Simplified Small-Systems Process, outside the DER Program.\nDefinitions of terms used in this standard follow IEEE 1547-2018 and, where applicable, the definitions in the\nAPUC-filed tariff. The terms aggregate nameplate capacity and point of common coupling (PCC) have the meanings\nassigned by IEEE 1547-2018 and reflected in Company practice. The terms point of interconnection (POI),\nexport-capable, and pure-generation site are used in accordance with their ordinary meaning in utility\ninterconnection practice.\n\n---\n\nDIS-01 Rev 4 — Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard\nPage 3\nDIS-01 Rev 4 · Effective 03/01/2024\nTable 3-1 — DER classes\nClass\nAggregate\nnameplate at PCC\nProgram status\nReview path\nProtective\nequipment\nreference\nResidential / small\ncommercial\n< 500 kW\nSimplified Small-Systems\nProcess — outside the\nDER Program\nSimplified small-systems\nscreening\nTable 5-1, Category\n1\nCommercial small-scale\n500 kW – 1 MW\nIn DER Program scope\nInitial screening +\nsupplemental review under\nSRP-3\nTable 5-1, Category\n2\nMedium-scale\n1 MW – 5 MW\nIn DER Program scope\nInitial screening + system\nimpact study\nTable 5-1, Category\n3\nLarge-scale (out of scope\nfor this standard)\n> 5 MW\nHandled under transmissio\nn-interconnection\nprocedures\nFull study process\nNot addressed\nThe program-scope threshold is 500 kW. Facilities below 500 kW, whether commercial or residential in customer\nclass, are routed to the Simplified Small-Systems Process and are not administered by the DER Program.\n§3.1 Determination of aggregate nameplate capacity\nAggregate nameplate capacity is the sum of the manufacturer-rated ac output ratings of all generating equipment\ninterconnected, or proposed to be interconnected, at a single point of common coupling. Manufacturer datasheets\ngovern; derated or "operating" figures do not substitute for nameplate except as provided in §3.6.\n§3.2 Point of common coupling\nThe point of common coupling is the point at which the DER facility is electrically connected to the Company's\ndistribution system, as further defined in IEEE 1547-2018. The PCC is distinct from the point of interconnection (POI),\nthe latter being the physical demarcation of Company-owned protective and metering equipment.\n§3.3 Determination on the basis of application\nClassification is determined on the facility as filed and as physically configured on site. Filing captions, project titles,\nand cover-letter characterizations do not alter classification; the connection diagram, the equipment schedule, and\nthe address of record govern.\n§3.4 Aggregation\nCo-located DER behind the same point of common coupling counts toward one site total.\nPreviously interconnected legacy equipment counts toward the aggregate regardless of the vintage of its permission\nto operate, regardless of whether it predates any current Alleniom standard, and regardless of the sequence or form\nin which subsequent applications are submitted at the same PCC. The aggregate governs the facility class and the\nresulting program-scope determination.\nAn applicant proposing a new DER at a premises that already hosts DER shall identify the existing equipment on the\napplication form and shall submit a one-line diagram showing the electrical relationship of the proposed equipment,\nthe existing equipment, the customer load, the customer meter, and the PCC. Interconnection Studies verifies against\nthe Company existing-DER register.\n§3.5 Shared-POI eligibility\nA new DER may share an existing POI only where the combined facility, including legacy equipment, can be brought\nto current monitoring/control standards; otherwise a separate POI and a new application are required. Where the\nexisting system operates behind the meter, a new DER may instead be interconnected front-of-the-meter under its\nown service and POI, and is then evaluated on its own capacity without aggregation.\n\n\n---\n\nDIS-01 Rev 4 — Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard\nPage 4\nDIS-01 Rev 4 · Effective 03/01/2024\nThe two dispositions available under §3.5 are:\n1.  Shared POI, aggregated. The combined facility (legacy equipment plus proposed equipment) is brought to\ncurrent monitoring/control standards as prescribed by TCS-02 and by Table 5-1 at the aggregate class. The\napplicant is responsible for the retrofit and for demonstrating retrofit feasibility to Interconnection Studies. Where\nfeasibility is confirmed, the application proceeds at the aggregate capacity at the shared POI.\n2.  Separate POI, non-aggregated front-of-the-meter alternative. Where the existing system operates behind\nthe meter and the applicant elects to interconnect the new DER front-of-the-meter under a separate service and\nits own POI, the new DER is evaluated on its own capacity, without aggregation with the legacy equipment.\nClass placement, review path, and Table 5-1 category are determined from the new DER's capacity alone.\nRetrofit feasibility of legacy equipment is a factual question the Company will not decide from records alone; the\napplicant or its authorized developer must confirm feasibility in writing. Absent that confirmation, the shared-POI path\nunder §3.5(1) is not available and the application is redirected to §3.5(2) or withdrawn.\n§3.6 Controlled-capacity recognition\nA limit below nameplate is recognized only with a valid UL 1741 / UL 3141 PCS certification plus an approved Control\nNarrative — sufficient in themselves for recognition. Where the site's arrangement includes utility equipment, the\nrecognized limit is enforced as applicable: telemetry monitoring, and on pure-generation sites the POI recloser's ANSI\n32 directional-power element set to the recognized limit and tripping on violation.\nRequired submissions. To have a controlled operating limit recognized in place of aggregate nameplate for\nprogram-scope and study purposes, the applicant shall submit both of the following:\n1.  A valid Power Control System (PCS) certification issued under UL 1741 (including Supplement SB where\napplicable) and/or UL 3141, identifying the specific equipment arrangement at the applicant's site and the\nsetpoint range.\n2.  An approved Control Narrative — a written engineering document describing how the site's control system\nenforces the requested operating limit, including the sensed quantities, the response actions, the response\ntimes, the interlocks, and any allowable modes and their setpoint ceilings.\nThese two documents, taken together, are sufficient in themselves to establish recognition of the controlled limit.\nRecognition establishes the capacity at which the facility is reviewed, classified, and studied.\nEnforcement backstops as arrangement provides. Where the site's arrangement includes utility equipment, the\nrecognized limit is enforced by the arrangement's existing equipment as follows:\n Telemetry monitoring. For sites at or above the TCS-02 threshold, the Company's RTU-to-SCADA telemetry\nmonitors real-power export at the point of interconnection against the recognized limit. Sustained deviation\ntriggers investigation under TCS-02 and may result in a hold on permission to operate.\n POI recloser directional-power element (pure-generation sites). On pure-generation sites — sites at which\nno customer load is served behind the point of common coupling — where a utility POI recloser is installed per\nTable 5-1 and DPS-07, the recloser's ANSI 32 directional-power element is set to the recognized limit and trips\nthe site on violation. The ANSI 32 element is inherently available in modern reclosers and does not require\nadditional utility hardware.\nEnforcement provisions apply only as the site's arrangement provides them. Their absence at a particular site does\nnot reopen the recognition determination once the two required submissions are on file.\nNo controlled limit without submissions. In the absence of both a valid UL 1741 / UL 3141 PCS certification and\nan approved Control Narrative, nameplate governs for classification, program-scope determination, and study.\nCertified Equipment List. CEL-2025-Q2 restates this section in its front matter. Listing of a PCS on the current CEL\ndoes not by itself constitute recognition of a controlled limit for a particular site; the applicant's site-specific\ncertification and Control Narrative are always required.\n\n\n---\n\nDIS-01 Rev 4 — Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard Page 5\nDIS-01 Rev 4 · Effective 03/01/2024\n§4 Screening and Study Process\nApplications in DER Program scope proceed through initial technical screening under Interconnection Studies.\nFacilities not passing initial screens may be evaluated under supplemental review per SRP-3 or advance to a system\nimpact study. This standard does not restate SRP-3; the daytime minimum-load convention and other screen\nmethodologies are prescribed there.\n§5 Protective and Control Equipment\nProtective equipment requirements are stated by category in Table 5-1, keyed to the classes of §3.\nTable 5-1 — Protective equipment by category\nItem Category 1 (< 500 kW) Category 2 (500 kW – 1 MW\nexport sites)\nCategory 3 (1 MW – 5 MW\nexport sites)\nApplicable class Residential / small\ncommercial\nCommercial small-scale Medium-scale\nDER-side disconnect Required (customer-owned\nlockable visible-break)\nRequired (customer-owned\nlockable visible-break)\nRequired (customer-owned\nlockable visible-break)\nInterconnection protective\nfunctions (IEEE 1547-2018\nclause 4)\nProvided by certified inverterProvided by certified inverter\nand/or dedicated protection\nrelay\nDedicated protection relay\nrequired\nDER breaker with protection\nrelay scheme (for\nload/generation-decoupling\narrangements)\nNot required As specified per site by\nprotection engineering\nAs specified per site by\nprotection engineering\nPOI recloser (utility-owned)Not required Category 2 export sites —\nPOI recloser: not required\nRequired\nAnti-islanding Certified inverter functions\nper IEEE 1547-2018\nCertified inverter functions per\nIEEE 1547-2018;\nsupplemented by relay scheme\nwhere dedicated relay is\ninstalled\nDedicated relay scheme with\ntransfer trip where required by\nprotection engineering\nDirectional-power element\n(ANSI 32) — pure-generation\nsites\nNot applicable Set on POI recloser where\ninstalled; used to enforce a\n§3.6 controlled limit where\napplicable\nSet on POI recloser; used to\nenforce a §3.6 controlled limit\nwhere applicable\nMetering Bidirectional revenue meterBidirectional revenue meterBidirectional revenue meter,\nprimary or secondary as\nfeeder configuration requires\nUtility RTU and SCADA\ntelemetry\nNot required Required per TCS-02 (≥500\nkW)\nRequired per TCS-02\nNotes to Table 5-1.\n1.  Category placement reflects the export configuration prevailing at the site. Where a site's configuration warrants\nrequirements differing from the tabular default — including, for example, load/generation-decoupling\narrangements in which generation is isolated by a DER breaker with a dedicated protection relay scheme —\nprotection engineering may specify the applicable requirements through the Engineering Referral Procedure\n(ERP-9 §3, Form ER-11).\n\n---\n\nDIS-01 Rev 4 — Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard\nPage 6\nDIS-01 Rev 4 · Effective 03/01/2024\n2.  Table 5-1 addresses protective and control equipment. Monitoring and communications requirements are\nestablished under TCS-02.\n3.  Where §3.6 recognition is in effect at a pure-generation site with a utility POI recloser, the ANSI 32\ndirectional-power element is set to the recognized limit and trips on violation.\n§6 Monitoring and Control\nFacilities at or above the TCS-02 program threshold shall provide real-time monitoring and control integration through\na utility-owned RTU with SCADA telemetry as prescribed by TCS-02. Live telemetry to the Company system\noperations center is a condition of permission to operate. This standard does not restate TCS-02; refer to TCS-02 §5\nfor the telemetry rule and to Alleniom system operations for RTU installation scheduling.\nSite-level control functions required by §3.6 (Power Control System recognition), by Table 5-1 (dedicated protection\nrelay schemes, DER breaker arrangements), and by ERP-9 site determinations, together satisfy the\ncontrol-integration expectations of this section as applied to the specific facility.\n§7 Capacity and Point-of-Interconnection Provisions\nPost-execution changes to executed ISA capacity or POI require withdrawal and reapplication; ISA edits are not\npermitted for capacity or POI changes.\nScope. This section governs changes proposed after an interconnection service agreement has been executed by\nboth parties. Prior to execution, ordinary revisions may be handled in the studies and drafting process without\ninvoking this section.\nPrincipal parameters. Facility capacity and point of interconnection are principal parameters of the interconnection\nservice agreement. Studies, protective-equipment scoping, and queue position each depend on these parameters as\nexecuted.\nRequired path. A customer or developer seeking to change either the executed capacity or the executed point of\ninterconnection after ISA execution shall:\n1.  Submit a written request to withdraw the current application and terminate the executed ISA;\n2.  File a new application at the desired capacity and, where applicable, the desired point of interconnection; and\n3.  Accept the new queue position corresponding to the filing date of the new application.\nThe new application is studied on its own facts. The withdrawal-and-reapplication path applies whether the requested\nchange is an increase or a decrease in capacity and whether or not the requested change is characterized by the\napplicant as involving new or existing equipment.\nOther post-execution changes. Modifications to protective schemes, telemetry endpoints, equipment models within\nthe same nameplate rating, and administrative items that do not alter capacity or POI are handled under the\nchange-order provisions of the executed ISA and are not subject to this section.\nDownstream effects. The withdrawal of an executed ISA under this section releases any related utility equipment\nreservations and any related feeder headroom allocations, which are then available to subsequent applications in\nqueue order. Studies previously performed at the withdrawn capacity are not carried forward; the new application\nreceives a new study appropriate to its filed parameters.\n§8 Records and Compliance\nInterconnection Studies retains the application file, executed ISA, and study record for each in-scope facility for the\nretention period established by Regulatory Affairs. Standards Engineering maintains the current revision of this\nstandard, the Certified Equipment List (CEL-2025-Q2 and subsequent editions), and the Engineering Referral\nProcedure (ERP-9) register of per-site determinations.\n\n\n---\n\nDIS-01 Rev 4 — Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard\nPage 7\nDIS-01 Rev 4 · Effective 03/01/2024\nAny conflict between this standard and the APUC-filed tariff is resolved in favor of the tariff. Any conflict between this\nstandard and another Alleniom operating standard encountered in screening or study is referred to per-site\nengineering determination under ERP-9 §3.\n End of DIS-01 Rev 4.	<h1 id="dis-01-rev4-der-interconnection-standard">DIS-01 Rev4 DER Interconnection Standard</h1>\n<p>DIS-01 Rev 4 — Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard<br>\nPage 1<br>\nALLENIOM UTILITIES<br>\nDER INTERCONNECTION STANDARD<br>\nDoc no.<br>\nRev<br>\nEffective date<br>\nOwner<br>\nDIS-01<br>\nRev 4<br>\n03/01/2024<br>\nAlleniom Standards Engineering — Evan Solberg<br>\nDocument control<br>\nItem<br>\nValue<br>\nDocument number<br>\nDIS-01<br>\nRevision<br>\nRev 4<br>\nEffective date<br>\n03/01/2024<br>\nSupersedes<br>\nDIS-01 Rev 3 (12/01/2021)<br>\nBasis<br>\nIEEE 1547-2018<br>\nOwner<br>\nAlleniom Standards Engineering (Evan Solberg)<br>\nApproved by<br>\nManager, Standards Engineering<br>\nDistribution<br>\nAlleniom DER Program Office; Interconnection Studies; DER Planning Group;<br>\nSCADA/EMS Engineering; Regulatory Affairs; contracted developers on request<br>\nNext scheduled review<br>\n03/01/2027<br>\nRevision history<br>\nRev<br>\nDate<br>\nSummary<br>\nRev 1<br>\n06/01/2016<br>\nInitial issue.<br>\nRev 2<br>\n09/01/2018<br>\nAlignment with IEEE 1547-2018. Introduced Category 1/2/3 protective-equipment table.<br>\nRev 3<br>\n12/01/2021<br>\nProgram-scope threshold set at 500 kW; added §3.4 aggregation.<br>\nRev 4<br>\n03/01/2024<br>\nAdded §3.5 shared-POI eligibility; added §3.6 controlled-capacity recognition (UL 1741 / UL<br>\n3141 PCS + Control Narrative); §7 restated to require withdrawal and reapplication for<br>\npost-execution capacity or POI changes; Table 5-1 rewritten by category.<br>\n§1 Purpose and Scope<br>\nThis standard establishes the technical and procedural requirements for interconnection of distributed energy<br>\nresources (DER) to the Alleniom Utilities distribution system. It is based on IEEE 1547-2018 and applicable state<br>\ninterconnection rules filed with the Alleron Public Utilities Commission (APUC), and it applies to all DER classes<br>\ndefined in §3.<br>\nDIS-01 Rev 4 governs:<br>\n classification of DER facilities by aggregate nameplate capacity at the point of common coupling (§3);<br>\n eligibility to share an existing point of interconnection where legacy equipment is on site (§3.5);<br>\n recognition of a controlled operating limit below nameplate (§3.6);<br>\n protective and control equipment requirements by class and category (§5);</p>\n<hr>\n<p>DIS-01 Rev 4 — Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard Page 2<br>\nDIS-01 Rev 4 · Effective 03/01/2024<br>\n treatment of post-execution changes to executed interconnection service agreements (§7).<br>\nThis standard is read together with the APUC-filed interconnection tariff (§12). Where a topic is addressed by both<br>\nthis standard and the tariff, the tariff controls. Requirements for real-time monitoring and communications integration<br>\nare established under TCS-02 and are not restated here except by cross-reference.<br>\n§2 References<br>\nThe following documents are referenced within this standard. Unless otherwise noted, the latest published revision<br>\napplies.<br>\n IEEE 1547-2018 — IEEE Standard for Interconnection and Interoperability of Distributed Energy Resources<br>\nwith Associated Electric Power Systems Interfaces.<br>\n DPS-07 Rev 2 — Alleniom Distribution Protection Standard (effective 09/15/2023). Establishes distribution<br>\nprotection requirements including protective devices at points of interconnection with export-capable in-scope<br>\nfacilities.<br>\n TCS-02 — Alleniom Telemetry &amp; Communications Standard (effective 06/01/2021). Governs utility-owned RTU<br>\ninstallation and SCADA telemetry for DER at and above the program-scope threshold; establishes the condition<br>\nfor permission to operate.<br>\n CEL-2025-Q2 — Alleniom Certified Equipment List (published 04/30/2025). Listing basis: UL 1741-SB and UL<br>\n3141. The CEL front matter restates §3.6 of this standard.<br>\n ERP-9 — Alleniom Engineering Referral Procedure (Form ER-11). §3 routes any standards <br>\n§3 DER Classification<br>\nDER facilities are classified by aggregate nameplate capacity at the point of common coupling, in accordance with<br>\nIEEE 1547-2018. Class thresholds, applicable review path, and equipment references are stated in Table 3-1.<br>\nCommercial small-scale 500 kW–1 MW; medium-scale 1 MW–5 MW; program scope ≥500 kW; sub-500 kW DER<br>\n(commercial and residential) is handled under the Simplified Small-Systems Process, outside the DER Program.<br>\nDefinitions of terms used in this standard follow IEEE 1547-2018 and, where applicable, the definitions in the<br>\nAPUC-filed tariff. The terms aggregate nameplate capacity and point of common coupling (PCC) have the meanings<br>\nassigned by IEEE 1547-2018 and reflected in Company practice. The terms point of interconnection (POI),<br>\nexport-capable, and pure-generation site are used in accordance with their ordinary meaning in utility<br>\ninterconnection practice.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>DIS-01 Rev 4 — Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard<br>\nPage 3<br>\nDIS-01 Rev 4 · Effective 03/01/2024<br>\nTable 3-1 — DER classes<br>\nClass<br>\nAggregate<br>\nnameplate at PCC<br>\nProgram status<br>\nReview path<br>\nProtective<br>\nequipment<br>\nreference<br>\nResidential / small<br>\ncommercial<br>\n&lt; 500 kW<br>\nSimplified Small-Systems<br>\nProcess — outside the<br>\nDER Program<br>\nSimplified small-systems<br>\nscreening<br>\nTable 5-1, Category<br>\n1<br>\nCommercial small-scale<br>\n500 kW – 1 MW<br>\nIn DER Program scope<br>\nInitial screening +<br>\nsupplemental review under<br>\nSRP-3<br>\nTable 5-1, Category<br>\n2<br>\nMedium-scale<br>\n1 MW – 5 MW<br>\nIn DER Program scope<br>\nInitial screening + system<br>\nimpact study<br>\nTable 5-1, Category<br>\n3<br>\nLarge-scale (out of scope<br>\nfor this standard)</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>5 MW<br>\nHandled under transmissio<br>\nn-interconnection<br>\nprocedures<br>\nFull study process<br>\nNot addressed<br>\nThe program-scope threshold is 500 kW. Facilities below 500 kW, whether commercial or residential in customer<br>\nclass, are routed to the Simplified Small-Systems Process and are not administered by the DER Program.<br>\n§3.1 Determination of aggregate nameplate capacity<br>\nAggregate nameplate capacity is the sum of the manufacturer-rated ac output ratings of all generating equipment<br>\ninterconnected, or proposed to be interconnected, at a single point of common coupling. Manufacturer datasheets<br>\ngovern; derated or "operating" figures do not substitute for nameplate except as provided in §3.6.<br>\n§3.2 Point of common coupling<br>\nThe point of common coupling is the point at which the DER facility is electrically connected to the Company's<br>\ndistribution system, as further defined in IEEE 1547-2018. The PCC is distinct from the point of interconnection (POI),<br>\nthe latter being the physical demarcation of Company-owned protective and metering equipment.<br>\n§3.3 Determination on the basis of application<br>\nClassification is determined on the facility as filed and as physically configured on site. Filing captions, project titles,<br>\nand cover-letter characterizations do not alter classification; the connection diagram, the equipment schedule, and<br>\nthe address of record govern.<br>\n§3.4 Aggregation<br>\nCo-located DER behind the same point of common coupling counts toward one site total.<br>\nPreviously interconnected legacy equipment counts toward the aggregate regardless of the vintage of its permission<br>\nto operate, regardless of whether it predates any current Alleniom standard, and regardless of the sequence or form<br>\nin which subsequent applications are submitted at the same PCC. The aggregate governs the facility class and the<br>\nresulting program-scope determination.<br>\nAn applicant proposing a new DER at a premises that already hosts DER shall identify the existing equipment on the<br>\napplication form and shall submit a one-line diagram showing the electrical relationship of the proposed equipment,<br>\nthe existing equipment, the customer load, the customer meter, and the PCC. Interconnection Studies verifies against<br>\nthe Company existing-DER register.<br>\n§3.5 Shared-POI eligibility<br>\nA new DER may share an existing POI only where the combined facility, including legacy equipment, can be brought<br>\nto current monitoring/control standards; otherwise a separate POI and a new application are required. Where the<br>\nexisting system operates behind the meter, a new DER may instead be interconnected front-of-the-meter under its<br>\nown service and POI, and is then evaluated on its own capacity without aggregation.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr>\n<p>DIS-01 Rev 4 — Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard<br>\nPage 4<br>\nDIS-01 Rev 4 · Effective 03/01/2024<br>\nThe two dispositions available under §3.5 are:<br>\n1.  Shared POI, aggregated. The combined facility (legacy equipment plus proposed equipment) is brought to<br>\ncurrent monitoring/control standards as prescribed by TCS-02 and by Table 5-1 at the aggregate class. The<br>\napplicant is responsible for the retrofit and for demonstrating retrofit feasibility to Interconnection Studies. Where<br>\nfeasibility is confirmed, the application proceeds at the aggregate capacity at the shared POI.<br>\n2.  Separate POI, non-aggregated front-of-the-meter alternative. Where the existing system operates behind<br>\nthe meter and the applicant elects to interconnect the new DER front-of-the-meter under a separate service and<br>\nits own POI, the new DER is evaluated on its own capacity, without aggregation with the legacy equipment.<br>\nClass placement, review path, and Table 5-1 category are determined from the new DER's capacity alone.<br>\nRetrofit feasibility of legacy equipment is a factual question the Company will not decide from records alone; the<br>\napplicant or its authorized developer must confirm feasibility in writing. Absent that confirmation, the shared-POI path<br>\nunder §3.5(1) is not available and the application is redirected to §3.5(2) or withdrawn.<br>\n§3.6 Controlled-capacity recognition<br>\nA limit below nameplate is recognized only with a valid UL 1741 / UL 3141 PCS certification plus an approved Control<br>\nNarrative — sufficient in themselves for recognition. Where the site's arrangement includes utility equipment, the<br>\nrecognized limit is enforced as applicable: telemetry monitoring, and on pure-generation sites the POI recloser's ANSI<br>\n32 directional-power element set to the recognized limit and tripping on violation.<br>\nRequired submissions. To have a controlled operating limit recognized in place of aggregate nameplate for<br>\nprogram-scope and study purposes, the applicant shall submit both of the following:<br>\n1.  A valid Power Control System (PCS) certification issued under UL 1741 (including Supplement SB where<br>\napplicable) and/or UL 3141, identifying the specific equipment arrangement at the applicant's site and the<br>\nsetpoint range.<br>\n2.  An approved Control Narrative — a written engineering document describing how the site's control system<br>\nenforces the requested operating limit, including the sensed quantities, the response actions, the response<br>\ntimes, the interlocks, and any allowable modes and their setpoint ceilings.<br>\nThese two documents, taken together, are sufficient in themselves to establish recognition of the controlled limit.<br>\nRecognition establishes the capacity at which the facility is reviewed, classified, and studied.<br>\nEnforcement backstops as arrangement provides. Where the site's arrangement includes utility equipment, the<br>\nrecognized limit is enforced by the arrangement's existing equipment as follows:<br>\n Telemetry monitoring. For sites at or above the TCS-02 threshold, the Company's RTU-to-SCADA telemetry<br>\nmonitors real-power export at the point of interconnection against the recognized limit. Sustained deviation<br>\ntriggers investigation under TCS-02 and may result in a hold on permission to operate.<br>\n POI recloser directional-power element (pure-generation sites). On pure-generation sites — sites at which<br>\nno customer load is served behind the point of common coupling — where a utility POI recloser is installed per<br>\nTable 5-1 and DPS-07, the recloser's ANSI 32 directional-power element is set to the recognized limit and trips<br>\nthe site on violation. The ANSI 32 element is inherently available in modern reclosers and does not require<br>\nadditional utility hardware.<br>\nEnforcement provisions apply only as the site's arrangement provides them. Their absence at a particular site does<br>\nnot reopen the recognition determination once the two required submissions are on file.<br>\nNo controlled limit without submissions. In the absence of both a valid UL 1741 / UL 3141 PCS certification and<br>\nan approved Control Narrative, nameplate governs for classification, program-scope determination, and study.<br>\nCertified Equipment List. CEL-2025-Q2 restates this section in its front matter. Listing of a PCS on the current CEL<br>\ndoes not by itself constitute recognition of a controlled limit for a particular site; the applicant's site-specific<br>\ncertification and Control Narrative are always required.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>DIS-01 Rev 4 — Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard Page 5<br>\nDIS-01 Rev 4 · Effective 03/01/2024<br>\n§4 Screening and Study Process<br>\nApplications in DER Program scope proceed through initial technical screening under Interconnection Studies.<br>\nFacilities not passing initial screens may be evaluated under supplemental review per SRP-3 or advance to a system<br>\nimpact study. This standard does not restate SRP-3; the daytime minimum-load convention and other screen<br>\nmethodologies are prescribed there.<br>\n§5 Protective and Control Equipment<br>\nProtective equipment requirements are stated by category in Table 5-1, keyed to the classes of §3.<br>\nTable 5-1 — Protective equipment by category<br>\nItem Category 1 (&lt; 500 kW) Category 2 (500 kW – 1 MW<br>\nexport sites)<br>\nCategory 3 (1 MW – 5 MW<br>\nexport sites)<br>\nApplicable class Residential / small<br>\ncommercial<br>\nCommercial small-scale Medium-scale<br>\nDER-side disconnect Required (customer-owned<br>\nlockable visible-break)<br>\nRequired (customer-owned<br>\nlockable visible-break)<br>\nRequired (customer-owned<br>\nlockable visible-break)<br>\nInterconnection protective<br>\nfunctions (IEEE 1547-2018<br>\nclause 4)<br>\nProvided by certified inverterProvided by certified inverter<br>\nand/or dedicated protection<br>\nrelay<br>\nDedicated protection relay<br>\nrequired<br>\nDER breaker with protection<br>\nrelay scheme (for<br>\nload/generation-decoupling<br>\narrangements)<br>\nNot required As specified per site by<br>\nprotection engineering<br>\nAs specified per site by<br>\nprotection engineering<br>\nPOI recloser (utility-owned)Not required Category 2 export sites —<br>\nPOI recloser: not required<br>\nRequired<br>\nAnti-islanding Certified inverter functions<br>\nper IEEE 1547-2018<br>\nCertified inverter functions per<br>\nIEEE 1547-2018;<br>\nsupplemented by relay scheme<br>\nwhere dedicated relay is<br>\ninstalled<br>\nDedicated relay scheme with<br>\ntransfer trip where required by<br>\nprotection engineering<br>\nDirectional-power element<br>\n(ANSI 32) — pure-generation<br>\nsites<br>\nNot applicable Set on POI recloser where<br>\ninstalled; used to enforce a<br>\n§3.6 controlled limit where<br>\napplicable<br>\nSet on POI recloser; used to<br>\nenforce a §3.6 controlled limit<br>\nwhere applicable<br>\nMetering Bidirectional revenue meterBidirectional revenue meterBidirectional revenue meter,<br>\nprimary or secondary as<br>\nfeeder configuration requires<br>\nUtility RTU and SCADA<br>\ntelemetry<br>\nNot required Required per TCS-02 (≥500<br>\nkW)<br>\nRequired per TCS-02<br>\nNotes to Table 5-1.<br>\n1.  Category placement reflects the export configuration prevailing at the site. Where a site's configuration warrants<br>\nrequirements differing from the tabular default — including, for example, load/generation-decoupling<br>\narrangements in which generation is isolated by a DER breaker with a dedicated protection relay scheme —<br>\nprotection engineering may specify the applicable requirements through the Engineering Referral Procedure<br>\n(ERP-9 §3, Form ER-11).</p>\n<hr>\n<p>DIS-01 Rev 4 — Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard<br>\nPage 6<br>\nDIS-01 Rev 4 · Effective 03/01/2024<br>\n2.  Table 5-1 addresses protective and control equipment. Monitoring and communications requirements are<br>\nestablished under TCS-02.<br>\n3.  Where §3.6 recognition is in effect at a pure-generation site with a utility POI recloser, the ANSI 32<br>\ndirectional-power element is set to the recognized limit and trips on violation.<br>\n§6 Monitoring and Control<br>\nFacilities at or above the TCS-02 program threshold shall provide real-time monitoring and control integration through<br>\na utility-owned RTU with SCADA telemetry as prescribed by TCS-02. Live telemetry to the Company system<br>\noperations center is a condition of permission to operate. This standard does not restate TCS-02; refer to TCS-02 §5<br>\nfor the telemetry rule and to Alleniom system operations for RTU installation scheduling.<br>\nSite-level control functions required by §3.6 (Power Control System recognition), by Table 5-1 (dedicated protection<br>\nrelay schemes, DER breaker arrangements), and by ERP-9 site determinations, together satisfy the<br>\ncontrol-integration expectations of this section as applied to the specific facility.<br>\n§7 Capacity and Point-of-Interconnection Provisions<br>\nPost-execution changes to executed ISA capacity or POI require withdrawal and reapplication; ISA edits are not<br>\npermitted for capacity or POI changes.<br>\nScope. This section governs changes proposed after an interconnection service agreement has been executed by<br>\nboth parties. Prior to execution, ordinary revisions may be handled in the studies and drafting process without<br>\ninvoking this section.<br>\nPrincipal parameters. Facility capacity and point of interconnection are principal parameters of the interconnection<br>\nservice agreement. Studies, protective-equipment scoping, and queue position each depend on these parameters as<br>\nexecuted.<br>\nRequired path. A customer or developer seeking to change either the executed capacity or the executed point of<br>\ninterconnection after ISA execution shall:<br>\n1.  Submit a written request to withdraw the current application and terminate the executed ISA;<br>\n2.  File a new application at the desired capacity and, where applicable, the desired point of interconnection; and<br>\n3.  Accept the new queue position corresponding to the filing date of the new application.<br>\nThe new application is studied on its own facts. The withdrawal-and-reapplication path applies whether the requested<br>\nchange is an increase or a decrease in capacity and whether or not the requested change is characterized by the<br>\napplicant as involving new or existing equipment.<br>\nOther post-execution changes. Modifications to protective schemes, telemetry endpoints, equipment models within<br>\nthe same nameplate rating, and administrative items that do not alter capacity or POI are handled under the<br>\nchange-order provisions of the executed ISA and are not subject to this section.<br>\nDownstream effects. The withdrawal of an executed ISA under this section releases any related utility equipment<br>\nreservations and any related feeder headroom allocations, which are then available to subsequent applications in<br>\nqueue order. Studies previously performed at the withdrawn capacity are not carried forward; the new application<br>\nreceives a new study appropriate to its filed parameters.<br>\n§8 Records and Compliance<br>\nInterconnection Studies retains the application file, executed ISA, and study record for each in-scope facility for the<br>\nretention period established by Regulatory Affairs. Standards Engineering maintains the current revision of this<br>\nstandard, the Certified Equipment List (CEL-2025-Q2 and subsequent editions), and the Engineering Referral<br>\nProcedure (ERP-9) register of per-site determinations.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>DIS-01 Rev 4 — Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard<br>\nPage 7<br>\nDIS-01 Rev 4 · Effective 03/01/2024<br>\nAny conflict between this standard and the APUC-filed tariff is resolved in favor of the tariff. Any conflict between this<br>\nstandard and another Alleniom operating standard encountered in screening or study is referred to per-site<br>\nengineering determination under ERP-9 §3.<br>\n End of DIS-01 Rev 4.</p>	[]	markdown	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	markdown	en	9	9	{}
42	der-program/standards/dps-07-rev2-distribution-protection-standard	77967b9145364924e0de	DPS-07 Rev2 Distribution Protection Standard	Distribution Protection Standard Rev 2 effective 09/15/2023 with POI recloser requirements	f	t				# DPS-07 Rev2 Distribution Protection Standard\n\nDPS-07 Rev 2  |  Distribution Protection Standard  |  Effective 09/15/2023\nPage 1\nALLENIOM UTILITIES — STANDARDS ENGINEERING\nDISTRIBUTION PROTECTION STANDARD\nDoc no.\nRev\nDate\nOwner\nDPS-07\nRev 2\n09/15/2023\nAlleniom Standards Engineering\nEffective date: 09/15/2023\nSupersedes: DPS-07 Rev 1 (11/04/2019)\nClassification: Engineering Standard — Distribution Protection\nApproved by: Manager, Standards Engineering\n1. PURPOSE\nDPS-07 establishes minimum protection requirements for the interconnection of distributed energy resources\n(DER) to the Alleniom Utilities distribution system. The requirements set forth herein preserve system-side fault\nclearing, prevent unintentional islanding of generation onto de-energized distribution circuits, and ensure that the\nutility maintains a controllable, visible interruption point at every generating facility capable of exporting power to\nthe distribution network.\nThis standard defines the equipment, functional performance, and coordination principles applicable to protective\ndevices installed at the point of interconnection (POI) of an export-capable DER site. It is issued by Standards\nEngineering under authority of the Director of Distribution Engineering and shall be applied to all in-scope\nprojects at initial screening, detailed engineering review, construction, and commissioning.\n2. SCOPE\nThis standard applies to all distribution-connected export-capable DER sites within the Alleniom Utilities service\nterritory, including but not limited to:\n1. Commercial small-scale DER (500 kW – 1 MW nameplate).\n2. Medium-scale DER (1 MW – 5 MW nameplate).\n3. Any DER of any size that is capable, by facility design or operating configuration, of exporting real power to\nthe distribution system through the point of common coupling.\nSub-scope DER handled under the Simplified Small-Systems Process are outside the direct application of this\nstandard; however, where such a facility is nonetheless configured for export, the protection principles of §4 shall\napply as adapted by the Small-Systems review.\nNon-export configurations (facilities operated behind a reverse-power lockout with no export capability under any\noperating condition) are outside the scope of this standard and are addressed under separate protection\nguidance.\n3. REFERENCES\nThe following documents are referenced in the application of this standard:\n IEEE 1547 — Standard for Interconnection and Interoperability of Distributed Energy Resources with\nAssociated Electric Power Systems Interfaces.\n IEEE C37.230 — Guide for Protective Relay Applications to Distribution Lines.\n IEEE C37.60 / IEC 62271-111 — Requirements for Overhead, Pad-Mounted, Dry-Vault, and Submersible\nAutomatic Circuit Reclosers.\n ANSI C57.13 — Requirements for Instrument Transformers.\n\n\n---\n\nDPS-07 Rev 2  |  Distribution Protection Standard  |  Effective 09/15/2023 Page 2\n Alleniom Utilities TCS-02 — Telemetry & Communications Standard.\n Alleniom Utilities ERP-9 — Engineering Referral Procedure.\n4. PROTECTION REQUIREMENTS\n4.1 General\nEvery distribution-connected DER site subject to this standard shall be equipped with protective equipment\nsufficient to accomplish, at minimum, the following:\n(a) Isolate the DER from the distribution system upon loss of the utility source, before the operation of any utility\nreclosing sequence that would attempt to re-energize the affected circuit.\n(b) Isolate the DER from the distribution system upon detection of abnormal voltage or frequency conditions on\nthe utility side of the point of interconnection, within the trip times required by the applicable interconnection\nstandard.\n(c)Present the utility system operator with a visible, remotely operable, and lockable point of interruption\nbetween the DER and the distribution circuit at all times when the DER is connected.\n(d) Coordinate with upstream and downstream distribution protective devices such that a DER-side fault is\ncleared by DER-side devices and a utility-side fault is cleared by utility-side devices, without unnecessary\ntripping of either.\n4.2 Point-of-Interconnection Recloser Requirement\nEvery export-capable in-scope DER site shall be equipped with a utility-owned point-of-interconnection\n(POI) recloser. No exceptions are specified.\nThe POI recloser shall satisfy the following requirements:\n1. Ownership and control. The POI recloser and its associated control cabinet, communications interface,\ncurrent and voltage transformers, and control power supply shall be owned, installed, maintained, and\noperated by Alleniom Utilities. The DER owner shall provide the physical site, the equipment pad, primary\nconductors to the recloser terminals, and any auxiliary services (grounding, station service, communications\nbackhaul) as specified in the site's construction package.\n2. Ratings. The recloser shall be rated for the maximum continuous current, momentary current, short-circuit\ninterrupting current, and system voltage of the feeder or lateral to which it is connected, with margin per\nDistribution Protection Engineering's feeder-specific settings sheet.\n3. Functional elements. At minimum, the recloser control shall implement the following ANSI device functions,\nwith settings determined per site by Distribution Protection Engineering:\n— 50/51 phase and 50N/51N ground overcurrent (instantaneous and time),\n— 27/59 undervoltage and overvoltage,\n— 81U/81O underfrequency and overfrequency,\n— 25 synchronism check for closing into an energized system,\n— 32 directional power element, available for use as an export-limit enforcement or reverse-power\nbackstop as directed by the site's engineering determination,\n— 79 automatic reclosing, enabled or blocked per the coordination scheme in §5.\n4. Operability. The recloser shall be capable of local manual operation, local electrical operation from the\ncontrol cabinet, and remote operation and monitoring via the utility SCADA system per TCS-02. Loss of\ncommunications shall not defeat local protection.\nWhere a referenced external standard is revised, the revision in effect on the date of the site's engineering\ndetermination shall govern, unless Standards Engineering directs otherwise in writing.\n\n---\n\nDPS-07 Rev 2  |  Distribution Protection Standard  |  Effective 09/15/2023\nPage 3\n5. Visible break and lockout. The recloser shall provide, or be paired with, a visible-break disconnect\noperable by utility personnel, with provision for utility padlocking in the open position for the purpose of\nclearance holds.\n6. Location. The POI recloser shall be located on the utility side of the point of interconnection, at a location\naccessible to Alleniom Utilities crews 24 hours per day, 7 days per week, without transiting locked customer\npremises.\n4.3 DER-Side Protection\nDER-side protection (generator step-up transformer protection, inverter interface protection, plant collection\nprotection, and any owner-installed disconnects) shall be provided by the DER owner, coordinated with the POI\nrecloser settings, and reviewed by Alleniom Utilities Distribution Protection Engineering prior to commissioning.\nDER-side protection does not substitute for the POI recloser required under §4.2.\n4.4 Grounding\nThe DER interconnection transformer grounding configuration shall be determined per site to be compatible with\nthe grounding of the connected distribution circuit, in accordance with Distribution Protection Engineering's\ngrounding practice memorandum for the affected feeder class.\n4.5 Anti-Islanding\nThe DER shall be equipped with anti-islanding protection meeting the trip times and non-detection zone\nrequirements of the applicable interconnection standard. Passive schemes based solely on utility-side voltage\nand frequency measurement are permitted only where the site's system impact study demonstrates the passive\nscheme's detection performance for the range of expected islanding conditions on the connected feeder;\notherwise an active or communications-assisted scheme shall be provided.\n5. COORDINATION REQUIREMENTS\n5.1 Coordination with Feeder Reclosing\nThe POI recloser shall be coordinated with the upstream feeder recloser and any mid-line reclosers such that:\n(a) The POI recloser opens on loss of the utility source before the first reclose attempt of any upstream\nrecloser.\n(b) The POI recloser remains open and locked out until the utility source has been restored, verified stable per\nthe site's synchronism-check settings, and the utility system operator has authorized reconnection per the\nsite's operating procedure.\n(c) Coordination time-current characteristics shall be documented in the site's protection coordination study\nprior to energization.\n5.2 Coordination with DER-Side Protection\nDER-side protective devices shall clear DER-side faults without operation of the POI recloser to the extent\nreasonably practicable. Where a DER-side fault is not cleared by DER-side devices within the allowable time, the\nPOI recloser shall trip as backup protection.\n5.3 Direct Transfer Trip\nDirect transfer trip (DTT) shall be applied at any site where the system impact study identifies an unacceptable\nnon-detection zone for anti-islanding, or where the connected feeder configuration requires supervised opening\nof the POI recloser on operation of an upstream device. Where DTT is applied, the associated communications\nchannel shall meet the availability and monitoring requirements of TCS-02.\n5.4 Reclosing Blocking\nAutomatic reclosing on the upstream feeder recloser may be blocked, delayed, or supervised at sites where the\nDER contribution to fault current, the DER anti-islanding characteristic, or the customer load composition on the\n\n\n---\n\nDPS-07 Rev 2  |  Distribution Protection Standard  |  Effective 09/15/2023 Page 4\naffected feeder warrants such a change. The determination shall be made per site by Distribution Protection\n5.5 Settings Governance\nAll protection settings for the POI recloser, and any changes to upstream feeder recloser settings driven by the\nDER interconnection, shall be issued in writing by Distribution Protection Engineering and shall not be altered in\nthe field without a written revision order.\n6. TESTING\n6.1 Factory Acceptance\nThe POI recloser and its control shall be subjected to factory acceptance testing per the manufacturer's standard\ntest protocol and per IEEE C37.60 / IEC 62271-111, with test records delivered to Alleniom Utilities prior to\nshipment.\n6.2 Site Acceptance\nPrior to placing the DER site in service, the following site acceptance activities shall be performed and witnessed\nby Alleniom Utilities:\n1. Insulation resistance and dielectric withstand testing of primary and secondary circuits.\n2. Current transformer and voltage transformer ratio, polarity, and burden verification.\n3. Functional testing of each enabled ANSI device function against its issued setting, including pickup, timing,\nand reset.\n4. End-to-end trip testing from each protection element through the recloser interrupter.\n5. Verification of local and remote (SCADA) control, status, and metering per TCS-02.\n6. Verification of synchronism check and reclose-block logic.\n7. Verification of visible-break disconnect operation and lockout provisions.\n6.3 Commissioning and Cutover\nCutover of the DER site to the distribution system shall not proceed until all §6.2 activities are complete and\naccepted in writing by Alleniom Utilities. The site's initial parallel operation shall be observed by the utility system\noperator with the POI recloser under active SCADA supervision.\n6.4 Periodic Testing\nThe POI recloser shall be included in the utility's periodic recloser maintenance program. DER-side protective\ndevices shall be tested by the DER owner on a schedule not to exceed that of comparable utility-owned\nequipment, with test records made available to Alleniom Utilities on request.\n6.5 Post-Event Testing\nFollowing any operation of the POI recloser attributable to a system disturbance, a DER-side event, or a\nmis-operation, Distribution Protection Engineering shall review the event records and direct such retesting or\nresetting as it determines appropriate before the site is returned to service.\nEnd of DPS-07 Rev 2 — Distribution Protection Standard. Distribution: Alleniom Standards Engineering master file;\nDistribution Protection Engineering; Distribution Operations; DER Program Office; APUC filing (informational).\nEngineering and recorded in the site's interconnection file.	<h1 id="dps-07-rev2-distribution-protection-standard">DPS-07 Rev2 Distribution Protection Standard</h1>\n<p>DPS-07 Rev 2  |  Distribution Protection Standard  |  Effective 09/15/2023<br>\nPage 1<br>\nALLENIOM UTILITIES — STANDARDS ENGINEERING<br>\nDISTRIBUTION PROTECTION STANDARD<br>\nDoc no.<br>\nRev<br>\nDate<br>\nOwner<br>\nDPS-07<br>\nRev 2<br>\n09/15/2023<br>\nAlleniom Standards Engineering<br>\nEffective date: 09/15/2023<br>\nSupersedes: DPS-07 Rev 1 (11/04/2019)<br>\nClassification: Engineering Standard — Distribution Protection<br>\nApproved by: Manager, Standards Engineering<br>\n1. PURPOSE<br>\nDPS-07 establishes minimum protection requirements for the interconnection of distributed energy resources<br>\n(DER) to the Alleniom Utilities distribution system. The requirements set forth herein preserve system-side fault<br>\nclearing, prevent unintentional islanding of generation onto de-energized distribution circuits, and ensure that the<br>\nutility maintains a controllable, visible interruption point at every generating facility capable of exporting power to<br>\nthe distribution network.<br>\nThis standard defines the equipment, functional performance, and coordination principles applicable to protective<br>\ndevices installed at the point of interconnection (POI) of an export-capable DER site. It is issued by Standards<br>\nEngineering under authority of the Director of Distribution Engineering and shall be applied to all in-scope<br>\nprojects at initial screening, detailed engineering review, construction, and commissioning.<br>\n2. SCOPE<br>\nThis standard applies to all distribution-connected export-capable DER sites within the Alleniom Utilities service<br>\nterritory, including but not limited to:<br>\n1. Commercial small-scale DER (500 kW – 1 MW nameplate).<br>\n2. Medium-scale DER (1 MW – 5 MW nameplate).<br>\n3. Any DER of any size that is capable, by facility design or operating configuration, of exporting real power to<br>\nthe distribution system through the point of common coupling.<br>\nSub-scope DER handled under the Simplified Small-Systems Process are outside the direct application of this<br>\nstandard; however, where such a facility is nonetheless configured for export, the protection principles of §4 shall<br>\napply as adapted by the Small-Systems review.<br>\nNon-export configurations (facilities operated behind a reverse-power lockout with no export capability under any<br>\noperating condition) are outside the scope of this standard and are addressed under separate protection<br>\nguidance.<br>\n3. REFERENCES<br>\nThe following documents are referenced in the application of this standard:<br>\n IEEE 1547 — Standard for Interconnection and Interoperability of Distributed Energy Resources with<br>\nAssociated Electric Power Systems Interfaces.<br>\n IEEE C37.230 — Guide for Protective Relay Applications to Distribution Lines.<br>\n IEEE C37.60 / IEC 62271-111 — Requirements for Overhead, Pad-Mounted, Dry-Vault, and Submersible<br>\nAutomatic Circuit Reclosers.<br>\n ANSI C57.13 — Requirements for Instrument Transformers.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>DPS-07 Rev 2  |  Distribution Protection Standard  |  Effective 09/15/2023 Page 2<br>\n Alleniom Utilities TCS-02 — Telemetry &amp; Communications Standard.<br>\n Alleniom Utilities ERP-9 — Engineering Referral Procedure.<br>\n4. PROTECTION REQUIREMENTS<br>\n4.1 General<br>\nEvery distribution-connected DER site subject to this standard shall be equipped with protective equipment<br>\nsufficient to accomplish, at minimum, the following:<br>\n(a) Isolate the DER from the distribution system upon loss of the utility source, before the operation of any utility<br>\nreclosing sequence that would attempt to re-energize the affected circuit.<br>\n(b) Isolate the DER from the distribution system upon detection of abnormal voltage or frequency conditions on<br>\nthe utility side of the point of interconnection, within the trip times required by the applicable interconnection<br>\nstandard.<br>\n(c)Present the utility system operator with a visible, remotely operable, and lockable point of interruption<br>\nbetween the DER and the distribution circuit at all times when the DER is connected.<br>\n(d) Coordinate with upstream and downstream distribution protective devices such that a DER-side fault is<br>\ncleared by DER-side devices and a utility-side fault is cleared by utility-side devices, without unnecessary<br>\ntripping of either.<br>\n4.2 Point-of-Interconnection Recloser Requirement<br>\nEvery export-capable in-scope DER site shall be equipped with a utility-owned point-of-interconnection<br>\n(POI) recloser. No exceptions are specified.<br>\nThe POI recloser shall satisfy the following requirements:<br>\n1. Ownership and control. The POI recloser and its associated control cabinet, communications interface,<br>\ncurrent and voltage transformers, and control power supply shall be owned, installed, maintained, and<br>\noperated by Alleniom Utilities. The DER owner shall provide the physical site, the equipment pad, primary<br>\nconductors to the recloser terminals, and any auxiliary services (grounding, station service, communications<br>\nbackhaul) as specified in the site's construction package.<br>\n2. Ratings. The recloser shall be rated for the maximum continuous current, momentary current, short-circuit<br>\ninterrupting current, and system voltage of the feeder or lateral to which it is connected, with margin per<br>\nDistribution Protection Engineering's feeder-specific settings sheet.<br>\n3. Functional elements. At minimum, the recloser control shall implement the following ANSI device functions,<br>\nwith settings determined per site by Distribution Protection Engineering:<br>\n— 50/51 phase and 50N/51N ground overcurrent (instantaneous and time),<br>\n— 27/59 undervoltage and overvoltage,<br>\n— 81U/81O underfrequency and overfrequency,<br>\n— 25 synchronism check for closing into an energized system,<br>\n— 32 directional power element, available for use as an export-limit enforcement or reverse-power<br>\nbackstop as directed by the site's engineering determination,<br>\n— 79 automatic reclosing, enabled or blocked per the coordination scheme in §5.<br>\n4. Operability. The recloser shall be capable of local manual operation, local electrical operation from the<br>\ncontrol cabinet, and remote operation and monitoring via the utility SCADA system per TCS-02. Loss of<br>\ncommunications shall not defeat local protection.<br>\nWhere a referenced external standard is revised, the revision in effect on the date of the site's engineering<br>\ndetermination shall govern, unless Standards Engineering directs otherwise in writing.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>DPS-07 Rev 2  |  Distribution Protection Standard  |  Effective 09/15/2023<br>\nPage 3<br>\n5. Visible break and lockout. The recloser shall provide, or be paired with, a visible-break disconnect<br>\noperable by utility personnel, with provision for utility padlocking in the open position for the purpose of<br>\nclearance holds.<br>\n6. Location. The POI recloser shall be located on the utility side of the point of interconnection, at a location<br>\naccessible to Alleniom Utilities crews 24 hours per day, 7 days per week, without transiting locked customer<br>\npremises.<br>\n4.3 DER-Side Protection<br>\nDER-side protection (generator step-up transformer protection, inverter interface protection, plant collection<br>\nprotection, and any owner-installed disconnects) shall be provided by the DER owner, coordinated with the POI<br>\nrecloser settings, and reviewed by Alleniom Utilities Distribution Protection Engineering prior to commissioning.<br>\nDER-side protection does not substitute for the POI recloser required under §4.2.<br>\n4.4 Grounding<br>\nThe DER interconnection transformer grounding configuration shall be determined per site to be compatible with<br>\nthe grounding of the connected distribution circuit, in accordance with Distribution Protection Engineering's<br>\ngrounding practice memorandum for the affected feeder class.<br>\n4.5 Anti-Islanding<br>\nThe DER shall be equipped with anti-islanding protection meeting the trip times and non-detection zone<br>\nrequirements of the applicable interconnection standard. Passive schemes based solely on utility-side voltage<br>\nand frequency measurement are permitted only where the site's system impact study demonstrates the passive<br>\nscheme's detection performance for the range of expected islanding conditions on the connected feeder;<br>\notherwise an active or communications-assisted scheme shall be provided.<br>\n5. COORDINATION REQUIREMENTS<br>\n5.1 Coordination with Feeder Reclosing<br>\nThe POI recloser shall be coordinated with the upstream feeder recloser and any mid-line reclosers such that:<br>\n(a) The POI recloser opens on loss of the utility source before the first reclose attempt of any upstream<br>\nrecloser.<br>\n(b) The POI recloser remains open and locked out until the utility source has been restored, verified stable per<br>\nthe site's synchronism-check settings, and the utility system operator has authorized reconnection per the<br>\nsite's operating procedure.<br>\n(c) Coordination time-current characteristics shall be documented in the site's protection coordination study<br>\nprior to energization.<br>\n5.2 Coordination with DER-Side Protection<br>\nDER-side protective devices shall clear DER-side faults without operation of the POI recloser to the extent<br>\nreasonably practicable. Where a DER-side fault is not cleared by DER-side devices within the allowable time, the<br>\nPOI recloser shall trip as backup protection.<br>\n5.3 Direct Transfer Trip<br>\nDirect transfer trip (DTT) shall be applied at any site where the system impact study identifies an unacceptable<br>\nnon-detection zone for anti-islanding, or where the connected feeder configuration requires supervised opening<br>\nof the POI recloser on operation of an upstream device. Where DTT is applied, the associated communications<br>\nchannel shall meet the availability and monitoring requirements of TCS-02.<br>\n5.4 Reclosing Blocking<br>\nAutomatic reclosing on the upstream feeder recloser may be blocked, delayed, or supervised at sites where the<br>\nDER contribution to fault current, the DER anti-islanding characteristic, or the customer load composition on the</p>\n<hr>\n<p>DPS-07 Rev 2  |  Distribution Protection Standard  |  Effective 09/15/2023 Page 4<br>\naffected feeder warrants such a change. The determination shall be made per site by Distribution Protection<br>\n5.5 Settings Governance<br>\nAll protection settings for the POI recloser, and any changes to upstream feeder recloser settings driven by the<br>\nDER interconnection, shall be issued in writing by Distribution Protection Engineering and shall not be altered in<br>\nthe field without a written revision order.<br>\n6. TESTING<br>\n6.1 Factory Acceptance<br>\nThe POI recloser and its control shall be subjected to factory acceptance testing per the manufacturer's standard<br>\ntest protocol and per IEEE C37.60 / IEC 62271-111, with test records delivered to Alleniom Utilities prior to<br>\nshipment.<br>\n6.2 Site Acceptance<br>\nPrior to placing the DER site in service, the following site acceptance activities shall be performed and witnessed<br>\nby Alleniom Utilities:<br>\n1. Insulation resistance and dielectric withstand testing of primary and secondary circuits.<br>\n2. Current transformer and voltage transformer ratio, polarity, and burden verification.<br>\n3. Functional testing of each enabled ANSI device function against its issued setting, including pickup, timing,<br>\nand reset.<br>\n4. End-to-end trip testing from each protection element through the recloser interrupter.<br>\n5. Verification of local and remote (SCADA) control, status, and metering per TCS-02.<br>\n6. Verification of synchronism check and reclose-block logic.<br>\n7. Verification of visible-break disconnect operation and lockout provisions.<br>\n6.3 Commissioning and Cutover<br>\nCutover of the DER site to the distribution system shall not proceed until all §6.2 activities are complete and<br>\naccepted in writing by Alleniom Utilities. The site's initial parallel operation shall be observed by the utility system<br>\noperator with the POI recloser under active SCADA supervision.<br>\n6.4 Periodic Testing<br>\nThe POI recloser shall be included in the utility's periodic recloser maintenance program. DER-side protective<br>\ndevices shall be tested by the DER owner on a schedule not to exceed that of comparable utility-owned<br>\nequipment, with test records made available to Alleniom Utilities on request.<br>\n6.5 Post-Event Testing<br>\nFollowing any operation of the POI recloser attributable to a system disturbance, a DER-side event, or a<br>\nmis-operation, Distribution Protection Engineering shall review the event records and direct such retesting or<br>\nresetting as it determines appropriate before the site is returned to service.<br>\nEnd of DPS-07 Rev 2 — Distribution Protection Standard. Distribution: Alleniom Standards Engineering master file;<br>\nDistribution Protection Engineering; Distribution Operations; DER Program Office; APUC filing (informational).<br>\nEngineering and recorded in the site's interconnection file.</p>	[]	markdown	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	markdown	en	9	9	{}
43	der-program/standards/erp-9-engineering-referral-procedure	8aea13ea57d86b380c82	ERP-9 Engineering Referral Procedure	Engineering Referral Procedure with Form ER-11 and Bulletin EB-2025-03	f	t				# ERP-9 Engineering Referral Procedure\n\nALLENIOM UTILITIES\nENGINEERING REFERRAL PROCEDURE\nStandards Engineering · DER Program\nDoc no.\nRev\nDate\nOwner\nERP-9\nRev 2\n03/24/2025\nAlleniom Standards Engineering\nDocument control\nDocument number\nERP-9\nRevision\nRev 2\nEffective date\n03/24/2025\nSupersedes\nERP-9 Rev 1 (07/10/2022)\nOwner\nAlleniom Standards Engineering\nApproved by\nManager, Standards Engineering\nDistribution\nAlleniom DER Program Office; Interconnection Studies; Distribution Protection\nEngineering; SCADA/EMS Engineering; DER Planning Group; Regulatory\nAffairs\nAttachments\nBulletin EB-2025-03 (03/24/2025); Form ER-11 template; Precedent Register\nRevision history\nRev\nDate\nSummary\nRev 1\n07/10/2022\nInitial issue. Established referral form and per-site engineering determination path.\nRev 2\n03/24/2025\nReissued in conjunction with Bulletin EB-2025-03. §3 rewritten to state routing rule\nfor standards conflicts; Form ER-11 fields expanded to capture site type and feeder;\nprecedent register appended.\n§1 PURPOSE\nERP-9 establishes the procedure by which an interconnection application encountering a standards\nconflict, a silence between standards, or site conditions falling outside the assumptions of a tabulated\nrequirement is referred to a per-site protection/SCADA engineering determination. The procedure exists so\nthat documentary conflicts between Alleniom standards do not accumulate as screening holds against\nactive applications, and so that each affected site is resolved on the merits of its own protection scheme,\non a schedule stated in §4.\nThis procedure is issued by Standards Engineering and applies uniformly across the Alleniom DER\nProgram. It is read together with DIS-01 (DER Interconnection Standard), DPS-07 (Distribution Protection\nStandard), TCS-02 (Telemetry & Communications Standard), and the APUC-filed interconnection tariff.\n\n\n---\n\n§2 SCOPE\nERP-9 applies to any in-scope DER application administered by the Alleniom DER Program at which one\nor more of the following conditions is encountered during initial screening, supplemental review, or system\nimpact study:\n(1)  Two applicable Alleniom standards state requirements that cannot both be applied on their face to\nthe affected site.\n(2)  An applicable standard is silent as to a site condition materially present in the application (a topic\non which the standard does not speak, as distinct from a topic on which it speaks and permits an\nexception).\n(3)  The reviewing engineer documents a reasoned basis to depart from a tabulated requirement in\nlight of the site's specific protection arrangement.\nSub-scope facilities handled under the Simplified Small-Systems Process are outside the direct application\nof ERP-9; the Small-Systems reviewer may nonetheless invoke Form ER-11 where an export configuration\nwarrants site-specific protection engineering input.\nNothing in this procedure permits a screening hold pending revision of a standard where ERP-9 provides a\ndetermination path. See §3 and Bulletin EB-2025-03 (attached).\n§3 REFERRAL ROUTING\nAny standards conflict encountered at screening or design shall be routed via Form ER-11 to a per-site\nprotection/SCADA engineering determination. No “winning” document is selected across the program;\neach site is resolved on its own protection scheme.\nThe reviewing engineer in Interconnection Studies initiates the referral by completing Form ER-11\n(template attached), attaching the relevant excerpts of the standards at issue, the application's connection\ndiagram and equipment schedule, and any feeder-specific information required to characterize the site's\nprotection arrangement. Interconnection Studies transmits the completed Form ER-11 to Distribution\nProtection Engineering; where SCADA/EMS input is required, the Manager, Distribution Protection\nEngineering routes a copy to the SCADA/EMS Engineering group for concurrent review.\nReferral does not itself alter the application's status in the DER Program queue. The affected application\nremains in queue during the referral period, and other screening activities that are not dependent on the\nreferred question shall proceed in parallel.\nThe per-site engineering determination is issued in writing on Form ER-11 by Distribution Protection\nEngineering, is signed by the responsible engineer and countersigned by the Manager, Distribution\nProtection Engineering, and is filed with the application record. The determination is binding for the\nreferred site and does not create a general rule across the program; a subsequent site presenting a similar\nquestion is referred and determined on its own facts.\nWhere the site's protection arrangement, as determined under this section, differs from the tabular default\nof DIS-01 §5 Table 5-1, the engineering determination on Form ER-11 supplements the site's\nprotective-equipment scoping and is provided to the DER Program Office and to the contracts desk for\nincorporation into the interconnection service agreement.\nWhere the referral resolves a recurring pattern that Standards Engineering considers appropriate to reflect\nin a future revision of a standard, the determinations are logged and reviewed as provided in §5.\n\n\n---\n\n§4 TIMELINES\nTimelines below run from the date on which Interconnection Studies transmits the completed Form ER-11\nto Distribution Protection Engineering. Business days exclude weekends and federal holidays.\nReferral class\nDescription\nDetermination due\nClass A — Standards conflict,\nno site protection novelty\nTwo Alleniom standards state conflicting requirements at\na site whose protection arrangement is otherwise\nordinary.\n10 business days\nClass B — Standards conflict\nwith site protection novelty\nConflict at a site whose arrangement (e.g.,\nload/generation decoupling, non-standard grounding,\nunusual feeder configuration) requires additional\nprotection engineering review.\n20 business days\nClass C — Standards silence,\nsite condition outside tabulated\nassumptions\nApplicable standard is silent as to a site condition\nmaterially present. Determination may require\ncoordination study.\n30 business days\nClass D — SCADA/EMS input\nrequired in addition to\nprotection engineering\nAny of the above where the site's telemetry or control\narrangement requires concurrent SCADA/EMS\nEngineering review.\n30 business days\nClassification is made by the Manager, Distribution Protection Engineering, on receipt of Form ER-11.\nWhere the reviewer requests reclassification, the request is decided in writing within two business days.\nExtensions to the timelines above are permitted only in writing, are issued by the Manager, Distribution\nProtection Engineering, and are copied to the DER Program Manager and Regulatory Affairs. Applications\nshall not be held in queue pending revision of a standard where this procedure provides a determination\npath; extensions do not authorize a screening hold outside this procedure.\n§5 DOCUMENTATION\nThe completed Form ER-11, the engineering determination, and any supporting studies are filed with the\napplication record retained by Interconnection Studies for the retention period established by Regulatory\nAffairs. A copy of the completed Form ER-11 is filed in the Standards Engineering ERP-9 register of\nper-site determinations.\nThe ERP-9 register records, at minimum: the referral number, the receipt date, the Project ID, the site type\nand feeder, the standards documents at issue, the assigned engineer, the determination date, and a short\nsummary of the determination. The register is reviewed by Standards Engineering quarterly for recurring\npatterns; where a pattern warrants a standards revision proposal, Standards Engineering initiates that\nrevision separately from, and in parallel with, ongoing per-site determinations under this procedure. A\npending revision proposal does not stay determinations issued under this procedure and does not\nauthorize a screening hold on affected applications.\nDeterminations issued under ERP-9 are cited in the interconnection service agreement (ISA) drafted for\nthe affected site by reference to the ER-11 referral number and determination date. Modifications to the\nsite's protection arrangement subsequent to ISA execution are handled under the change-order provisions\nof the executed ISA, subject to DIS-01 §7 where capacity or POI is affected.\n\n\n---\n\nATTACHMENT 1 — BULLETIN EB-2025-03\nBulletin EB-2025-03 (03/24/2025): Screening holds pending standards reconciliation are not permitted.\nApplications encountering standards conflict shall be routed via Form ER-11 immediately per ERP-9 §3.\nIssued by: Manager, Standards Engineering · Distribution: Alleniom DER Program Office; Interconnection\nStudies; Distribution Protection Engineering; SCADA/EMS Engineering; Regulatory Affairs.\nATTACHMENT 2 — FORM ER-11 TEMPLATE\nForm ER-11 — Engineering Referral for Per-Site Determination\nAlleniom Utilities · Standards Engineering · ERP-9\nReferral identification\nReferral number\nER-11-YYYY-NNN (assigned by Distribution Protection Engineering on\nreceipt)\nDate submitted\n______________\nSubmitting engineer (Interconnection\nStudies)\n______________\nReferral class (A / B / C / D per ERP-9\n§4)\n______________\nProject and site\nProject ID\n______________\nApplicant / developer\n______________\nSite name and address\n______________\nSite type (■ pure-export ■\nload-and-generation)\n______________\nFeeder\n______________\nRequested capacity (kW / MW, aggregate\nnameplate at PCC)\n______________\nRecognized §3.6 controlled capacity, if any\n______________\nStandards question presented\nStandards documents at issue (cite section)\n______________\nNature of the conflict or silence\n______________\nReviewer's summary of the site's protection\narrangement\n______________\nAttachments (application, one-line diagram,\nequipment schedule, standards excerpts)\n______________\nEngineering determination\n(completed by Distribution Protection Engineering; SCADA/EMS Engineering concurrent review as applicable)\n\n\n---\n\nAssigned engineer ______________\nDate determination issued ______________\nDetermination (specify protective and control\nequipment applicable to the site)\n______________\n______________\n______________\nSignatures\nRole Name Signature Date\nReviewing engineer, Interconnection\nStudies\nAssigned engineer, Distribution Protection\nEngineering\nManager, Distribution Protection\nEngineering (countersign)\nSCADA/EMS Engineering (where\napplicable)\nFile one copy with the application record (Interconnection Studies); file one copy in the ERP-9 register (Standards\nEngineering).\nATTACHMENT 3 — PRECEDENT REGISTER (resolved 04/2025–06/2025)\nThe following per-site determinations were issued and closed during the second quarter of 2025 under\nERP-9 §3. The register is provided for informational reference only; each entry recites the determination\nmade for the site listed and does not establish a general rule across the program. Subsequent applications\npresenting similar questions are referred and determined on their own facts.\nReferral no. Date\nclosed\nSite type Feeder class Standards\ndocuments at\nissue\nEngineering determination\nER-11-2025-\n004\n04/22/202\n5\npure-export Overhead 12.47\nkV, rural\nthree-phase\nDIS-01 §5 Table\n5-1 (Category 2)\nvs. DPS-07 §4.2\nPOI recloser specified. Site is\na pure-generation\n(pure-export) arrangement\nwith no customer load behind\nthe PCC; the protection\narrangement affords no\nalternative means of\naccomplishing the isolation,\nvisible interruption, and\ncoordination objectives of\nDPS-07 §4.1. Utility-owned\nPOI recloser installed per\nDPS-07 §4.2, with ANSI 32\ndirectional-power element\navailable for §3.6 export-limit\nenforcement should\nrecognition be sought.\nBasis (cite standards and IEEE\nreferences)\nDownstream actions (protective-equipment\nscoping, ISA reference)\n\n---\n\nReferral no.\nDate\nclosed\nSite type\nFeeder class\nStandards\ndocuments at\nissue\nEngineering determination\nER-11-2025-\n009\n05/29/202\n5\npure-export\nOverhead 12.47\nkV, suburban\nthree-phase with\nmid-line recloser\nDIS-01 §5 Table\n5-1 (Category 2)\nvs. DPS-07 §4.2\nPOI recloser specified. Site is\na pure-generation\n(pure-export) arrangement\nserved front-of-the-meter with\nno customer load behind the\nPCC; coordination with the\nupstream mid-line recloser is\ndocumented per DPS-07\n§5.1. Utility-owned POI\nrecloser installed per DPS-07\n§4.2.\nER-11-2025-\n014\n06/17/202\n5\nload/generatio\nn-decoupling\nUnderground\n12.47 kV,\nindustrial\npremises\nDIS-01 §5 Table\n5-1 (Category 2),\nDPS-07 §4.2,\nDPS-07 §4.3\nDER breaker + protection\nrelay scheme specified; no\nPOI recloser. Site is a\nload-and-generation\narrangement in which\ngeneration is isolated from\nthe utility-facing service by a\nDER breaker with a dedicated\nprotection relay scheme\nmeeting the isolation,\nanti-islanding, and\ncoordination objectives of\nDPS-07 §4.1, §4.3, and §4.5.\nThe arrangement decouples\ngeneration from utility-side\nfault clearing without a\nutility-owned POI recloser at\nthe point of interconnection;\nsite retains customer-owned\nlockable visible-break\ndisconnect per DIS-01 Table\n5-1 and provides utility\nSCADA visibility per TCS-02.\nEach of the above determinations is filed with the application record for the affected site and is binding for\nthat site only. The pattern reflected in these three closures does not select a controlling document across\nthe program: DIS-01 §5 Table 5-1 and DPS-07 §4.2 each remain in force as issued, and each new site\npresenting a comparable question is referred under §3 and determined on its own protection scheme.\n End of ERP-9 Rev 2 — Engineering Referral Procedure.	<h1 id="erp-9-engineering-referral-procedure">ERP-9 Engineering Referral Procedure</h1>\n<p>ALLENIOM UTILITIES<br>\nENGINEERING REFERRAL PROCEDURE<br>\nStandards Engineering · DER Program<br>\nDoc no.<br>\nRev<br>\nDate<br>\nOwner<br>\nERP-9<br>\nRev 2<br>\n03/24/2025<br>\nAlleniom Standards Engineering<br>\nDocument control<br>\nDocument number<br>\nERP-9<br>\nRevision<br>\nRev 2<br>\nEffective date<br>\n03/24/2025<br>\nSupersedes<br>\nERP-9 Rev 1 (07/10/2022)<br>\nOwner<br>\nAlleniom Standards Engineering<br>\nApproved by<br>\nManager, Standards Engineering<br>\nDistribution<br>\nAlleniom DER Program Office; Interconnection Studies; Distribution Protection<br>\nEngineering; SCADA/EMS Engineering; DER Planning Group; Regulatory<br>\nAffairs<br>\nAttachments<br>\nBulletin EB-2025-03 (03/24/2025); Form ER-11 template; Precedent Register<br>\nRevision history<br>\nRev<br>\nDate<br>\nSummary<br>\nRev 1<br>\n07/10/2022<br>\nInitial issue. Established referral form and per-site engineering determination path.<br>\nRev 2<br>\n03/24/2025<br>\nReissued in conjunction with Bulletin EB-2025-03. §3 rewritten to state routing rule<br>\nfor standards conflicts; Form ER-11 fields expanded to capture site type and feeder;<br>\nprecedent register appended.<br>\n§1 PURPOSE<br>\nERP-9 establishes the procedure by which an interconnection application encountering a standards<br>\nconflict, a silence between standards, or site conditions falling outside the assumptions of a tabulated<br>\nrequirement is referred to a per-site protection/SCADA engineering determination. The procedure exists so<br>\nthat documentary conflicts between Alleniom standards do not accumulate as screening holds against<br>\nactive applications, and so that each affected site is resolved on the merits of its own protection scheme,<br>\non a schedule stated in §4.<br>\nThis procedure is issued by Standards Engineering and applies uniformly across the Alleniom DER<br>\nProgram. It is read together with DIS-01 (DER Interconnection Standard), DPS-07 (Distribution Protection<br>\nStandard), TCS-02 (Telemetry &amp; Communications Standard), and the APUC-filed interconnection tariff.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>§2 SCOPE<br>\nERP-9 applies to any in-scope DER application administered by the Alleniom DER Program at which one<br>\nor more of the following conditions is encountered during initial screening, supplemental review, or system<br>\nimpact study:<br>\n(1)  Two applicable Alleniom standards state requirements that cannot both be applied on their face to<br>\nthe affected site.<br>\n(2)  An applicable standard is silent as to a site condition materially present in the application (a topic<br>\non which the standard does not speak, as distinct from a topic on which it speaks and permits an<br>\nexception).<br>\n(3)  The reviewing engineer documents a reasoned basis to depart from a tabulated requirement in<br>\nlight of the site's specific protection arrangement.<br>\nSub-scope facilities handled under the Simplified Small-Systems Process are outside the direct application<br>\nof ERP-9; the Small-Systems reviewer may nonetheless invoke Form ER-11 where an export configuration<br>\nwarrants site-specific protection engineering input.<br>\nNothing in this procedure permits a screening hold pending revision of a standard where ERP-9 provides a<br>\ndetermination path. See §3 and Bulletin EB-2025-03 (attached).<br>\n§3 REFERRAL ROUTING<br>\nAny standards conflict encountered at screening or design shall be routed via Form ER-11 to a per-site<br>\nprotection/SCADA engineering determination. No “winning” document is selected across the program;<br>\neach site is resolved on its own protection scheme.<br>\nThe reviewing engineer in Interconnection Studies initiates the referral by completing Form ER-11<br>\n(template attached), attaching the relevant excerpts of the standards at issue, the application's connection<br>\ndiagram and equipment schedule, and any feeder-specific information required to characterize the site's<br>\nprotection arrangement. Interconnection Studies transmits the completed Form ER-11 to Distribution<br>\nProtection Engineering; where SCADA/EMS input is required, the Manager, Distribution Protection<br>\nEngineering routes a copy to the SCADA/EMS Engineering group for concurrent review.<br>\nReferral does not itself alter the application's status in the DER Program queue. The affected application<br>\nremains in queue during the referral period, and other screening activities that are not dependent on the<br>\nreferred question shall proceed in parallel.<br>\nThe per-site engineering determination is issued in writing on Form ER-11 by Distribution Protection<br>\nEngineering, is signed by the responsible engineer and countersigned by the Manager, Distribution<br>\nProtection Engineering, and is filed with the application record. The determination is binding for the<br>\nreferred site and does not create a general rule across the program; a subsequent site presenting a similar<br>\nquestion is referred and determined on its own facts.<br>\nWhere the site's protection arrangement, as determined under this section, differs from the tabular default<br>\nof DIS-01 §5 Table 5-1, the engineering determination on Form ER-11 supplements the site's<br>\nprotective-equipment scoping and is provided to the DER Program Office and to the contracts desk for<br>\nincorporation into the interconnection service agreement.<br>\nWhere the referral resolves a recurring pattern that Standards Engineering considers appropriate to reflect<br>\nin a future revision of a standard, the determinations are logged and reviewed as provided in §5.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>§4 TIMELINES<br>\nTimelines below run from the date on which Interconnection Studies transmits the completed Form ER-11<br>\nto Distribution Protection Engineering. Business days exclude weekends and federal holidays.<br>\nReferral class<br>\nDescription<br>\nDetermination due<br>\nClass A — Standards conflict,<br>\nno site protection novelty<br>\nTwo Alleniom standards state conflicting requirements at<br>\na site whose protection arrangement is otherwise<br>\nordinary.<br>\n10 business days<br>\nClass B — Standards conflict<br>\nwith site protection novelty<br>\nConflict at a site whose arrangement (e.g.,<br>\nload/generation decoupling, non-standard grounding,<br>\nunusual feeder configuration) requires additional<br>\nprotection engineering review.<br>\n20 business days<br>\nClass C — Standards silence,<br>\nsite condition outside tabulated<br>\nassumptions<br>\nApplicable standard is silent as to a site condition<br>\nmaterially present. Determination may require<br>\ncoordination study.<br>\n30 business days<br>\nClass D — SCADA/EMS input<br>\nrequired in addition to<br>\nprotection engineering<br>\nAny of the above where the site's telemetry or control<br>\narrangement requires concurrent SCADA/EMS<br>\nEngineering review.<br>\n30 business days<br>\nClassification is made by the Manager, Distribution Protection Engineering, on receipt of Form ER-11.<br>\nWhere the reviewer requests reclassification, the request is decided in writing within two business days.<br>\nExtensions to the timelines above are permitted only in writing, are issued by the Manager, Distribution<br>\nProtection Engineering, and are copied to the DER Program Manager and Regulatory Affairs. Applications<br>\nshall not be held in queue pending revision of a standard where this procedure provides a determination<br>\npath; extensions do not authorize a screening hold outside this procedure.<br>\n§5 DOCUMENTATION<br>\nThe completed Form ER-11, the engineering determination, and any supporting studies are filed with the<br>\napplication record retained by Interconnection Studies for the retention period established by Regulatory<br>\nAffairs. A copy of the completed Form ER-11 is filed in the Standards Engineering ERP-9 register of<br>\nper-site determinations.<br>\nThe ERP-9 register records, at minimum: the referral number, the receipt date, the Project ID, the site type<br>\nand feeder, the standards documents at issue, the assigned engineer, the determination date, and a short<br>\nsummary of the determination. The register is reviewed by Standards Engineering quarterly for recurring<br>\npatterns; where a pattern warrants a standards revision proposal, Standards Engineering initiates that<br>\nrevision separately from, and in parallel with, ongoing per-site determinations under this procedure. A<br>\npending revision proposal does not stay determinations issued under this procedure and does not<br>\nauthorize a screening hold on affected applications.<br>\nDeterminations issued under ERP-9 are cited in the interconnection service agreement (ISA) drafted for<br>\nthe affected site by reference to the ER-11 referral number and determination date. Modifications to the<br>\nsite's protection arrangement subsequent to ISA execution are handled under the change-order provisions<br>\nof the executed ISA, subject to DIS-01 §7 where capacity or POI is affected.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>ATTACHMENT 1 — BULLETIN EB-2025-03<br>\nBulletin EB-2025-03 (03/24/2025): Screening holds pending standards reconciliation are not permitted.<br>\nApplications encountering standards conflict shall be routed via Form ER-11 immediately per ERP-9 §3.<br>\nIssued by: Manager, Standards Engineering · Distribution: Alleniom DER Program Office; Interconnection<br>\nStudies; Distribution Protection Engineering; SCADA/EMS Engineering; Regulatory Affairs.<br>\nATTACHMENT 2 — FORM ER-11 TEMPLATE<br>\nForm ER-11 — Engineering Referral for Per-Site Determination<br>\nAlleniom Utilities · Standards Engineering · ERP-9<br>\nReferral identification<br>\nReferral number<br>\nER-11-YYYY-NNN (assigned by Distribution Protection Engineering on<br>\nreceipt)<br>\nDate submitted</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Submitting engineer (Interconnection<br>\nStudies)</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Referral class (A / B / C / D per ERP-9<br>\n§4)</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Project and site<br>\nProject ID</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Applicant / developer</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Site name and address</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Site type (■ pure-export ■<br>\nload-and-generation)</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Feeder</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Requested capacity (kW / MW, aggregate<br>\nnameplate at PCC)</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Recognized §3.6 controlled capacity, if any</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Standards question presented<br>\nStandards documents at issue (cite section)</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Nature of the conflict or silence</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Reviewer's summary of the site's protection<br>\narrangement</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Attachments (application, one-line diagram,<br>\nequipment schedule, standards excerpts)</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Engineering determination<br>\n(completed by Distribution Protection Engineering; SCADA/EMS Engineering concurrent review as applicable)</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Assigned engineer <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><br>\nDate determination issued <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><br>\nDetermination (specify protective and control<br>\nequipment applicable to the site)</p>\n<hr>\n<hr>\n<hr>\n<p>Signatures<br>\nRole Name Signature Date<br>\nReviewing engineer, Interconnection<br>\nStudies<br>\nAssigned engineer, Distribution Protection<br>\nEngineering<br>\nManager, Distribution Protection<br>\nEngineering (countersign)<br>\nSCADA/EMS Engineering (where<br>\napplicable)<br>\nFile one copy with the application record (Interconnection Studies); file one copy in the ERP-9 register (Standards<br>\nEngineering).<br>\nATTACHMENT 3 — PRECEDENT REGISTER (resolved 04/2025–06/2025)<br>\nThe following per-site determinations were issued and closed during the second quarter of 2025 under<br>\nERP-9 §3. The register is provided for informational reference only; each entry recites the determination<br>\nmade for the site listed and does not establish a general rule across the program. Subsequent applications<br>\npresenting similar questions are referred and determined on their own facts.<br>\nReferral no. Date<br>\nclosed<br>\nSite type Feeder class Standards<br>\ndocuments at<br>\nissue<br>\nEngineering determination<br>\nER-11-2025-<br>\n004<br>\n04/22/202<br>\n5<br>\npure-export Overhead 12.47<br>\nkV, rural<br>\nthree-phase<br>\nDIS-01 §5 Table<br>\n5-1 (Category 2)<br>\nvs. DPS-07 §4.2<br>\nPOI recloser specified. Site is<br>\na pure-generation<br>\n(pure-export) arrangement<br>\nwith no customer load behind<br>\nthe PCC; the protection<br>\narrangement affords no<br>\nalternative means of<br>\naccomplishing the isolation,<br>\nvisible interruption, and<br>\ncoordination objectives of<br>\nDPS-07 §4.1. Utility-owned<br>\nPOI recloser installed per<br>\nDPS-07 §4.2, with ANSI 32<br>\ndirectional-power element<br>\navailable for §3.6 export-limit<br>\nenforcement should<br>\nrecognition be sought.<br>\nBasis (cite standards and IEEE<br>\nreferences)<br>\nDownstream actions (protective-equipment<br>\nscoping, ISA reference)</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Referral no.<br>\nDate<br>\nclosed<br>\nSite type<br>\nFeeder class<br>\nStandards<br>\ndocuments at<br>\nissue<br>\nEngineering determination<br>\nER-11-2025-<br>\n009<br>\n05/29/202<br>\n5<br>\npure-export<br>\nOverhead 12.47<br>\nkV, suburban<br>\nthree-phase with<br>\nmid-line recloser<br>\nDIS-01 §5 Table<br>\n5-1 (Category 2)<br>\nvs. DPS-07 §4.2<br>\nPOI recloser specified. Site is<br>\na pure-generation<br>\n(pure-export) arrangement<br>\nserved front-of-the-meter with<br>\nno customer load behind the<br>\nPCC; coordination with the<br>\nupstream mid-line recloser is<br>\ndocumented per DPS-07<br>\n§5.1. Utility-owned POI<br>\nrecloser installed per DPS-07<br>\n§4.2.<br>\nER-11-2025-<br>\n014<br>\n06/17/202<br>\n5<br>\nload/generatio<br>\nn-decoupling<br>\nUnderground<br>\n12.47 kV,<br>\nindustrial<br>\npremises<br>\nDIS-01 §5 Table<br>\n5-1 (Category 2),<br>\nDPS-07 §4.2,<br>\nDPS-07 §4.3<br>\nDER breaker + protection<br>\nrelay scheme specified; no<br>\nPOI recloser. Site is a<br>\nload-and-generation<br>\narrangement in which<br>\ngeneration is isolated from<br>\nthe utility-facing service by a<br>\nDER breaker with a dedicated<br>\nprotection relay scheme<br>\nmeeting the isolation,<br>\nanti-islanding, and<br>\ncoordination objectives of<br>\nDPS-07 §4.1, §4.3, and §4.5.<br>\nThe arrangement decouples<br>\ngeneration from utility-side<br>\nfault clearing without a<br>\nutility-owned POI recloser at<br>\nthe point of interconnection;<br>\nsite retains customer-owned<br>\nlockable visible-break<br>\ndisconnect per DIS-01 Table<br>\n5-1 and provides utility<br>\nSCADA visibility per TCS-02.<br>\nEach of the above determinations is filed with the application record for the affected site and is binding for<br>\nthat site only. The pattern reflected in these three closures does not select a controlling document across<br>\nthe program: DIS-01 §5 Table 5-1 and DPS-07 §4.2 each remain in force as issued, and each new site<br>\npresenting a comparable question is referred under §3 and determined on its own protection scheme.<br>\n End of ERP-9 Rev 2 — Engineering Referral Procedure.</p>	[]	markdown	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	markdown	en	9	9	{}
44	der-program/standards/og-17-equipment-deployment-prioritization	d81fe34247c683c5703d	OG-17 Equipment Deployment Prioritization	Equipment deployment prioritization guideline effective 04/07/2025 with commissioning criticality criteria	f	t				# OG-17 Equipment Deployment Prioritization\n\nOG-17 Rev 1 — Equipment Deployment Prioritization Guideline Page 1\nALLENIOM UTILITIES — DISTRIBUTION OPERATIONS\nEQUIPMENT DEPLOYMENT PRIORITIZATION\nGUIDELINE\nDoc no. Rev Effective Date Owner\nOG-17 Rev 1 04/07/2025 Alleniom Distribution\nOperations\nDocument Control\nSupersedes: none (initial issue).\nReview cycle: annual, or on material change to supplier lead times or operating criticality categories.\nApproving authority: VP, Distribution Operations.\nDistribution: DER Program Office; Interconnection Studies; DER Planning Group; System Operations Center\n(SOC); Standards Engineering; Supply Chain — Distribution Equipment.\n§1  SCOPE\nThis guideline governs deployment sequencing of constrained utility equipment (RTUs, reclosers) across\ncompleted sites awaiting energization. It applies to Alleniom-owned devices installed by Distribution\nOperations for the purpose of energizing a construction-complete distributed energy resource (DER) site,\nwhen supplier lead times or quarterly delivery quantities constrain the number of sites that can be\nequipped in a given deployment window.\nThis guideline does NOT apply to application processing. Nondiscriminatory queue-order treatment of\ninterconnection applications is governed by Interconnection Tariff §12.7 and by the procedures\nadministered by Interconnection Studies; nothing in this guideline modifies, displaces, or supplements\nthose procedures. The rankings established under §2 operate solely on sites for which interconnection\napplication processing is complete, the interconnection agreement (where required) is executed, and\nconstruction is certified complete by the customer or its contractor.\nDevices out of scope: customer-owned protective equipment; utility-owned equipment already installed at\nthe site; distribution-system reinforcement equipment scheduled under an approved capital work order.\nProgram scope for the underlying DER remains as defined in DIS-01 Rev 4 §3 and Interconnection Tariff\n§12.2.\n§2  COMMISSIONING CRITICALITY\n4200 Veldmere Boulevard, Halston, Alleron 01HA\nAs a general rule, deployment priority for constrained equipment follows interconnection queue order,\nconsistent with the nondiscriminatory treatment principles of Interconnection Tariff §12.7.\nA site may be advanced ahead of queue order under certain circumstances — the urgent nature of a\nrequest, in particular for public infrastructure (schools, hospitals, water/wastewater facilities), or an\nexpedited deployment pathway on reasonable customer request — but only where substantiated by the\nsite's own recorded attributes (per §3), in order of priority: (1) essential-service function (water/wastewater,\nemergency, health); (2) committed in-service or grant-funding deadline; (3) construction-complete date.\nAmong sites of the same critical nature, the deciding factor is interconnection queue order; requests not\nsubstantiated by these attributes remain in queue order.\n\n---\n\nOG-17 Rev 1 — Equipment Deployment Prioritization Guideline\nPage 2\nFactor definitions:\n Essential-service function. A site whose primary served load is a public water or wastewater facility;\na hospital, emergency-response, or first-responder facility; or a public-health facility for which loss of\nservice is treated as an emergency by the responsible jurisdiction. Essential-service status is\nevidenced by the customer's facility classification of record and, where applicable, the served-load\ndesignation on the interconnection application.\n Committed in-service or grant-funding deadline. A contractually committed in-service date, or a\ndate on which grant funding lapses if the site is not in service, established in a fully executed written\ninstrument between the customer and a governmental funder, project sponsor, or off-taker. Internal\ncustomer construction targets, marketing dates, budget-cycle expectations, and school-year,\nfiscal-year, or seasonal preferences do not constitute a committed deadline under this factor.\n Construction-complete date. The calendar date on which the customer's construction of the site\nwas certified complete and ready for utility energization steps. Where more than one date is recorded,\nthe earlier of (a) the customer's written notice of construction completion and (b) the utility's on-site\nverification, controls.\n Queue date as tiebreaker. The interconnection queue date of the site's underlying application, used\nonly to break a tie among sites that rank equally on factors (1), (2), and (3).\nExternal advocacy, correspondence volume, elected-official inquiries, and stakeholder communications\nreceived in relation to a site do not enter the ranking. Ranking is determined from the raw attributes above,\nevidenced by the source documents in §3.\n§3  DOCUMENTATION\nFor each deployment window the DER Program Office shall assemble a deployment worksheet listing\nevery site meeting the eligibility conditions of §1, with the following fields drawn from the identified system\nof record:\n Site name and interconnection application ID (DER Program queue).\n Customer / party of record and site service address (DER Program queue).\n Nameplate capacity and program-scope classification per DIS-01 Rev 4 §3 (application file).\n Devices required to energize (RTU, POI recloser, or both), quantities, and specification references\n(Standards Engineering).\n Construction-complete date and source of certification (project construction file).\n Contracted in-service or grant-funding deadline, if any, with the executed instrument establishing it\nattached by reference (customer submission).\n Essential-service classification, if any, with the customer's facility classification of record attached by\nreference (application file).\n Interconnection queue date (DER Program queue).\nThe worksheet shall record the §2 factor on which each ranked site qualifies. Sites that do not qualify at\nranks (1) or (2) are ranked by (3) with (4) as tiebreaker. The worksheet, the ranked deployment list, and\nthe assigned equipment-set identifiers shall be retained in the DER Program equipment-planning file for\nthe applicable window and provided to the SOC concurrent with the coordination step described in §5.\n\n\n---\n\nOG-17 Rev 1 — Equipment Deployment Prioritization Guideline\nPage 3\nAmendments to the worksheet arising after issuance — for example, receipt of previously missing\ndocumentation for a listed site, or arrival of a new site into the waiting population — shall be recorded in an\naddendum bearing its own date; prior ranked lists are not overwritten.\n§4  VENDOR ENGAGEMENT EXPECTATIONS\nDistribution Operations, working through Supply Chain, shall maintain rolling engagement with the current\napproved suppliers of RTUs and POI reclosers with the following expectations:\n Lead-time visibility. A monthly written statement of committed lead times, next scheduled delivery\ndate, and delivery quantity per device type, retained in the equipment-planning file. Any change to a\npreviously committed date or quantity is to be flagged in writing on receipt.\n Order coverage. Standing purchase orders shall cover no fewer than the number of device sets\nrequired to satisfy the sites eligible under §1 for the current deployment window plus the next\nscheduled delivery window. Expedite requests shall be pursued where a delivery-date change would\ndisplace a rank-(1) or rank-(2) site.\n Alternate sourcing. Where committed lead times exceed the interval between deliveries, Supply\nChain shall document the shortcoming of the current approved suppliers and evaluate qualification of\nadditional vendors under Standards Engineering's certification procedures. Qualification effort shall\nnot condition or delay deployment decisions for the current window.\n Substitution. Any proposed substitution of device model or configuration relative to the site's\napproved specification is routed to Standards Engineering; substitutions are not accepted on\nschedule pressure alone.\n Escrow / spares. A minimum spare quantity per device type shall be held at the operations\nwarehouse, replenished on drawdown, and not consumed to accelerate a lower-ranked site ahead of\na higher-ranked site whose device set is en route.\nVendor engagement records are subject to audit by Regulatory Affairs on request.\n§5  COORDINATION WITH THE SYSTEM OPERATIONS CENTER\nThe ranked deployment list issued under §3 is transmitted to the SOC concurrent with its issuance.\nCoordination steps are as follows:\n Pre-deployment briefing. For each device set scheduled to arrive in the deployment window, the\nDER Program Office and the SOC Shift Supervisor confirm the receiving site, the anticipated\nfield-installation window, the commissioning sequence, and the telemetry integration steps required\nfor permission-to-operate (PTO). Nothing in this guideline modifies the SOC's PTO gating criteria;\nTCS-02 §5 requires utility-owned RTU and SCADA telemetry with live telemetry established prior to\nPTO for sites at or above 500 kW, and this guideline does not authorize interim or provisional\nenergization ahead of that condition.\n Change control. A change to the ranked list after transmittal to the SOC — for example, arrival of a\nnew rank-(1) site, or documented lapse of a committed deadline previously credited at rank (2) — is\nissued by written addendum jointly to Supply Chain and to the SOC. Verbal reprioritization is not to be\nrelied upon.\n Field constraints. Where feeder condition, crew availability, or outage-window constraints prevent\ninstallation at a higher-ranked site within the window, the SOC records the constraint and the DER\nProgram Office reissues the deployment assignment for that device set to the next-eligible site; the\n\n\n---\n\nOG-17 Rev 1 — Equipment Deployment Prioritization Guideline\nPage 4\nhigher-ranked site retains its rank for the following window.\n Commissioning readiness. The SOC's PTO-hold log continues to govern which completed sites are\neligible for energization steps; sites without established telemetry remain on the hold log until the\nutility-owned RTU is installed and telemetry is verified in accordance with TCS-02.\n Reporting. A written record of device-set assignments, installation dates, commissioning outcomes,\nand any addenda issued during the window is retained by the DER Program Office and made\navailable to Regulatory Affairs and to the SOC on request.\nEnd of Guideline OG-17.	<h1 id="og-17-equipment-deployment-prioritization">OG-17 Equipment Deployment Prioritization</h1>\n<p>OG-17 Rev 1 — Equipment Deployment Prioritization Guideline Page 1<br>\nALLENIOM UTILITIES — DISTRIBUTION OPERATIONS<br>\nEQUIPMENT DEPLOYMENT PRIORITIZATION<br>\nGUIDELINE<br>\nDoc no. Rev Effective Date Owner<br>\nOG-17 Rev 1 04/07/2025 Alleniom Distribution<br>\nOperations<br>\nDocument Control<br>\nSupersedes: none (initial issue).<br>\nReview cycle: annual, or on material change to supplier lead times or operating criticality categories.<br>\nApproving authority: VP, Distribution Operations.<br>\nDistribution: DER Program Office; Interconnection Studies; DER Planning Group; System Operations Center<br>\n(SOC); Standards Engineering; Supply Chain — Distribution Equipment.<br>\n§1  SCOPE<br>\nThis guideline governs deployment sequencing of constrained utility equipment (RTUs, reclosers) across<br>\ncompleted sites awaiting energization. It applies to Alleniom-owned devices installed by Distribution<br>\nOperations for the purpose of energizing a construction-complete distributed energy resource (DER) site,<br>\nwhen supplier lead times or quarterly delivery quantities constrain