in in queue order.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>OG-17 Rev 1 — Equipment Deployment Prioritization Guideline<br>\nPage 2<br>\nFactor definitions:<br>\n Essential-service function. A site whose primary served load is a public water or wastewater facility;<br>\na hospital, emergency-response, or first-responder facility; or a public-health facility for which loss of<br>\nservice is treated as an emergency by the responsible jurisdiction. Essential-service status is<br>\nevidenced by the customer's facility classification of record and, where applicable, the served-load<br>\ndesignation on the interconnection application.<br>\n Committed in-service or grant-funding deadline. A contractually committed in-service date, or a<br>\ndate on which grant funding lapses if the site is not in service, established in a fully executed written<br>\ninstrument between the customer and a governmental funder, project sponsor, or off-taker. Internal<br>\ncustomer construction targets, marketing dates, budget-cycle expectations, and school-year,<br>\nfiscal-year, or seasonal preferences do not constitute a committed deadline under this factor.<br>\n Construction-complete date. The calendar date on which the customer's construction of the site<br>\nwas certified complete and ready for utility energization steps. Where more than one date is recorded,<br>\nthe earlier of (a) the customer's written notice of construction completion and (b) the utility's on-site<br>\nverification, controls.<br>\n Queue date as tiebreaker. The interconnection queue date of the site's underlying application, used<br>\nonly to break a tie among sites that rank equally on factors (1), (2), and (3).<br>\nExternal advocacy, correspondence volume, elected-official inquiries, and stakeholder communications<br>\nreceived in relation to a site do not enter the ranking. Ranking is determined from the raw attributes above,<br>\nevidenced by the source documents in §3.<br>\n§3  DOCUMENTATION<br>\nFor each deployment window the DER Program Office shall assemble a deployment worksheet listing<br>\nevery site meeting the eligibility conditions of §1, with the following fields drawn from the identified system<br>\nof record:<br>\n Site name and interconnection application ID (DER Program queue).<br>\n Customer / party of record and site service address (DER Program queue).<br>\n Nameplate capacity and program-scope classification per DIS-01 Rev 4 §3 (application file).<br>\n Devices required to energize (RTU, POI recloser, or both), quantities, and specification references<br>\n(Standards Engineering).<br>\n Construction-complete date and source of certification (project construction file).<br>\n Contracted in-service or grant-funding deadline, if any, with the executed instrument establishing it<br>\nattached by reference (customer submission).<br>\n Essential-service classification, if any, with the customer's facility classification of record attached by<br>\nreference (application file).<br>\n Interconnection queue date (DER Program queue).<br>\nThe worksheet shall record the §2 factor on which each ranked site qualifies. Sites that do not qualify at<br>\nranks (1) or (2) are ranked by (3) with (4) as tiebreaker. The worksheet, the ranked deployment list, and<br>\nthe assigned equipment-set identifiers shall be retained in the DER Program equipment-planning file for<br>\nthe applicable window and provided to the SOC concurrent with the coordination step described in §5.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>OG-17 Rev 1 — Equipment Deployment Prioritization Guideline<br>\nPage 3<br>\nAmendments to the worksheet arising after issuance — for example, receipt of previously missing<br>\ndocumentation for a listed site, or arrival of a new site into the waiting population — shall be recorded in an<br>\naddendum bearing its own date; prior ranked lists are not overwritten.<br>\n§4  VENDOR ENGAGEMENT EXPECTATIONS<br>\nDistribution Operations, working through Supply Chain, shall maintain rolling engagement with the current<br>\napproved suppliers of RTUs and POI reclosers with the following expectations:<br>\n Lead-time visibility. A monthly written statement of committed lead times, next scheduled delivery<br>\ndate, and delivery quantity per device type, retained in the equipment-planning file. Any change to a<br>\npreviously committed date or quantity is to be flagged in writing on receipt.<br>\n Order coverage. Standing purchase orders shall cover no fewer than the number of device sets<br>\nrequired to satisfy the sites eligible under §1 for the current deployment window plus the next<br>\nscheduled delivery window. Expedite requests shall be pursued where a delivery-date change would<br>\ndisplace a rank-(1) or rank-(2) site.<br>\n Alternate sourcing. Where committed lead times exceed the interval between deliveries, Supply<br>\nChain shall document the shortcoming of the current approved suppliers and evaluate qualification of<br>\nadditional vendors under Standards Engineering's certification procedures. Qualification effort shall<br>\nnot condition or delay deployment decisions for the current window.<br>\n Substitution. Any proposed substitution of device model or configuration relative to the site's<br>\napproved specification is routed to Standards Engineering; substitutions are not accepted on<br>\nschedule pressure alone.<br>\n Escrow / spares. A minimum spare quantity per device type shall be held at the operations<br>\nwarehouse, replenished on drawdown, and not consumed to accelerate a lower-ranked site ahead of<br>\na higher-ranked site whose device set is en route.<br>\nVendor engagement records are subject to audit by Regulatory Affairs on request.<br>\n§5  COORDINATION WITH THE SYSTEM OPERATIONS CENTER<br>\nThe ranked deployment list issued under §3 is transmitted to the SOC concurrent with its issuance.<br>\nCoordination steps are as follows:<br>\n Pre-deployment briefing. For each device set scheduled to arrive in the deployment window, the<br>\nDER Program Office and the SOC Shift Supervisor confirm the receiving site, the anticipated<br>\nfield-installation window, the commissioning sequence, and the telemetry integration steps required<br>\nfor permission-to-operate (PTO). Nothing in this guideline modifies the SOC's PTO gating criteria;<br>\nTCS-02 §5 requires utility-owned RTU and SCADA telemetry with live telemetry established prior to<br>\nPTO for sites at or above 500 kW, and this guideline does not authorize interim or provisional<br>\nenergization ahead of that condition.<br>\n Change control. A change to the ranked list after transmittal to the SOC — for example, arrival of a<br>\nnew rank-(1) site, or documented lapse of a committed deadline previously credited at rank (2) — is<br>\nissued by written addendum jointly to Supply Chain and to the SOC. Verbal reprioritization is not to be<br>\nrelied upon.<br>\n Field constraints. Where feeder condition, crew availability, or outage-window constraints prevent<br>\ninstallation at a higher-ranked site within the window, the SOC records the constraint and the DER<br>\nProgram Office reissues the deployment assignment for that device set to the next-eligible site; the</p>\n<hr>\n<p>OG-17 Rev 1 — Equipment Deployment Prioritization Guideline<br>\nPage 4<br>\nhigher-ranked site retains its rank for the following window.<br>\n Commissioning readiness. The SOC's PTO-hold log continues to govern which completed sites are<br>\neligible for energization steps; sites without established telemetry remain on the hold log until the<br>\nutility-owned RTU is installed and telemetry is verified in accordance with TCS-02.<br>\n Reporting. A written record of device-set assignments, installation dates, commissioning outcomes,<br>\nand any addenda issued during the window is retained by the DER Program Office and made<br>\navailable to Regulatory Affairs and to the SOC on request.<br>\nEnd of Guideline OG-17.</p>	[]	markdown	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	2026-11-20