45	der-program/standards/queue-management-withdrawal-policy	acc724d72c965c49062b	Queue Management Withdrawal Policy	Internal policy on queue management, withdrawal confirmations, and deemed-withdrawal procedures	f	t				# Queue Management Withdrawal Policy\n\nAlleniom Utilities — DER Program Office\n4200 Veldmere Boulevard, Halston, Alleron 01HA  ·  (612) 555-0100  ·  alleniom.com\nDoc no. POL-QM-05  ·  Rev 3  ·  Date 03/15/2025  ·  Owner: DER Program Office\nQueue Management & Withdrawal Policy\nApplies to:  All DER Interconnection Program applications (≥500 kW) administered by the DER Program Office.\nSupersedes:  POL-QM-05 Rev 2 (07/12/2023).\nOwner:  DER Program Office.\nApproving Authority:  Manager, Interconnection Studies.\n§1  Purpose\nThis policy sets out the internal procedures by which the DER Program Office administers queue integrity and orderly withdrawal of applications from the active DER interconnection queue. It is intended to ensure that:\n(a)\tapplications are closed only on a documented basis;\n(b)\tqueue positions are established, retained, and released in a manner consistent with the Company's APUC-filed interconnection tariff (in particular §12.3, §12.4, and §12.6) and with the DER Interconnection Standard (DIS-01);\n(c)\tstaff processing intake, screening, and case-management actions apply a single, uniform standard when an application appears stalled, abandoned, superseded, or replaced by a subsequent filing at the same site; and\n(d)\tthe record supporting each queue action is complete and auditable.\nNothing in this policy modifies the tariff. Where any provision of this policy is read to conflict with the tariff or with an applicable engineering standard, the tariff and the standard control, and the conflict shall be referred to Regulatory Affairs for reconciliation.\n§2  Withdrawal upon Customer Request\nA stalled or abandoned application is closed only upon receipt of the customer's official withdrawal confirmation, provided in writing by letter or email. Where a new filing is submitted for the same site while a prior application remains open, the program shall request the customer's official withdrawal of the prior filing; the new application takes its own queue date at time of filing. No queue-date inheritance is permitted.\n§2.1  Scope of §2\nThis §2 governs closure of applications that have not been the subject of a formal Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation under tariff §12.4. It applies where:\n(a)\tthe customer has ceased substantive engagement with the program (whether by prolonged non-response, express indication of intent not to proceed, or otherwise);\n(b)\tthe customer or an authorized developer has requested closure of an application without such a notice having issued; or\n(c)\ta new application is filed for the same site (as identified by service address, meter, or point of common coupling) while a prior application for that site remains in the active queue.\n§2.2  Form of the Withdrawal Confirmation\nTo constitute an “official withdrawal confirmation” for purposes of this §2, the writing must:\n(a)\tbe from the applicant of record (or from a person whose authority to act for the applicant has been established on the record — e.g., the developer named as the applicant's designated representative on the intake form);\n(b)\tbe received by the DER Program Office by letter or email, addressed to der.program@alleniom.com or delivered to the DER Program Office at the letterhead address above;\n(c)\tidentify the application by application identifier (PXA-YYYY-NNNN) and site; and\n(d)\tstate expressly that the applicant withdraws the identified application.\nNeither an oral communication (telephone, meeting, or voicemail), nor a status note recorded by staff, nor the mere filing of a new application at the same site, shall by itself constitute the official withdrawal confirmation required under this §2.\n§2.3  Procedure — Stalled or Abandoned Application (Non-Notice Path)\nWhere staff identify an application within the scope of §2.1(a) or §2.1(b), the case handler shall:\n1.\tConfirm the application's status in the active queue and document the basis for concluding that the application is stalled or abandoned;\n2.\tPrepare and send a written communication (letter or email) to the applicant of record requesting the applicant's official withdrawal confirmation in the form specified in §2.2;\n3.\tLog the request in the case file and in the queue-management system;\n4.\tOn receipt of the official withdrawal confirmation, close the application and record the closure date, the closure basis (“§2 — customer request”), and the reference to the writing on file; and\n5.\tPreserve the withdrawal writing in the case file in accordance with §4.\nAbsent receipt of the official withdrawal confirmation, the application shall not be closed under this §2. If the applicant does not provide the confirmation and non-response persists, the case handler may refer the matter for issuance of a formal Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation under §3.\n§2.4  Duplicate Filings at the Same Site\nWhere a new application is filed for the same site while a prior application for that site remains open in the queue:\n(a)\tThe DER Program Office shall accept the new application in the ordinary course and shall assign the new application its own queue date determined as of the date the new application is determined to be complete. No queue-date inheritance is permitted between the prior and the new application.\n(b)\tThe program shall request the customer's official withdrawal of the prior filing in the form specified in §2.2. That request may be combined with the completeness/acknowledgement communication for the new application.\n(c)\tThe prior application shall remain open in the queue until the official withdrawal confirmation is received, or until the prior application is otherwise closed by operation of §3 or under another tariff mechanism. The two applications shall not be merged, consolidated, or treated as continuations of a single filing.\n(d)\tWhere the applicant states a preference or expectation that the earlier queue date apply to the new filing, staff shall respond in writing that the new application's queue date is fixed by §12.3 of the tariff and by this §2.4, and that closure of the prior application does not alter the queue date of the new application.\n§2.5  Consistency with DIS-01 §7 and Tariff §12.6\nNothing in this §2 authorizes the use of a customer-requested withdrawal to reopen or amend an executed Interconnection Service Agreement. Post-execution changes to material terms of an ISA — including nameplate capacity and point of interconnection — require withdrawal-and-reapplication in accordance with DIS-01 §7 and tariff §12.6. A customer-requested withdrawal in that context establishes a new queue date for the successor application; no queue-date inheritance is permitted.\n§3  Deemed Withdrawal (Procedural Restatement of Tariff §12.4)\nFollowing issuance of a formal Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation, if the applicant fails to respond within 30 business days, the application is deemed withdrawn.\n§3.1  Relationship to the Tariff\nThis §3 is a procedural restatement, for internal use, of the deemed-withdrawal mechanism established by tariff §12.4. In the event of any inconsistency between this §3 and §12.4, the tariff controls. Staff shall consult §12.4 for the authoritative statement of the mechanism.\n§3.2  Prerequisites to Issuance of a Notice\nThe DER Program Office shall not issue a Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation unless:\n(a)\tthe application has been open in the queue and staff have been unable to advance it because of the applicant's non-response or failure to supply required information or documentation;\n(b)\tat least one prior written follow-up has been sent to the applicant identifying the item or items required and requesting response by a stated date; and\n(c)\tthe case handler has confirmed that the matter does not fall within the construction-payment default procedure under tariff §12.5, which is a distinct mechanism (project on hold, not withdrawn, while a 30-business-day payment deadline runs).\n§3.3  Form and Service of the Notice\nEvery Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation shall:\n(a)\tbe issued in writing on Alleniom Utilities letterhead;\n(b)\tbe served by certified mail or by verified electronic delivery, in accordance with tariff §12.4;\n(c)\tidentify the application by identifier and site;\n(d)\tstate the item(s) requiring response;\n(e)\tstate the date of issuance and the date on which the 30 business-day response window expires, computed under §3.4; and\n(f)\tstate the consequence of non-response, i.e., that the application shall be deemed withdrawn and closed from the active queue without further action.\n§3.4  Computation of 30 Business Days\nFor purposes of this §3 and of tariff §12.4:\n(a)\t“Business days” excludes Saturdays, Sundays, and federal legal public holidays observed within the response window;\n(b)\tDay 1 is the first business day after the date of issuance stated on the notice;\n(c)\tThe following federal holidays observed within response windows relevant to the current filing period are recognized as non-business days and are not counted:\n(d)\tWhere a response window straddles a federal holiday not listed above, the case handler shall exclude that holiday from the count and document the calculation in the case file.\n§3.5  Effect of Non-Response\nIf the applicant fails to submit a substantive response curing the matter identified in the notice within 30 business days computed under §3.4, the application shall be deemed withdrawn as of the day following expiration of the window. The DER Program Office shall:\n(a)\tmark the application “deemed withdrawn — §12.4” in the queue-management system;\n(b)\trecord the notice-issuance date, the expiration date, and the closure date in the withdrawal-notice log; and\n(c)\tpreserve the notice, proof of service, and the closure entry in the case file in accordance with §4.\n§3.6  Effect of Timely Response\nA substantive response received on or before expiration of the 30 business-day window suspends the deemed-withdrawal consequence. The case handler shall evaluate the response and, if the identified matter is cured, release the application to resume processing. If the response is non-substantive or does not cure the identified matter, the case handler shall so document and, if warranted, issue a further follow-up or a subsequent Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation.\n§3.7  Non-Substitution\nIssuance of a Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation is not a substitute for the customer-request path under §2, and the customer-request path under §2 is not a substitute for the deemed-withdrawal path under §3. Case handlers shall not use one mechanism to accomplish what should be effected under the other.\n§4  Record-Keeping Requirements\n§4.1  Case File Contents\nFor every application closed under §2 or §3, the DER Program Office shall maintain a case file containing, at minimum:\n(a)\tthe original application and all completeness/acknowledgement communications;\n(b)\tfor §2 closures: the writing (letter or email) requesting official withdrawal confirmation from the applicant; the applicant's official withdrawal confirmation (letter or email) as received; and any related correspondence bearing on the applicant's intent to withdraw;\n(c)\tfor §3 closures: the prior follow-up correspondence relied upon under §3.2(b); the Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation as issued; proof of service (certified mail receipt or verified electronic delivery record); the 30 business-day computation worksheet; and any response received;\n(d)\tthe queue-management system entry recording the closure basis, closure date, and (for §2 duplicate-filing cases) the queue-date determination applied to the successor application under §2.4(a); and\n(e)\tany related engineering or standards correspondence relied upon in the closure decision.\n§4.2  Queue-Management System Entries\nEvery closure shall be recorded in the queue-management system with:\n(a)\tapplication identifier and site;\n(b)\tclosure basis, expressed as one of: “§2 — customer request (letter)”, “§2 — customer request (email)”, “§2.4 — superseded by successor application”, “§12.4 — deemed withdrawn”, or such other codes as the DER Program Office may prescribe;\n(c)\tclosure date;\n(d)\tcase handler; and\n(e)\treference to the writing(s) supporting the closure basis.\n§4.3  Withdrawal-Notice Log\nThe DER Program Office shall maintain a running Withdrawal-Notice Log recording every Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation issued. The log shall include, at minimum: application identifier; site; date of issuance; method of service; response deadline (computed under §3.4); response received (date and disposition, if any); and closure date (if any). The log is subject to periodic reconciliation against the on-hold queue report; unreconciled entries shall be brought to the attention of the Manager, Interconnection Studies.\n§4.4  Retention\nCase files and log entries shall be retained in accordance with the Company's records retention schedule for regulated interconnection records. Records shall not be purged, redacted, or altered while an application remains open, while a response window under §3 is running, or while the closure is within the period during which reconsideration may be sought.\n§4.5  Audit and Reporting\nThe Manager, Interconnection Studies shall be prepared, on request of Regulatory Affairs, to produce for any closed application: the closure basis; the writing(s) supporting the closure basis; the queue-date determination for any successor application; and, where applicable, the notice, proof of service, and 30 business-day computation. Reports to the Alleron Public Utilities Commission concerning queue closures shall be prepared from the withdrawal-notice log and the queue-management system entries described above.\nDocument Control\n\n| Holiday | Observed Date |\n| --- | --- |\n| Memorial Day | 05/26/2025 |\n| Juneteenth National Independence Day | 06/19/2025 |\n| Independence Day | 07/04/2025 |\n\n\n| Field | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Document number | POL-QM-05 |\n| Revision | Rev 3 |\n| Effective date | 03/15/2025 |\n| Owner | DER Program Office |\n| Approving authority | Manager, Interconnection Studies |\n| Prior revision | POL-QM-05 Rev 2 (07/12/2023) |\n| Reference — tariff | §12.3, §12.4, §12.5, §12.6, §12.7 |\n| Reference — standards | DIS-01 §7 |	<h1 id="queue-management-withdrawal-policy">Queue Management Withdrawal Policy</h1>\n<p>Alleniom Utilities — DER Program Office<br>\n4200 Veldmere Boulevard, Halston, Alleron 01HA  ·  (612) 555-0100  ·  alleniom.com<br>\nDoc no. POL-QM-05  ·  Rev 3  ·  Date 03/15/2025  ·  Owner: DER Program Office<br>\nQueue Management &amp; Withdrawal Policy<br>\nApplies to:  All DER Interconnection Program applications (≥500 kW) administered by the DER Program Office.<br>\nSupersedes:  POL-QM-05 Rev 2 (07/12/2023).<br>\nOwner:  DER Program Office.<br>\nApproving Authority:  Manager, Interconnection Studies.<br>\n§1  Purpose<br>\nThis policy sets out the internal procedures by which the DER Program Office administers queue integrity and orderly withdrawal of applications from the active DER interconnection queue. It is intended to ensure that:<br>\n(a) applications are closed only on a documented basis;<br>\n(b) queue positions are established, retained, and released in a manner consistent with the Company's APUC-filed interconnection tariff (in particular §12.3, §12.4, and §12.6) and with the DER Interconnection Standard (DIS-01);<br>\n(c) staff processing intake, screening, and case-management actions apply a single, uniform standard when an application appears stalled, abandoned, superseded, or replaced by a subsequent filing at the same site; and<br>\n(d) the record supporting each queue action is complete and auditable.<br>\nNothing in this policy modifies the tariff. Where any provision of this policy is read to conflict with the tariff or with an applicable engineering standard, the tariff and the standard control, and the conflict shall be referred to Regulatory Affairs for reconciliation.<br>\n§2  Withdrawal upon Customer Request<br>\nA stalled or abandoned application is closed only upon receipt of the customer's official withdrawal confirmation, provided in writing by letter or email. Where a new filing is submitted for the same site while a prior application remains open, the program shall request the customer's official withdrawal of the prior filing; the new application takes its own queue date at time of filing. No queue-date inheritance is permitted.<br>\n§2.1  Scope of §2<br>\nThis §2 governs closure of applications that have not been the subject of a formal Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation under tariff §12.4. It applies where:<br>\n(a) the customer has ceased substantive engagement with the program (whether by prolonged non-response, express indication of intent not to proceed, or otherwise);<br>\n(b) the customer or an authorized developer has requested closure of an application without such a notice having issued; or<br>\n(c) a new application is filed for the same site (as identified by service address, meter, or point of common coupling) while a prior application for that site remains in the active queue.<br>\n§2.2  Form of the Withdrawal Confirmation<br>\nTo constitute an “official withdrawal confirmation” for purposes of this §2, the writing must:<br>\n(a) be from the applicant of record (or from a person whose authority to act for the applicant has been established on the record — e.g., the developer named as the applicant's designated representative on the intake form);<br>\n(b) be received by the DER Program Office by letter or email, addressed to der.program@alleniom.com or delivered to the DER Program Office at the letterhead address above;<br>\n(c) identify the application by application identifier (PXA-YYYY-NNNN) and site; and<br>\n(d) state expressly that the applicant withdraws the identified application.<br>\nNeither an oral communication (telephone, meeting, or voicemail), nor a status note recorded by staff, nor the mere filing of a new application at the same site, shall by itself constitute the official withdrawal confirmation required under this §2.<br>\n§2.3  Procedure — Stalled or Abandoned Application (Non-Notice Path)<br>\nWhere staff identify an application within the scope of §2.1(a) or §2.1(b), the case handler shall:<br>\n1.  Confirm the application's status in the active queue and document the basis for concluding that the application is stalled or abandoned;<br>\n2.  Prepare and send a written communication (letter or email) to the applicant of record requesting the applicant's official withdrawal confirmation in the form specified in §2.2;<br>\n3.  Log the request in the case file and in the queue-management system;<br>\n4.  On receipt of the official withdrawal confirmation, close the application and record the closure date, the closure basis (“§2 — customer request”), and the reference to the writing on file; and<br>\n5.  Preserve the withdrawal writing in the case file in accordance with §4.<br>\nAbsent receipt of the official withdrawal confirmation, the application shall not be closed under this §2. If the applicant does not provide the confirmation and non-response persists, the case handler may refer the matter for issuance of a formal Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation under §3.<br>\n§2.4  Duplicate Filings at the Same Site<br>\nWhere a new application is filed for the same site while a prior application for that site remains open in the queue:<br>\n(a) The DER Program Office shall accept the new application in the ordinary course and shall assign the new application its own queue date determined as of the date the new application is determined to be complete. No queue-date inheritance is permitted between the prior and the new application.<br>\n(b) The program shall request the customer's official withdrawal of the prior filing in the form specified in §2.2. That request may be combined with the completeness/acknowledgement communication for the new application.<br>\n(c) The prior application shall remain open in the queue until the official withdrawal confirmation is received, or until the prior application is otherwise closed by operation of §3 or under another tariff mechanism. The two applications shall not be merged, consolidated, or treated as continuations of a single filing.<br>\n(d) Where the applicant states a preference or expectation that the earlier queue date apply to the new filing, staff shall respond in writing that the new application's queue date is fixed by §12.3 of the tariff and by this §2.4, and that closure of the prior application does not alter the queue date of the new application.<br>\n§2.5  Consistency with DIS-01 §7 and Tariff §12.6<br>\nNothing in this §2 authorizes the use of a customer-requested withdrawal to reopen or amend an executed Interconnection Service Agreement. Post-execution changes to material terms of an ISA — including nameplate capacity and point of interconnection — require withdrawal-and-reapplication in accordance with DIS-01 §7 and tariff §12.6. A customer-requested withdrawal in that context establishes a new queue date for the successor application; no queue-date inheritance is permitted.<br>\n§3  Deemed Withdrawal (Procedural Restatement of Tariff §12.4)<br>\nFollowing issuance of a formal Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation, if the applicant fails to respond within 30 business days, the application is deemed withdrawn.<br>\n§3.1  Relationship to the Tariff<br>\nThis §3 is a procedural restatement, for internal use, of the deemed-withdrawal mechanism established by tariff §12.4. In the event of any inconsistency between this §3 and §12.4, the tariff controls. Staff shall consult §12.4 for the authoritative statement of the mechanism.<br>\n§3.2  Prerequisites to Issuance of a Notice<br>\nThe DER Program Office shall not issue a Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation unless:<br>\n(a) the application has been open in the queue and staff have been unable to advance it because of the applicant's non-response or failure to supply required information or documentation;<br>\n(b) at least one prior written follow-up has been sent to the applicant identifying the item or items required and requesting response by a stated date; and<br>\n(c) the case handler has confirmed that the matter does not fall within the construction-payment default procedure under tariff §12.5, which is a distinct mechanism (project on hold, not withdrawn, while a 30-business-day payment deadline runs).<br>\n§3.3  Form and Service of the Notice<br>\nEvery Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation shall:<br>\n(a) be issued in writing on Alleniom Utilities letterhead;<br>\n(b) be served by certified mail or by verified electronic delivery, in accordance with tariff §12.4;<br>\n(c) identify the application by identifier and site;<br>\n(d) state the item(s) requiring response;<br>\n(e) state the date of issuance and the date on which the 30 business-day response window expires, computed under §3.4; and<br>\n(f) state the consequence of non-response, i.e., that the application shall be deemed withdrawn and closed from the active queue without further action.<br>\n§3.4  Computation of 30 Business Days<br>\nFor purposes of this §3 and of tariff §12.4:<br>\n(a) “Business days” excludes Saturdays, Sundays, and federal legal public holidays observed within the response window;<br>\n(b) Day 1 is the first business day after the date of issuance stated on the notice;<br>\n(c) The following federal holidays observed within response windows relevant to the current filing period are recognized as non-business days and are not counted:<br>\n(d) Where a response window straddles a federal holiday not listed above, the case handler shall exclude that holiday from the count and document the calculation in the case file.<br>\n§3.5  Effect of Non-Response<br>\nIf the applicant fails to submit a substantive response curing the matter identified in the notice within 30 business days computed under §3.4, the application shall be deemed withdrawn as of the day following expiration of the window. The DER Program Office shall:<br>\n(a) mark the application “deemed withdrawn — §12.4” in the queue-management system;<br>\n(b) record the notice-issuance date, the expiration date, and the closure date in the withdrawal-notice log; and<br>\n(c) preserve the notice, proof of service, and the closure entry in the case file in accordance with §4.<br>\n§3.6  Effect of Timely Response<br>\nA substantive response received on or before expiration of the 30 business-day window suspends the deemed-withdrawal consequence. The case handler shall evaluate the response and, if the identified matter is cured, release the application to resume processing. If the response is non-substantive or does not cure the identified matter, the case handler shall so document and, if warranted, issue a further follow-up or a subsequent Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation.<br>\n§3.7  Non-Substitution<br>\nIssuance of a Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation is not a substitute for the customer-request path under §2, and the customer-request path under §2 is not a substitute for the deemed-withdrawal path under §3. Case handlers shall not use one mechanism to accomplish what should be effected under the other.<br>\n§4  Record-Keeping Requirements<br>\n§4.1  Case File Contents<br>\nFor every application closed under §2 or §3, the DER Program Office shall maintain a case file containing, at minimum:<br>\n(a) the original application and all completeness/acknowledgement communications;<br>\n(b) for §2 closures: the writing (letter or email) requesting official withdrawal confirmation from the applicant; the applicant's official withdrawal confirmation (letter or email) as received; and any related correspondence bearing on the applicant's intent to withdraw;<br>\n(c) for §3 closures: the prior follow-up correspondence relied upon under §3.2(b); the Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation as issued; proof of service (certified mail receipt or verified electronic delivery record); the 30 business-day computation worksheet; and any response received;<br>\n(d) the queue-management system entry recording the closure basis, closure date, and (for §2 duplicate-filing cases) the queue-date determination applied to the successor application under §2.4(a); and<br>\n(e) any related engineering or standards correspondence relied upon in the closure decision.<br>\n§4.2  Queue-Management System Entries<br>\nEvery closure shall be recorded in the queue-management system with:<br>\n(a) application identifier and site;<br>\n(b) closure basis, expressed as one of: “§2 — customer request (letter)”, “§2 — customer request (email)”, “§2.4 — superseded by successor application”, “§12.4 — deemed withdrawn”, or such other codes as the DER Program Office may prescribe;<br>\n(c) closure date;<br>\n(d) case handler; and<br>\n(e) reference to the writing(s) supporting the closure basis.<br>\n§4.3  Withdrawal-Notice Log<br>\nThe DER Program Office shall maintain a running Withdrawal-Notice Log recording every Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation issued. The log shall include, at minimum: application identifier; site; date of issuance; method of service; response deadline (computed under §3.4); response received (date and disposition, if any); and closure date (if any). The log is subject to periodic reconciliation against the on-hold queue report; unreconciled entries shall be brought to the attention of the Manager, Interconnection Studies.<br>\n§4.4  Retention<br>\nCase files and log entries shall be retained in accordance with the Company's records retention schedule for regulated interconnection records. Records shall not be purged, redacted, or altered while an application remains open, while a response window under §3 is running, or while the closure is within the period during which reconsideration may be sought.<br>\n§4.5  Audit and Reporting<br>\nThe Manager, Interconnection Studies shall be prepared, on request of Regulatory Affairs, to produce for any closed application: the closure basis; the writing(s) supporting the closure basis; the queue-date determination for any successor application; and, where applicable, the notice, proof of service, and 30 business-day computation. Reports to the Alleron Public Utilities Commission concerning queue closures shall be prepared from the withdrawal-notice log and the queue-management system entries described above.<br>\nDocument Control</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Holiday</th>\n<th>Observed Date</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Memorial Day</td>\n<td>05/26/2025</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Juneteenth National Independence Day</td>\n<td>06/19/2025</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Independence Day</td>\n<td>07/04/2025</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Document number</td>\n<td>POL-QM-05</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Revision</td>\n<td>Rev 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Effective date</td>\n<td>03/15/2025</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Owner</td>\n<td>DER Program Office</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Approving authority</td>\n<td>Manager, Interconnection Studies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prior revision</td>\n<td>POL-QM-05 Rev 2 (07/12/2023)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reference — tariff</td>\n<td>§12.3, §12.4, §12.5, §12.6, §12.7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reference — standards</td>\n<td>DIS-01 §7</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>	[]	markdown	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	markdown	en	5	5	{}
46	der-program/standards/srp-3-rev3-supplemental-review-procedure	6b7725e79e1c121aebc4	SRP-3 Rev3 Supplemental Review Procedure	Supplemental Review Procedure Rev 3 effective 02/10/2025 with daytime window requirement	f	t				# SRP-3 Rev3 Supplemental Review Procedure\n\nSRP-3 Rev 3 — Supplemental Review Procedure  ·  Effective 02/10/2025  ·  Alleniom Utilities — Standards Engineering\nPage 1\nALLENIOM UTILITIES — STANDARDS ENGINEERING\nSUPPLEMENTAL REVIEW PROCEDURE\nDoc no.\nRev\nEffective date\nOwner\nSRP-3\nRev 3\n02/10/2025\nAlleniom Standards Engineering\nSupersedes: SRP-3 Rev 2 (effective 07/22/2022)\nClassification: Technical Standard — Distributed Energy Resource Interconnection\nDistribution: DER Program Office; Interconnection Studies; Distribution Planning; Standards Engineering;\nRegulatory Affairs\n§1   Purpose\nThis procedure defines the supplemental review process applied to distributed energy resource (DER)\ninterconnection applications that fail one or more of the fast-track technical screens set out in the Alleniom DER\nInterconnection Standard (DIS-01 Rev 4). It establishes the ordered screens, data sources, and documentation to\nbe produced by the interconnection engineer conducting supplemental review, and identifies the conditions under\nwhich a study agreement is required before the application may proceed.\nSupplemental review is intended to allow an application that fails an initial fast-track screen to be evaluated\nagainst a bounded set of additional technical criteria without incurring the cost and schedule of a full system\nimpact study, where that evaluation can be completed from existing feeder data and standard engineering\njudgment.\nNothing in this procedure alters the substantive interconnection standards contained in DIS-01, the protection\nrequirements contained in DPS-07, or the telemetry and communications requirements contained in TCS-02.\n§2   Scope\nThis procedure applies to:\n(a)  all in-scope DER interconnection applications (≥500 kW) as defined in DIS-01 §3 that have entered\nsupplemental review following failure of an initial screen; and\n(b)  all DER classes recognized in DIS-01 §3 — commercial small-scale (500 kW – 1 MW) and medium-scale (1\nMW – 5 MW).\nThis procedure does not apply to:\n(a)  sub-500 kW installations processed under the Simplified Small-Systems Process;\n(b)  capacity or point-of-interconnection changes to an executed interconnection service agreement, which are\ngoverned by DIS-01 §7; or\n(c)  domestic rooftop solar, which is out of scope for the DER Program.\nWhere a supplemental-review screen result is inconclusive on the record before the engineer, the application shall\nbe advanced to a study agreement rather than passed or failed under this procedure.\n§3   Screens\n\n\n---\n\nSRP-3 Rev 3 — Supplemental Review Procedure  ·  Effective 02/10/2025  ·  Alleniom Utilities — Standards Engineering\nPage 2\nApplications entering supplemental review shall be evaluated under the following screens, in the order given. An\napplication that fails any screen shall be considered a candidate for a study agreement; the reviewing engineer\nshall document the failing screen, the underlying data, and the recommended disposition per §6.\nScreen A — Aggregate DER penetration on the feeder. Total nameplate DER (existing plus queued plus\nrequested) on the subject feeder shall not exceed the applicable penetration threshold set in DIS-01. Queue\npositions are those on record with the DER Program Office at the date of screen evaluation.\nScreen B — Voltage regulation and flicker. Reviewed against the feeder's most recent planning study or, where\nnone is on file for the subject feeder within twenty-four (24) months, against a screening voltage-rise calculation\nperformed using conductor and transformer impedance from the distribution planning record.\nScreen C — Short-circuit contribution. DER short-circuit contribution shall not cause any feeder-protective\ndevice to exceed 87.5% of its interrupting rating, nor cause any downstream protection coordination margin to\nfall below the values specified in DPS-07.\nScreen D — 100%-of-minimum-load screen for solar. Governed by §4.\nScreen E — Reverse power flow at substation. Where the aggregate DER on the subject feeder exceeds the\nsubstation transformer's certified reverse-power capability, or where reverse-power protective settings are not in\nplace, the application shall be routed to study.\nPassing all applicable screens is a necessary but not sufficient condition for interconnection; the application\nremains subject to the balance of the DIS-01 evaluation, including protection and telemetry requirements.\n§4   100%-of-minimum-load screen for solar\n100%-of-minimum-load screen for solar. For solar DER, the minimum load used in this screen shall be the\nminimum load within the daytime window 10:00–16:00, determined from feeder AMI interval data. The absolute\novernight minimum shall NOT be used. Requested DER capacity must be ≤ this daytime-window minimum for\nthe site to pass the screen.\nThe daytime-window minimum shall be computed from feeder AMI interval data covering the most recent\ncomplete twelve (12) calendar months, aggregated to fifteen-minute intervals aligned to the feeder AMI\ncollection cadence, and evaluated only over intervals whose start time falls within 10:00–16:00 local clock time\non the feeder. Intervals affected by known feeder outages, load transfers, or planned switching shall be excluded\nand the exclusion documented in the reviewer's worksheet. Where fewer than twelve (12) months of AMI interval\ndata are available, the reviewer shall use the longest continuous complete period available and note the\ndata-window length in the supplemental review memo.\nThe daytime-window minimum determined under this section is the sole minimum-load figure used for the solar\n100%-of-minimum-load screen. Feeder minimum-load figures produced for other purposes — planning studies,\nhosting-capacity map inputs, protection coordination — are not substitutes for the value computed under this\nsection and shall not be carried into the Screen D determination without recomputation on the AMI record.\nWhere a prior supplemental review memo on the same feeder has applied a different minimum-load basis, the\nreviewer shall recompute the value under this section and, if the two disagree, supersede the earlier memo in the\nfile.\n§5   Data sources\nThe following data sources are authoritative for supplemental review under this procedure:\n\n\n---\n\nSRP-3 Rev 3 — Supplemental Review Procedure  ·  Effective 02/10/2025  ·  Alleniom Utilities — Standards EngineeringPage 3\n(a)  Feeder AMI interval data — from the Alleniom AMI Data Warehouse, at the meter aggregation level\npublished for the subject feeder. AMI interval data is the sole authoritative source for the Screen D\nminimum-load determination described in §4.\n(b)  Distribution planning record — most recent feeder planning study, conductor and transformer impedance\ndata, and protection coordination settings, held by Distribution Planning.\n(c)  DER queue — active-queue and on-hold registers maintained by the DER Program Office as of the screen\nevaluation date. Queue entries not yet reflected in a published hosting-capacity map remain applicable to Screen\nA.\n(d)  Certified Equipment List (CEL) — current edition, for verification that proposed inverters and PCS\nequipment are eligible to be considered under the screens.\n(e)  Interconnection application record — the applicant's filed capacity, single-line diagram, inverter\nspecifications, and control narrative (where controlled-capacity operation is asserted under DIS-01 §3.6).\nSubstitution of a source not listed above shall be documented in the memo produced under §6, together with the\nreason for the substitution.\n§6   Documentation\nThe reviewing engineer shall produce a supplemental review memo for each application processed under this\nprocedure. The memo shall contain, at minimum:\n(a)  application identifier, applicant, subject feeder, and requested capacity;\n(b)  the screens applied and the pass/fail determination for each;\n(c)  for Screen D, the daytime-window minimum load determined per §4, the data window used, the count of\nintervals evaluated, and any interval exclusions with reason;\n(d)  the recommended disposition — advance without study agreement, advance to study agreement, or return to\napplicant with specified deficiency;\n(e)  reviewer signature and date; and\n(f)  the identifiers of any earlier supplemental review memos superseded.\nThe memo shall be filed to the interconnection application record within five (5) business days of screen\ncompletion. Where a memo supersedes an earlier determination on the same application or the same feeder, the\nsuperseded memo shall be flagged in the file and a cross-reference to the superseding memo added to its cover.\nAlleniom Utilities — DER Program Office · 4200 Veldmere Boulevard, Halston, Alleron 01HA · (612) 555-0100 · alleniom.com	<h1 id="srp-3-rev3-supplemental-review-procedure">SRP-3 Rev3 Supplemental Review Procedure</h1>\n<p>SRP-3 Rev 3 — Supplemental Review Procedure  ·  Effective 02/10/2025  ·  Alleniom Utilities — Standards Engineering<br>\nPage 1<br>\nALLENIOM UTILITIES — STANDARDS ENGINEERING<br>\nSUPPLEMENTAL REVIEW PROCEDURE<br>\nDoc no.<br>\nRev<br>\nEffective date<br>\nOwner<br>\nSRP-3<br>\nRev 3<br>\n02/10/2025<br>\nAlleniom Standards Engineering<br>\nSupersedes: SRP-3 Rev 2 (effective 07/22/2022)<br>\nClassification: Technical Standard — Distributed Energy Resource Interconnection<br>\nDistribution: DER Program Office; Interconnection Studies; Distribution Planning; Standards Engineering;<br>\nRegulatory Affairs<br>\n§1   Purpose<br>\nThis procedure defines the supplemental review process applied to distributed energy resource (DER)<br>\ninterconnection applications that fail one or more of the fast-track technical screens set out in the Alleniom DER<br>\nInterconnection Standard (DIS-01 Rev 4). It establishes the ordered screens, data sources, and documentation to<br>\nbe produced by the interconnection engineer conducting supplemental review, and identifies the conditions under<br>\nwhich a study agreement is required before the application may proceed.<br>\nSupplemental review is intended to allow an application that fails an initial fast-track screen to be evaluated<br>\nagainst a bounded set of additional technical criteria without incurring the cost and schedule of a full system<br>\nimpact study, where that evaluation can be completed from existing feeder data and standard engineering<br>\njudgment.<br>\nNothing in this procedure alters the substantive interconnection standards contained in DIS-01, the protection<br>\nrequirements contained in DPS-07, or the telemetry and communications requirements contained in TCS-02.<br>\n§2   Scope<br>\nThis procedure applies to:<br>\n(a)  all in-scope DER interconnection applications (≥500 kW) as defined in DIS-01 §3 that have entered<br>\nsupplemental review following failure of an initial screen; and<br>\n(b)  all DER classes recognized in DIS-01 §3 — commercial small-scale (500 kW – 1 MW) and medium-scale (1<br>\nMW – 5 MW).<br>\nThis procedure does not apply to:<br>\n(a)  sub-500 kW installations processed under the Simplified Small-Systems Process;<br>\n(b)  capacity or point-of-interconnection changes to an executed interconnection service agreement, which are<br>\ngoverned by DIS-01 §7; or<br>\n(c)  domestic rooftop solar, which is out of scope for the DER Program.<br>\nWhere a supplemental-review screen result is inconclusive on the record before the engineer, the application shall<br>\nbe advanced to a study agreement rather than passed or failed under this procedure.<br>\n§3   Screens</p>\n<hr>\n<p>SRP-3 Rev 3 — Supplemental Review Procedure  ·  Effective 02/10/2025  ·  Alleniom Utilities — Standards Engineering<br>\nPage 2<br>\nApplications entering supplemental review shall be evaluated under the following screens, in the order given. An<br>\napplication that fails any screen shall be considered a candidate for a study agreement; the reviewing engineer<br>\nshall document the failing screen, the underlying data, and the recommended disposition per §6.<br>\nScreen A — Aggregate DER penetration on the feeder. Total nameplate DER (existing plus queued plus<br>\nrequested) on the subject feeder shall not exceed the applicable penetration threshold set in DIS-01. Queue<br>\npositions are those on record with the DER Program Office at the date of screen evaluation.<br>\nScreen B — Voltage regulation and flicker. Reviewed against the feeder's most recent planning study or, where<br>\nnone is on file for the subject feeder within twenty-four (24) months, against a screening voltage-rise calculation<br>\nperformed using conductor and transformer impedance from the distribution planning record.<br>\nScreen C — Short-circuit contribution. DER short-circuit contribution shall not cause any feeder-protective<br>\ndevice to exceed 87.5% of its interrupting rating, nor cause any downstream protection coordination margin to<br>\nfall below the values specified in DPS-07.<br>\nScreen D — 100%-of-minimum-load screen for solar. Governed by §4.<br>\nScreen E — Reverse power flow at substation. Where the aggregate DER on the subject feeder exceeds the<br>\nsubstation transformer's certified reverse-power capability, or where reverse-power protective settings are not in<br>\nplace, the application shall be routed to study.<br>\nPassing all applicable screens is a necessary but not sufficient condition for interconnection; the application<br>\nremains subject to the balance of the DIS-01 evaluation, including protection and telemetry requirements.<br>\n§4   100%-of-minimum-load screen for solar<br>\n100%-of-minimum-load screen for solar. For solar DER, the minimum load used in this screen shall be the<br>\nminimum load within the daytime window 10:00–16:00, determined from feeder AMI interval data. The absolute<br>\novernight minimum shall NOT be used. Requested DER capacity must be ≤ this daytime-window minimum for<br>\nthe site to pass the screen.<br>\nThe daytime-window minimum shall be computed from feeder AMI interval data covering the most recent<br>\ncomplete twelve (12) calendar months, aggregated to fifteen-minute intervals aligned to the feeder AMI<br>\ncollection cadence, and evaluated only over intervals whose start time falls within 10:00–16:00 local clock time<br>\non the feeder. Intervals affected by known feeder outages, load transfers, or planned switching shall be excluded<br>\nand the exclusion documented in the reviewer's worksheet. Where fewer than twelve (12) months of AMI interval<br>\ndata are available, the reviewer shall use the longest continuous complete period available and note the<br>\ndata-window length in the supplemental review memo.<br>\nThe daytime-window minimum determined under this section is the sole minimum-load figure used for the solar<br>\n100%-of-minimum-load screen. Feeder minimum-load figures produced for other purposes — planning studies,<br>\nhosting-capacity map inputs, protection coordination — are not substitutes for the value computed under this<br>\nsection and shall not be carried into the Screen D determination without recomputation on the AMI record.<br>\nWhere a prior supplemental review memo on the same feeder has applied a different minimum-load basis, the<br>\nreviewer shall recompute the value under this section and, if the two disagree, supersede the earlier memo in the<br>\nfile.<br>\n§5   Data sources<br>\nThe following data sources are authoritative for supplemental review under this procedure:</p>\n<hr>\n<p>SRP-3 Rev 3 — Supplemental Review Procedure  ·  Effective 02/10/2025  ·  Alleniom Utilities — Standards EngineeringPage 3<br>\n(a)  Feeder AMI interval data — from the Alleniom AMI Data Warehouse, at the meter aggregation level<br>\npublished for the subject feeder. AMI interval data is the sole authoritative source for the Screen D<br>\nminimum-load determination described in §4.<br>\n(b)  Distribution planning record — most recent feeder planning study, conductor and transformer impedance<br>\ndata, and protection coordination settings, held by Distribution Planning.<br>\n(c)  DER queue — active-queue and on-hold registers maintained by the DER Program Office as of the screen<br>\nevaluation date. Queue entries not yet reflected in a published hosting-capacity map remain applicable to Screen<br>\nA.<br>\n(d)  Certified Equipment List (CEL) — current edition, for verification that proposed inverters and PCS<br>\nequipment are eligible to be considered under the screens.<br>\n(e)  Interconnection application record — the applicant's filed capacity, single-line diagram, inverter<br>\nspecifications, and control narrative (where controlled-capacity operation is asserted under DIS-01 §3.6).<br>\nSubstitution of a source not listed above shall be documented in the memo produced under §6, together with the<br>\nreason for the substitution.<br>\n§6   Documentation<br>\nThe reviewing engineer shall produce a supplemental review memo for each application processed under this<br>\nprocedure. The memo shall contain, at minimum:<br>\n(a)  application identifier, applicant, subject feeder, and requested capacity;<br>\n(b)  the screens applied and the pass/fail determination for each;<br>\n(c)  for Screen D, the daytime-window minimum load determined per §4, the data window used, the count of<br>\nintervals evaluated, and any interval exclusions with reason;<br>\n(d)  the recommended disposition — advance without study agreement, advance to study agreement, or return to<br>\napplicant with specified deficiency;<br>\n(e)  reviewer signature and date; and<br>\n(f)  the identifiers of any earlier supplemental review memos superseded.<br>\nThe memo shall be filed to the interconnection application record within five (5) business days of screen<br>\ncompletion. Where a memo supersedes an earlier determination on the same application or the same feeder, the<br>\nsuperseded memo shall be flagged in the file and a cross-reference to the superseding memo added to its cover.<br>\nAlleniom Utilities — DER Program Office · 4200 Veldmere Boulevard, Halston, Alleron 01HA · (612) 555-0100 · alleniom.com</p>	[]	markdown	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	markdown	en	9	9	{}
47	der-program/standards/superseded/cel-2024-q4-certified-equipment-list	fe152e2ed6f69bf6eb39	CEL-2024-Q4 Certified Equipment List	Prior Certified Equipment List published 10/31/2024, superseded by CEL-2025-Q2	f	t				# CEL-2024-Q4 Certified Equipment List\n\n## Front Matter\n\n| Alleniom Utilities — Certified Equipment List |   |\n| --- | --- |\n| Edition: CEL-2024-Q4  ·  Published 10/31/2024  ·  SUPERSEDED 04/30/2025 by CEL-2025-Q2. |   |\n| PCS listing basis: UL 1741-SB / UL 3141. |   |\n| Doc no. | CEL-2024-Q4 |\n| Revision | Q4-2024 |\n| Effective date | 10/31/2024 |\n| Superseded on | 04/30/2025 by CEL-2025-Q2 |\n| Owner | Standards Engineering (Evan Solberg) |\n| Approved by | Manager, Standards Engineering |\n| Distribution | DER Program Office; Interconnection Studies; DER Planning Group; SCADA/EMS Engineering; Commissioning; Regulatory Affairs; contracted developers on request |\n| Next scheduled edition | CEL-2025-Q1 (planned 01/31/2025) |\n| Cross-reference — DIS-01 Rev 4 §3.6 (Controlled-capacity recognition) |   |\n| A limit below nameplate is recognized only with a valid UL 1741 / UL 3141 PCS certification plus an approved Control Narrative — sufficient in themselves for recognition. Where the site's arrangement includes utility equipment, the recognized limit is enforced as applicable: telemetry monitoring, and on pure-generation sites the POI recloser's ANSI 32 directional-power element set to the recognized limit and tripping on violation. |   |\n| How to use this list |   |\n| The Inverters tab lists smart-inverter models with a valid UL 1741-SB certification of record as of the edition date, together with quarterly-addendum listings whose certification became final during the edition's active period. The Power Control Systems tab lists UL 3141-certified PCS assemblies eligible to satisfy the controlled-capacity recognition path under DIS-01 §3.6. Listing on this edition does not by itself authorize interconnection; project-specific screening, protection, and commissioning requirements continue to apply. Models delisted, discontinued, or with certifications not renewed between quarterly editions are removed from succeeding editions; historical records are retained by Standards Engineering. |   |\n| Change summary since CEL-2024-Q3 |   |\n| — 9 inverter models added on new UL 1741-SB certifications received 08/2024–10/2024. |   |\n| — 2 inverter models removed (manufacturer discontinued line). |   |\n| — Selvix SV-720 added on receipt of UL 1741-SB certification (initial listing). |   |\n| — Quenda Controls QC-PCS-500 and QC-PCS-1000 added on UL 3141 certification (initial PCS listings). |   |\n| Contact | Evan Solberg, Standards Engineering — evan.solberg@alleniom.com · (612) 555-0113 |\n\n## Inverters\n\n| Manufacturer | Model | Rated kW | Listing Basis | Effective Date | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Solmery Power | SM-60K-3P | 60 | UL 1741-SB | 03/14/2024 | Three-phase string inverter. |\n| Solmery Power | SM-100K-3P | 100 | UL 1741-SB | 03/14/2024 | Three-phase string inverter. |\n| Solmery Power | SM-125K-3P | 125 | UL 1741-SB | 03/14/2024 | Three-phase string inverter. |\n| Solmery Power | SM-160K-3P | 160 | UL 1741-SB | 07/22/2024 | — |\n| Solmery Power | SM-215K-3P | 215 | UL 1741-SB | 11/18/2024 | Q4 addendum listing; certification finalized post-publication. |\n| Solmery Power | SM-245K-3P | 245 | UL 1741-SB | 01/27/2025 | Q4 addendum listing; certification finalized during edition period. |\n| Torvane Electric | TE-33K | 33 | UL 1741-SB | 11/05/2024 | Q4 addendum listing. |\n| Torvane Electric | TE-75K | 75 | UL 1741 | 08/18/2023 | Non-SB certification of record; SB recertification pending. |\n| Torvane Electric | TE-100K | 100 | UL 1741-SB | 11/05/2024 | Q4 addendum listing. |\n| Torvane Electric | TE-125K | 125 | UL 1741-SB | 11/05/2024 | Q4 addendum listing. |\n| Torvane Electric | TE-180K | 180 | UL 1741-SB | 12/09/2024 | Q4 addendum listing. |\n| Vexlar Energy | VX-50C | 50 | UL 1741-SB | 06/02/2024 | — |\n| Vexlar Energy | VX-90C | 90 | UL 1741-SB | 06/02/2024 | — |\n| Vexlar Energy | VX-140C | 140 | UL 1741-SB | 09/16/2024 | — |\n| Vexlar Energy | VX-200C | 200 | UL 1741-SB | 06/02/2024 | — |\n| Vexlar Energy | VX-250C | 250 | UL 1741-SB | 06/02/2024 | — |\n| Halbrick Solar | HB-50 | 50 | UL 1741-SB | 04/19/2024 | Single-phase 208 V. |\n| Halbrick Solar | HB-125 | 125 | UL 1741-SB | 04/19/2024 | — |\n| Halbrick Solar | HB-175 | 175 | UL 1741-SB | 04/19/2024 | — |\n| Halbrick Solar | HB-250 | 250 | UL 1741-SB | 08/07/2024 | — |\n| Fenwyck Power | FW-Grid 100 | 100 | UL 1741-SB | 05/23/2024 | — |\n| Fenwyck Power | FW-Grid 175 | 175 | UL 1741-SB | 10/14/2024 | — |\n| Ostreme Electric | OE-3P-60 | 60 | UL 1741-SB | 05/06/2024 | — |\n| Ostreme Electric | OE-3P-150 | 150 | UL 1741-SB | 05/06/2024 | — |\n| Nordreem Grid | NG-XT-125 | 125 | UL 1741-SB | 08/26/2024 | — |\n| Cerlant PV | CL-208-100 | 100 | UL 1741-SB | 07/08/2024 | Low-voltage commercial. |\n| Cerlant PV | CL-480-250 | 250 | UL 1741-SB | 07/08/2024 | 480 V commercial three-phase. |\n| Selvix SV-720 | SV-720 | 720 | UL 1741-SB | 10/31/2024 | Initial listing this edition; central inverter, ≥500 kW class. |\n\n## Power Control Systems\n\n| Manufacturer | Model | Rated kW | Listing Basis | Effective Date | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Quenda Controls | QC-PCS-500 | 500 | UL 3141 | 09/27/2024 | Initial listing this edition. |\n| Quenda Controls | QC-PCS-1000 | 1000 | UL 3141 | 09/27/2024 | Initial listing this edition. |\n| Delrix Grid Sciences | DGS-PCS-2000 | 2000 | UL 3141 | 12/04/2024 | Q4 addendum listing; medium-scale central PCS. |\n\n## Notes and Cross-References\n\n| Item | Reference |\n| --- | --- |\n| Controlled-capacity recognition | DIS-01 Rev 4 §3.6 — restated verbatim in Front Matter. |\n| Telemetry & communications for ≥500 kW | TCS-02 §5. |\n| Protective device requirements at export-capable POI | DPS-07 Rev 2 §4.2. |\n| Standards conflict routing | ERP-9 §3 (Form ER-11). |\n| Witness test & waiver eligibility | Witness Test & Waiver Procedure (Standards Engineering) — eligibility requires the installed inverter to appear on the CURRENT CEL edition. |\n| Successor edition | CEL-2025-Q2 (published 04/30/2025) supersedes this edition. |\n| Listing removals | Models delisted between editions are removed from successor tabs without further notation; historical certification records are retained by Standards Engineering. |	<h1 id="cel-2024-q4-certified-equipment-list">CEL-2024-Q4 Certified Equipment List</h1>\n<h2 id="front-matter">Front Matter</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Alleniom Utilities — Certified Equipment List</th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Edition: CEL-2024-Q4  ·  Published 10/31/2024  ·  SUPERSEDED 04/30/2025 by CEL-2025-Q2.</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PCS listing basis: UL 1741-SB / UL 3141.</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Doc no.</td>\n<td>CEL-2024-Q4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Revision</td>\n<td>Q4-2024</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Effective date</td>\n<td>10/31/2024</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Superseded on</td>\n<td>04/30/2025 by CEL-2025-Q2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Owner</td>\n<td>Standards Engineering (Evan Solberg)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Approved by</td>\n<td>Manager, Standards Engineering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distribution</td>\n<td>DER Program Office; Interconnection Studies; DER Planning Group; SCADA/EMS Engineering; Commissioning; Regulatory Affairs; contracted developers on request</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Next scheduled edition</td>\n<td>CEL-2025-Q1 (planned 01/31/2025)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cross-reference — DIS-01 Rev 4 §3.6 (Controlled-capacity recognition)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A limit below nameplate is recognized only with a valid UL 1741 / UL 3141 PCS certification plus an approved Control Narrative — sufficient in themselves for recognition. Where the site's arrangement includes utility equipment, the recognized limit is enforced as applicable: telemetry monitoring, and on pure-generation sites the POI recloser's ANSI 32 directional-power element set to the recognized limit and tripping on violation.</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How to use this list</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The Inverters tab lists smart-inverter models with a valid UL 1741-SB certification of record as of the edition date, together with quarterly-addendum listings whose certification became final during the edition's active period. The Power Control Systems tab lists UL 3141-certified PCS assemblies eligible to satisfy the controlled-capacity recognition path under DIS-01 §3.6. Listing on this edition does not by itself authorize interconnection; project-specific screening, protection, and commissioning requirements continue to apply. Models delisted, discontinued, or with certifications not renewed between quarterly editions are removed from succeeding editions; historical records are retained by Standards Engineering.</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Change summary since CEL-2024-Q3</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>— 9 inverter models added on new UL 1741-SB certifications received 08/2024–10/2024.</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>— 2 inverter models removed (manufacturer discontinued line).</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>— Selvix SV-720 added on receipt of UL 1741-SB certification (initial listing).</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>— Quenda Controls QC-PCS-500 and QC-PCS-1000 added on UL 3141 certification (initial PCS listings).</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Contact</td>\n<td>Evan Solberg, Standards Engineering — evan.solberg@alleniom.com · (612) 555-0113</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id="inverters">Inverters</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Manufacturer</th>\n<th>Model</th>\n<th>Rated kW</th>\n<th>Listing Basis</th>\n<th>Effective Date</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Solmery Power</td>\n<td>SM-60K-3P</td>\n<td>60</td>\n<td>UL 1741-SB</td>\n<td>03/14/2024</td>\n<td>Three-phase string inverter.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Solmery Power</td>\n<td>SM-100K-3P</td>\n<td>100</td>\n<td>UL 1741-SB</td>\n<td>03/14/2024</td>\n<td>Three-phase string inverter.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Solmery Power</td>\n<td>SM-125K-3P</td>\n<td>125</td>\n<td>UL 1741-SB</td>\n<td>03/14/2024</td>\n<td>Three-phase string inverter.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Solmery Power</td>\n<td>SM-160K-3P</td>\n<td>160</td>\n<td>UL 1741-SB</td>\n<td>07/22/2024</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Solmery Power</td>\n<td>SM-215K-3P</td>\n<td>215</td>\n<td>UL 1741-SB</td>\n<td>11/18/2024</td>\n<td>Q4 addendum listing; certification finalized post-publication.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Solmery Power</td>\n<td>SM-245K-3P</td>\n<td>245</td>\n<td>UL 1741-SB</td>\n<td>01/27/2025</td>\n<td>Q4 addendum listing; certification finalized during edition period.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Torvane Electric</td>\n<td>TE-33K</td>\n<td>33</td>\n<td>UL 1741-SB</td>\n<td>11/05/2024</td>\n<td>Q4 addendum listing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Torvane Electric</td>\n<td>TE-75K</td>\n<td>75</td>\n<td>UL 1741</td>\n<td>08/18/2023</td>\n<td>Non-SB certification of record; SB recertification pending.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Torvane Electric</td>\n<td>TE-100K</td>\n<td>100</td>\n<td>UL 1741-SB</td>\n<td>11/05/2024</td>\n<td>Q4 addendum listing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Torvane Electric</td>\n<td>TE-125K</td>\n<td>125</td>\n<td>UL 1741-SB</td>\n<td>11/05/2024</td>\n<td>Q4 addendum listing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Torvane Electric</td>\n<td>TE-180K</td>\n<td>180</td>\n<td>UL 1741-SB</td>\n<td>12/09/2024</td>\n<td>Q4 addendum listing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vexlar Energy</td>\n<td>VX-50C</td>\n<td>50</td>\n<td>UL 1741-SB</td>\n<td>06/02/2024</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vexlar Energy</td>\n<td>VX-90C</td>\n<td>90</td>\n<td>UL 1741-SB</td>\n<td>06/02/2024</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vexlar Energy</td>\n<td>VX-140C</td>\n<td>140</td>\n<td>UL 1741-SB</td>\n<td>09/16/2024</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vexlar Energy</td>\n<td>VX-20