Basis</th>\n<th>Effective Date</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Quenda Controls</td>\n<td>QC-PCS-500</td>\n<td>500</td>\n<td>UL 3141</td>\n<td>09/27/2024</td>\n<td>Initial listing this edition.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quenda Controls</td>\n<td>QC-PCS-1000</td>\n<td>1000</td>\n<td>UL 3141</td>\n<td>09/27/2024</td>\n<td>Initial listing this edition.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delrix Grid Sciences</td>\n<td>DGS-PCS-2000</td>\n<td>2000</td>\n<td>UL 3141</td>\n<td>12/04/2024</td>\n<td>Q4 addendum listing; medium-scale central PCS.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id="notes-and-cross-references">Notes and Cross-References</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Reference</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Controlled-capacity recognition</td>\n<td>DIS-01 Rev 4 §3.6 — restated verbatim in Front Matter.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Telemetry &amp; communications for ≥500 kW</td>\n<td>TCS-02 §5.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Protective device requirements at export-capable POI</td>\n<td>DPS-07 Rev 2 §4.2.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standards conflict routing</td>\n<td>ERP-9 §3 (Form ER-11).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Witness test &amp; waiver eligibility</td>\n<td>Witness Test &amp; Waiver Procedure (Standards Engineering) — eligibility requires the installed inverter to appear on the CURRENT CEL edition.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Successor edition</td>\n<td>CEL-2025-Q2 (published 04/30/2025) supersedes this edition.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Listing removals</td>\n<td>Models delisted between editions are removed from successor tabs without further notation; historical certification records are retained by Standards Engineering.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>	[]	markdown	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	markdown	en	9	9	{}
48	der-program/standards/tcs-02-telemetry-comms-standard	d822db425cb2956d5df0	TCS-02 Telemetry Comms Standard	Telemetry and Communications Standard effective 06/01/2021 requiring RTU and SCADA for sites 500 kW and above	f	t				# TCS-02 Telemetry Comms Standard\n\nTCS-02  Rev 1  |  Effective 06/01/2021Alleniom Utilities  |  Standards Engineering\nPage 1Telemetry & Communications Standard TCS-02\nALLENIOM UTILITIES — STANDARDS ENGINEERING\nTELEMETRY & COMMUNICATIONS STANDARD\nDoc no. Rev Date Owner\nTCS-02 Rev 1 06/01/2021 Alleniom Standards Engineering\nDocument title: Telemetry & Communications Standard\nApplies to: Distributed Energy Resource (DER) facilities interconnected to the Alleniom Utilities\ndistribution system within the DER Program scope\nEffective date: 06/01/2021\nApproving authority: Manager, Standards Engineering\n§1   PURPOSE\nThis standard establishes the minimum technical and operational requirements for real-time\ntelemetry and communications between in-scope Distributed Energy Resource (DER) facilities\nand the Alleniom Utilities System Operations Center (SOC). It governs the equipment, data\ncontent, refresh cadence, communications transport, and cybersecurity posture required for a\nDER facility to be observed and dispatchable through the utility's Supervisory Control and Data\nAcquisition (SCADA) system.\nTCS-02 is one component of Alleniom's DER interconnection framework. Its provisions are limited\nto telemetry and communications. Requirements for interconnection classification, protective\nfunctions, service agreements, commissioning, and other lifecycle activities are addressed in the\nstandards and procedures cited by the interconnection standard (DIS-01) and are outside the\nscope of this document.\n§2   SCOPE\nTCS-02 applies to every in-scope DER site as defined by DIS-01 §3 — that is, every DER whose\nnameplate capacity meets or exceeds the DER Program threshold of 500 kW. Sub-500 kW DER\nhandled under the Simplified Small-Systems Process is outside the scope of this standard.\nTCS-02 applies to:\n a.   Commercial small-scale DER (500 kW – 1 MW).\n b.   Medium-scale DER (1 MW – 5 MW).\n c.   Aggregated DER facilities recognized as a single site under DIS-01 §3.4, where the\naggregate capacity meets or exceeds the DER Program threshold.\n d.   DER commissioned on or after the effective date of this standard, and DER\ncommissioned before the effective date that undergo material modification, POI relocation, or\ncapacity change requiring a new interconnection application.\n4200 Veldmere Boulevard, Halston, Alleron 01HA  ·  (612) 555-0100  ·  alleniom.com\n\n---\n\nTCS-02  Rev 1  |  Effective 06/01/2021Alleniom Utilities  |  Standards Engineering\nPage 2Telemetry & Communications Standard TCS-02\nWhere a DER site interfaces with the distribution system through a shared point of common\ncoupling with other generation, the aggregate facility is the object of this standard, and the\naggregate is measured and controlled as a single interconnected entity.\n§3   RTU REQUIREMENTS\n§3.1   Ownership and installation\nEach in-scope DER site shall be equipped with a utility-owned Remote Terminal Unit (RTU)\ninstalled at, or immediately adjacent to, the point of interconnection. The RTU shall be procured,\nowned, installed, configured, maintained, and — where required — replaced by Alleniom Utilities.\nThe customer shall provide the physical mounting location, environmental enclosure, station AC\nservice, and communications entrance conduit specified in the utility's site-specific RTU\ninstallation drawing.\n§3.3   Functional requirements\nEach RTU shall, at minimum:\n a.   Poll the DER facility's generation metering, inverter/PCS gateway, and station\ninstrumentation at the refresh cadence specified in §6.\n b.   Encode and forward the required data points (§6) to the Alleniom SCADA front-end\nprocessors over an approved communications path (§4).\n c.   Timestamp all values to a common reference (§3.5) and buffer data locally for a minimum\nof seventy-two (72) hours in the event of communications loss, with automatic backfill upon\nrestoration.\n d.   Report communications status, device health, and configuration version to SCADA as\ndiagnostic points.\n e.   Accept authenticated remote configuration updates and firmware from Alleniom\nSCADA/EMS Engineering only.\n§3.4   Environmental and station-service requirements\nThe RTU enclosure shall meet the environmental rating specified in the site-specific installation\ndrawing. Station AC service to the enclosure shall be provided by the customer through a\ndedicated branch circuit backed by an uninterruptible supply sized for a minimum four (4) hour\noutage ride-through, sufficient to maintain telemetry through short-duration station outages.\n§3.5   Time synchronization\nThe RTU shall discipline its internal clock to an authoritative time source using either GNSS (GPS)\ntime or the Alleniom SCADA network time reference. All values reported to SCADA shall carry a\ntimestamp accurate to within ±1 second of UTC.\n§3.6   Commissioning and end-to-end verification\n§3.2   Approved models\nThe utility-owned RTU shall be one of the three models currently used for utility-owned RTU\nservice: YOT-8963, YOT-8856, or YOT-7963. Substitution is not permitted. A customer power\nplant controller (PPC) may use any RTU on the plant side, provided it can communicate with the\nutility-owned RTU via DNP3 (§4.2).\n\n---\n\nTCS-02  Rev 1  |  Effective 06/01/2021\nAlleniom Utilities  |  Standards Engineering\nPage 3\nTelemetry & Communications Standard\nTCS-02\nPrior to the RTU being accepted as commissioned, Alleniom shall perform an end-to-end\npoint-to-point test that verifies each required data point (§6) at the SCADA operator display,\nexercises any control points released to SCADA, and validates alarm and quality flags.\nCommissioning of the RTU is a prerequisite to the telemetry condition stated in §5.\n§4   COMMUNICATIONS\n§4.1   Communications transport\nThe RTU shall communicate with Alleniom SCADA over one of the transports approved by\nSCADA/EMS Engineering:\n a.   Utility-managed fiber to an Alleniom aggregation node, or\n b.   Utility-provisioned licensed private wireless (point-to-multipoint), or\n c.   Utility-provisioned cellular service on the approved carrier list, using SIM credentials held\nby Alleniom.\nCustomer-owned or shared-use networks are not permitted as the primary telemetry transport.\nWhere site conditions do not permit any of the transports listed above, the site is not eligible for\ninterconnection under the DER Program until a suitable transport can be provisioned.\n§4.2   SCADA protocol\nThe RTU shall communicate with SCADA using DNP3 Serial or DNP3 over TCP/IP. Secure\nAuthentication v5 shall be enabled on the DNP3 link where supported by the RTU. Configuration\nparameters — DNP3 addresses, timeouts, unsolicited response classes, integrity poll intervals —\nshall follow the site-specific configuration record issued by SCADA/EMS Engineering.\n§4.3   Availability\nThe end-to-end telemetry path (RTU, transport, front-end processor) shall be designed for a\nmonthly availability of not less than 99.5 percent, measured at the SCADA front-end. Persistent\nloss of telemetry constitutes a departure from the site's interconnection basis and shall be\ncorrected by the customer and utility working together to the extent each owns the affected\nequipment.\n§4.4   Address and IP management\nAll IP addresses, DNP3 addresses, VLAN assignments, and routing between the RTU and\nAlleniom SCADA are assigned and managed by Alleniom. Customer equipment shall not initiate\nconnections toward the RTU other than as expressly permitted in the site-specific configuration\nrecord.\n§5   TELEMETRY CONDITION FOR PERMISSION TO OPERATE\n§5.   ≥500 kW requires utility-owned RTU + SCADA telemetry; no PTO without live telemetry.\nThis requirement applies to all in-scope DER sites, including aggregated facilities under\nDIS-01 §3.4.\nLive telemetry, for the purposes of §5, means that the RTU installed under §3 is:\n a.   Physically installed, configured, and end-to-end commissioned in accordance with §3.6;\n\n\n---\n\nTCS-02  Rev 1  |  Effective 06/01/2021\nAlleniom Utilities  |  Standards Engineering\nPage 4\nTelemetry & Communications Standard\nTCS-02\n b.   Communicating with Alleniom SCADA over an approved transport (§4) at the refresh\ncadence required by §6; and\n c.   Presenting all required data points (§6) as valid (not stale, not forced, not flagged\nbad-quality) at the SCADA operator display at the moment Permission to Operate (PTO) is\nissued.\nCompliance with §5 is confirmed by the SOC Shift Supervisor on shift at the time PTO is\nrequested. The SOC's confirmation is recorded against the interconnection file. PTO shall not be\nissued to any in-scope DER site until §5 is satisfied.\n§6   DATA POINTS AND REFRESH RATES\n§6.1   Required data points\nEach in-scope DER site shall present, at minimum, the following analog and status points to\nAlleniom SCADA. Where the site's arrangement includes multiple inverter blocks or multiple\naggregated facilities behind a common POI, values shall be reported both individually per block\nand as a facility total.\nAnalog values (per site, and per block where applicable):\nPoint\nUnits\nSign convention\nReal power, net export at POI\nkW\nExport positive\nReactive power, net at POI\nkVAr\nAbsorbing positive\nPOI voltage, per phase\nV\n—\nPOI current, per phase\nA\n—\nFrequency at POI\nHz\n—\nGross generation (per inverter block)\nkW\nGeneration positive\nAuxiliary/parasitic load (where separately metered)\nkW\nLoad positive\nCumulative energy delivered\nkWh\nExport positive\nCumulative energy received\nkWh\nImport positive\nAmbient / cabinet temperature (RTU health)\n°C\n—\nStatus and diagnostic points:\nPoint\nDescription\nSite online / offline\nAggregate operating state of the DER\nInverter/PCS block status\nPer block: run, standby, fault\nCommunications heartbeat\nRTU-to-SCADA path health\nTime-sync quality\nGNSS/NTP discipline status\nConfiguration version\nActive RTU configuration identifier\nAlarm summary\nRolled-up device alarm state\n§6.2   Refresh rates\n\n\n---\n\nTCS-02  Rev 1  |  Effective 06/01/2021\nAlleniom Utilities  |  Standards Engineering\nPage 5\nTelemetry & Communications Standard\nTCS-02\nAnalog values shall be updated to SCADA at intervals no longer than four (4) seconds using DNP3\nClass 1/2 event reporting for point-on-change and Class 0 integrity poll no less frequently than\nevery sixty (60) seconds. Status points shall be reported by exception on change of state, with an\nintegrity poll no less frequently than every sixty (60) seconds. Cumulative energy registers shall be\nreported no less frequently than every five (5) minutes.\n§6.3   Data quality\nEvery value delivered to SCADA shall carry a DNP3 quality flag. Values shall be marked online,\nrestart, communications lost, or over-range as circumstances warrant. Values held constant by the\nRTU during communications loss shall not be reported as online upon restoration; they shall be\nreported with the communications-lost flag until fresh source values are acquired.\n§6.4   Retention\nAlleniom retains all telemetry from in-scope DER sites in the historian for a minimum of thirty-six\n(36) months at the raw acquisition cadence, and indefinitely at hourly aggregation, for planning,\nengineering analysis, and regulatory reporting.\n§7   CYBERSECURITY\n§7.1   Applicability\nThe RTU, the communications transport between the RTU and Alleniom SCADA, and any\ncustomer equipment that shares a physical or logical interface with the RTU are subject to this\nsection. Alleniom-owned assets covered here are administered under Alleniom's cyber-security\nprogram consistent with NIST SP 800-82 and NERC CIP where applicable.\n§7.2   Access control\nLocal and remote access to the RTU is limited to Alleniom SCADA/EMS Engineering and to\nAlleniom-authorized service personnel. Local craft access requires two-factor authentication\nagainst Alleniom's identity system. Passwords, shared secrets, and cryptographic keys are\nmanaged by Alleniom and are not disclosed to the customer, developer, or any third party.\n§7.3   Communications security\nDNP3 Secure Authentication v5 shall be enabled where supported by the RTU. All\ncommunications traversing any non-utility segment shall be tunneled through an\nAlleniom-managed VPN using IPsec (IKEv2) with mutual certificate authentication. Certificates\nshall have a maximum validity of thirteen (13) months and are rotated by Alleniom.\n§7.4   Firmware, configuration, and change management\nRTU firmware and configuration are updated only by Alleniom SCADA/EMS Engineering, following\nthe Alleniom change-management process. Firmware images shall be verified against a\ncryptographic signature prior to installation. The customer shall not attempt to modify, upgrade, or\nreconfigure the RTU or the utility-owned communications equipment.\n§7.5   Logging and monitoring\nThe RTU shall log authentication events, configuration changes, communications state transitions,\nand firmware operations. Logs are forwarded to Alleniom's centralized log-management system.\nAnomalies detected by that system are handled per Alleniom's cyber-security incident response\nprocedure.\n\n\n---\n\nTCS-02  Rev 1  |  Effective 06/01/2021\nAlleniom Utilities  |  Standards Engineering\nPage 6\nTelemetry & Communications Standard\nTCS-02\n§7.6   Physical security\nThe RTU shall be housed in a locked, tamper-evident enclosure. Enclosure keys, cores, and any\nphysical access tokens are held by Alleniom. Physical intrusion of the enclosure shall generate a\nstatus point (tamper alarm) reported to SCADA under §6.\n§7.7   Incident response\nThe customer shall report, without delay, any known or suspected compromise of the RTU, its\nenclosure, its communications transport, or any interface between customer equipment and\nutility-owned telemetry equipment. Reports shall be directed to the Alleniom SOC and to\nSCADA/EMS Engineering.\n§7.8   Decommissioning\nAt end of life, the RTU and its cryptographic material are returned to Alleniom for secure disposal.\nCustomer equipment previously interfaced to the RTU shall be sanitized of any Alleniom-provided\ncredentials before it is repurposed or disposed of.\nEnd of TCS-02.	<h1 id="tcs-02-telemetry-comms-standard">TCS-02 Telemetry Comms Standard</h1>\n<p>TCS-02  Rev 1  |  Effective 06/01/2021Alleniom Utilities  |  Standards Engineering<br>\nPage 1Telemetry &amp; Communications Standard TCS-02<br>\nALLENIOM UTILITIES — STANDARDS ENGINEERING<br>\nTELEMETRY &amp; COMMUNICATIONS STANDARD<br>\nDoc no. Rev Date Owner<br>\nTCS-02 Rev 1 06/01/2021 Alleniom Standards Engineering<br>\nDocument title: Telemetry &amp; Communications Standard<br>\nApplies to: Distributed Energy Resource (DER) facilities interconnected to the Alleniom Utilities<br>\ndistribution system within the DER Program scope<br>\nEffective date: 06/01/2021<br>\nApproving authority: Manager, Standards Engineering<br>\n§1   PURPOSE<br>\nThis standard establishes the minimum technical and operational requirements for real-time<br>\ntelemetry and communications between in-scope Distributed Energy Resource (DER) facilities<br>\nand the Alleniom Utilities System Operations Center (SOC). It governs the equipment, data<br>\ncontent, refresh cadence, communications transport, and cybersecurity posture required for a<br>\nDER facility to be observed and dispatchable through the utility's Supervisory Control and Data<br>\nAcquisition (SCADA) system.<br>\nTCS-02 is one component of Alleniom's DER interconnection framework. Its provisions are limited<br>\nto telemetry and communications. Requirements for interconnection classification, protective<br>\nfunctions, service agreements, commissioning, and other lifecycle activities are addressed in the<br>\nstandards and procedures cited by the interconnection standard (DIS-01) and are outside the<br>\nscope of this document.<br>\n§2   SCOPE<br>\nTCS-02 applies to every in-scope DER site as defined by DIS-01 §3 — that is, every DER whose<br>\nnameplate capacity meets or exceeds the DER Program threshold of 500 kW. Sub-500 kW DER<br>\nhandled under the Simplified Small-Systems Process is outside the scope of this standard.<br>\nTCS-02 applies to:<br>\n a.   Commercial small-scale DER (500 kW – 1 MW).<br>\n b.   Medium-scale DER (1 MW – 5 MW).<br>\n c.   Aggregated DER facilities recognized as a single site under DIS-01 §3.4, where the<br>\naggregate capacity meets or exceeds the DER Program threshold.<br>\n d.   DER commissioned on or after the effective date of this standard, and DER<br>\ncommissioned before the effective date that undergo material modification, POI relocation, or<br>\ncapacity change requiring a new interconnection application.<br>\n4200 Veldmere Boulevard, Halston, Alleron 01HA  ·  (612) 555-0100  ·  alleniom.com</p>\n<hr>\n<p>TCS-02  Rev 1  |  Effective 06/01/2021Alleniom Utilities  |  Standards Engineering<br>\nPage 2Telemetry &amp; Communications Standard TCS-02<br>\nWhere a DER site interfaces with the distribution system through a shared point of common<br>\ncoupling with other generation, the aggregate facility is the object of this standard, and the<br>\naggregate is measured and controlled as a single interconnected entity.<br>\n§3   RTU REQUIREMENTS<br>\n§3.1   Ownership and installation<br>\nEach in-scope DER site shall be equipped with a utility-owned Remote Terminal Unit (RTU)<br>\ninstalled at, or immediately adjacent to, the point of interconnection. The RTU shall be procured,<br>\nowned, installed, configured, maintained, and — where required — replaced by Alleniom Utilities.<br>\nThe customer shall provide the physical mounting location, environmental enclosure, station AC<br>\nservice, and communications entrance conduit specified in the utility's site-specific RTU<br>\ninstallation drawing.<br>\n§3.3   Functional requirements<br>\nEach RTU shall, at minimum:<br>\n a.   Poll the DER facility's generation metering, inverter/PCS gateway, and station<br>\ninstrumentation at the refresh cadence specified in §6.<br>\n b.   Encode and forward the required data points (§6) to the Alleniom SCADA front-end<br>\nprocessors over an approved communications path (§4).<br>\n c.   Timestamp all values to a common reference (§3.5) and buffer data locally for a minimum<br>\nof seventy-two (72) hours in the event of communications loss, with automatic backfill upon<br>\nrestoration.<br>\n d.   Report communications status, device health, and configuration version to SCADA as<br>\ndiagnostic points.<br>\n e.   Accept authenticated remote configuration updates and firmware from Alleniom<br>\nSCADA/EMS Engineering only.<br>\n§3.4   Environmental and station-service requirements<br>\nThe RTU enclosure shall meet the environmental rating specified in the site-specific installation<br>\ndrawing. Station AC service to the enclosure shall be provided by the customer through a<br>\ndedicated branch circuit backed by an uninterruptible supply sized for a minimum four (4) hour<br>\noutage ride-through, sufficient to maintain telemetry through short-duration station outages.<br>\n§3.5   Time synchronization<br>\nThe RTU shall discipline its internal clock to an authoritative time source using either GNSS (GPS)<br>\ntime or the Alleniom SCADA network time reference. All values reported to SCADA shall carry a<br>\ntimestamp accurate to within ±1 second of UTC.<br>\n§3.6   Commissioning and end-to-end verification<br>\n§3.2   Approved models<br>\nThe utility-owned RTU shall be one of the three models currently used for utility-owned RTU<br>\nservice: YOT-8963, YOT-8856, or YOT-7963. Substitution is not permitted. A customer power<br>\nplant controller (PPC) may use any RTU on the plant side, provided it can communicate with the<br>\nutility-owned RTU via DNP3 (§4.2).</p>\n<hr>\n<p>TCS-02  Rev 1  |  Effective 06/01/2021<br>\nAlleniom Utilities  |  Standards Engineering<br>\nPage 3<br>\nTelemetry &amp; Communications Standard<br>\nTCS-02<br>\nPrior to the RTU being accepted as commissioned, Alleniom shall perform an end-to-end<br>\npoint-to-point test that verifies each required data point (§6) at the SCADA operator display,<br>\nexercises any control points released to SCADA, and validates alarm and quality flags.<br>\nCommissioning of the RTU is a prerequisite to the telemetry condition stated in §5.<br>\n§4   COMMUNICATIONS<br>\n§4.1   Communications transport<br>\nThe RTU shall communicate with Alleniom SCADA over one of the transports approved by<br>\nSCADA/EMS Engineering:<br>\n a.   Utility-managed fiber to an Alleniom aggregation node, or<br>\n b.   Utility-provisioned licensed private wireless (point-to-multipoint), or<br>\n c.   Utility-provisioned cellular service on the approved carrier list, using SIM credentials held<br>\nby Alleniom.<br>\nCustomer-owned or shared-use networks are not permitted as the primary telemetry transport.<br>\nWhere site conditions do not permit any of the transports listed above, the site is not eligible for<br>\ninterconnection under the DER Program until a suitable transport can be provisioned.<br>\n§4.2   SCADA protocol<br>\nThe RTU shall communicate with SCADA using DNP3 Serial or DNP3 over TCP/IP. Secure<br>\nAuthentication v5 shall be enabled on the DNP3 link where supported by the RTU. Configuration<br>\nparameters — DNP3 addresses, timeouts, unsolicited response classes, integrity poll intervals —<br>\nshall follow the site-specific configuration record issued by SCADA/EMS Engineering.<br>\n§4.3   Availability<br>\nThe end-to-end telemetry path (RTU, transport, front-end processor) shall be designed for a<br>\nmonthly availability of not less than 99.5 percent, measured at the SCADA front-end. Persistent<br>\nloss of telemetry constitutes a departure from the site's interconnection basis and shall be<br>\ncorrected by the customer and utility working together to the extent each owns the affected<br>\nequipment.<br>\n§4.4   Address and IP management<br>\nAll IP addresses, DNP3 addresses, VLAN assignments, and routing between the RTU and<br>\nAlleniom SCADA are assigned and managed by Alleniom. Customer equipment shall not initiate<br>\nconnections toward the RTU other than as expressly permitted in the site-specific configuration<br>\nrecord.<br>\n§5   TELEMETRY CONDITION FOR PERMISSION TO OPERATE<br>\n§5.   ≥500 kW requires utility-owned RTU + SCADA telemetry; no PTO without live telemetry.<br>\nThis requirement applies to all in-scope DER sites, including aggregated facilities under<br>\nDIS-01 §3.4.<br>\nLive telemetry, for the purposes of §5, means that the RTU installed under §3 is:<br>\n a.   Physically installed, configured, and end-to-end commissioned in accordance with §3.6;</p>\n<hr>\n<p>TCS-02  Rev 1  |  Effective 06/01/2021<br>\nAlleniom Utilities  |  Standards Engineering<br>\nPage 4<br>\nTelemetry &amp; Communications Standard<br>\nTCS-02<br>\n b.   Communicating with Alleniom SCADA over an approved transport (§4) at the refresh<br>\ncadence required by §6; and<br>\n c.   Presenting all required data points (§6) as valid (not stale, not forced, not flagged<br>\nbad-quality) at the SCADA operator display at the moment Permission to Operate (PTO) is<br>\nissued.<br>\nCompliance with §5 is confirmed by the SOC Shift Supervisor on shift at the time PTO is<br>\nrequested. The SOC's confirmation is recorded against the interconnection file. PTO shall not be<br>\nissued to any in-scope DER site until §5 is satisfied.<br>\n§6   DATA POINTS AND REFRESH RATES<br>\n§6.1   Required data points<br>\nEach in-scope DER site shall present, at minimum, the following analog and status points to<br>\nAlleniom SCADA. Where the site's arrangement includes multiple inverter blocks or multiple<br>\naggregated facilities behind a common POI, values shall be reported both individually per block<br>\nand as a facility total.<br>\nAnalog values (per site, and per block where applicable):<br>\nPoint<br>\nUnits<br>\nSign convention<br>\nReal power, net export at POI<br>\nkW<br>\nExport positive<br>\nReactive power, net at POI<br>\nkVAr<br>\nAbsorbing positive<br>\nPOI voltage, per phase<br>\nV<br>\n—<br>\nPOI current, per phase<br>\nA<br>\n—<br>\nFrequency at POI<br>\nHz<br>\n—<br>\nGross generation (per inverter block)<br>\nkW<br>\nGeneration positive<br>\nAuxiliary/parasitic load (where separately metered)<br>\nkW<br>\nLoad positive<br>\nCumulative energy delivered<br>\nkWh<br>\nExport positive<br>\nCumulative energy received<br>\nkWh<br>\nImport positive<br>\nAmbient / cabinet temperature (RTU health)<br>\n°C<br>\n—<br>\nStatus and diagnostic points:<br>\nPoint<br>\nDescription<br>\nSite online / offline<br>\nAggregate operating state of the DER<br>\nInverter/PCS block status<br>\nPer block: run, standby, fault<br>\nCommunications heartbeat<br>\nRTU-to-SCADA path health<br>\nTime-sync quality<br>\nGNSS/NTP discipline status<br>\nConfiguration version<br>\nActive RTU configuration identifier<br>\nAlarm summary<br>\nRolled-up device alarm state<br>\n§6.2   Refresh rates</p>\n<hr>\n<p>TCS-02  Rev 1  |  Effective 06/01/2021<br>\nAlleniom Utilities  |  Standards Engineering<br>\nPage 5<br>\nTelemetry &amp; Communications Standard<br>\nTCS-02<br>\nAnalog values shall be updated to SCADA at intervals no longer than four (4) seconds using DNP3<br>\nClass 1/2 event reporting for point-on-change and Class 0 integrity poll no less frequently than<br>\nevery sixty (60) seconds. Status points shall be reported by exception on change of state, with an<br>\nintegrity poll no less frequently than every sixty (60) seconds. Cumulative energy registers shall be<br>\nreported no less frequently than every five (5) minutes.<br>\n§6.3   Data quality<br>\nEvery value delivered to SCADA shall carry a DNP3 quality flag. Values shall be marked online,<br>\nrestart, communications lost, or over-range as circumstances warrant. Values held constant by the<br>\nRTU during communications loss shall not be reported as online upon restoration; they shall be<br>\nreported with the communications-lost flag until fresh source values are acquired.<br>\n§6.4   Retention<br>\nAlleniom retains all telemetry from in-scope DER sites in the historian for a minimum of thirty-six<br>\n(36) months at the raw acquisition cadence, and indefinitely at hourly aggregation, for planning,<br>\nengineering analysis, and regulatory reporting.<br>\n§7   CYBERSECURITY<br>\n§7.1   Applicability<br>\nThe RTU, the communications transport between the RTU and Alleniom SCADA, and any<br>\ncustomer equipment that shares a physical or logical interface with the RTU are subject to this<br>\nsection. Alleniom-owned assets covered here are administered under Alleniom's cyber-security<br>\nprogram consistent with NIST SP 800-82 and NERC CIP where applicable.<br>\n§7.2   Access control<br>\nLocal and remote access to the RTU is limited to Alleniom SCADA/EMS Engineering and to<br>\nAlleniom-authorized service personnel. Local craft access requires two-factor authentication<br>\nagainst Alleniom's identity system. Passwords, shared secrets, and cryptographic keys are<br>\nmanaged by Alleniom and are not disclosed to the customer, developer, or any third party.<br>\n§7.3   Communications security<br>\nDNP3 Secure Authentication v5 shall be enabled where supported by the RTU. All<br>\ncommunications traversing any non-utility segment shall be tunneled through an<br>\nAlleniom-managed VPN using IPsec (IKEv2) with mutual certificate authentication. Certificates<br>\nshall have a maximum validity of thirteen (13) months and are rotated by Alleniom.<br>\n§7.4   Firmware, configuration, and change management<br>\nRTU firmware and configuration are updated only by Alleniom SCADA/EMS Engineering, following<br>\nthe Alleniom change-management process. Firmware images shall be verified against a<br>\ncryptographic signature prior to installation. The customer shall not attempt to modify, upgrade, or<br>\nreconfigure the RTU or the utility-owned communications equipment.<br>\n§7.5   Logging and monitoring<br>\nThe RTU shall log authentication events, configuration changes, communications state transitions,<br>\nand firmware operations. Logs are forwarded to Alleniom's centralized log-management system.<br>\nAnomalies detected by that system are handled per Alleniom's cyber-security incident response<br>\nprocedure.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>TCS-02  Rev 1  |  Effective 06/01/2021<br>\nAlleniom Utilities  |  Standards Engineering<br>\nPage 6<br>\nTelemetry &amp; Communications Standard<br>\nTCS-02<br>\n§7.6   Physical security<br>\nThe RTU shall be housed in a locked, tamper-evident enclosure. Enclosure keys, cores, and any<br>\nphysical access tokens are held by Alleniom. Physical intrusion of the enclosure shall generate a<br>\nstatus point (tamper alarm) reported to SCADA under §6.<br>\n§7.7   Incident response<br>\nThe customer shall report, without delay, any known or suspected compromise of the RTU, its<br>\nenclosure, its communications transport, or any interface between customer equipment and<br>\nutility-owned telemetry equipment. Reports shall be directed to the Alleniom SOC and to<br>\nSCADA/EMS Engineering.<br>\n§7.8   Decommissioning<br>\nAt end of life, the RTU and its cryptographic material are returned to Alleniom for secure disposal.<br>\nCustomer equipment previously interfaced to the RTU shall be sanitized of any Alleniom-provided<br>\ncredentials before it is repurposed or disposed of.<br>\nEnd of TCS-02.</p>	[]	markdown	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	markdown	en	9	9	{}
49	der-program/standards/tariff-excerpt-s12-interconnection	edee4809d8a1e31f5c59	Tariff Excerpt S12 Interconnection	Excerpt of APUC-filed tariff section 12 covering withdrawal, payment defaults, and queue-order processing	f	t				# Tariff Excerpt S12 Interconnection\n\nAPUC Tariff Filing — Alleniom Utilities Electric Tariff, Vol. III, §12 Page 1 of 6\nALLENIOM UTILITIES\nAPUC-Filed Interconnection Tariff\n§12 Interconnection Provisions\nFiled with the Alleron Public Utilities Commission\nElectric Tariff — Volume III · Original Sheet No. 214 · Effective: March 1, 2024\nSECTION 12 — INTERCONNECTION OF DISTRIBUTED ENERGY\nRESOURCES\n§12.1 Applicability\nThis section governs the interconnection to Alleniom Utilities' (the “Company”) distribution system\nof distributed energy resources (“DER”) within the Alleron service territory. Technical\nrequirements, screens, study procedures, and equipment standards referenced herein are set out\nin the Company's DER Interconnection Standard (DIS-01) and companion standards, each of\nwhich is incorporated by reference. Nothing in this section shall be construed to modify or\nsupersede any applicable safety, reliability, or protection requirement of the Company's\nengineering standards.\nFiled by: Alleniom Utilities · 4200 Veldmere Boulevard, Halston, Alleron 01HA\n\n---\n\nAlleniom Utilities — APUC-Filed Interconnection Tariff · §12 Interconnection\nVolume III · Sheet No. 214\nAPUC Tariff Filing — Alleniom Utilities Electric Tariff, Vol. III, §12\nPage 2 of 6\n§12.2 Scope of the DER Interconnection Program\nThe DER Interconnection Program administered under this section applies to applications for DER\ninterconnection at or above five hundred kilowatts (≥500 kW). Within the Program, DER shall be\nclassified according to the DER Interconnection Standard as follows:\n(a)  Commercial small-scale DER — five hundred kilowatts to one megawatt (500 kW – 1\nMW);\n(b)  Medium-scale DER — one megawatt to five megawatts (1 MW – 5 MW).\nThe Program scope threshold is ≥500 kW. DER below five hundred kilowatts (sub-500 kW),\nwhether commercial or residential, are not administered under this section and shall instead be\nprocessed under the Company's Simplified Small-Systems Process, which is filed separately\nand is outside the DER Interconnection Program.\nAggregation, controlled-capacity recognition, and shared point-of-interconnection eligibility are\naddressed in the DER Interconnection Standard and shall control the classification of any facility\nwhose nameplate rating, aggregate site rating, or point of common coupling brings it within\nProgram scope.\n\n\n---\n\nAlleniom Utilities — APUC-Filed Interconnection Tariff · §12 Interconnection\nVolume III · Sheet No. 214\nAPUC Tariff Filing — Alleniom Utilities Electric Tariff, Vol. III, §12\nPage 3 of 6\n§12.3 Application Intake and Queue Position\nA complete application shall establish the applicant's queue position as of the date the Company\ndetermines the application to be complete. Applications determined to be incomplete shall be\nreturned with a written deficiency notice identifying each item requiring cure and the date by which\ncure is required. Queue position is not transferable between applications except as expressly\nprovided in the DER Interconnection Standard.\n§12.4 Deemed Withdrawal Following Notice of Impending Withdrawal or Cancellation\nUpon issuance of a formal written Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation by the\nCompany, the applicant shall have thirty (30) business days from the date of the notice to submit\na substantive response curing the matter identified therein. If the applicant fails to respond within\nthirty (30) business days, the application shall be deemed withdrawn and shall be closed from the\nactive queue without further action of the Company.\nFor purposes of this section, “business days” shall exclude Saturdays, Sundays, and federal\nlegal public holidays observed within the response window. Without limitation, the following\nfederal holidays falling within response windows relevant to the current filing period are recognized\nas non-business days and shall not be counted:\nHoliday\nObserved Date\nMemorial Day\n05/26/2025\nJuneteenth National Independence Day\n06/19/2025\nIndependence Day\n07/04/2025\nThe Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation shall be served in writing (certified mail or\nverified electronic delivery) and shall state the date of issuance, the response deadline computed\nunder this section, and the consequence of non-response. The Company shall maintain a log of all\nnotices issued, dates of service, and dispositions.\n\n\n---\n\nAlleniom Utilities — APUC-Filed Interconnection Tariff · §12 Interconnection\nVolume III · Sheet No. 214\nAPUC Tariff Filing — Alleniom Utilities Electric Tariff, Vol. III, §12\nPage 4 of 6\n§12.5 Construction-Payment Defaults\nWhere a construction work order or interconnection facilities invoice becomes payable and the\ncustomer has not remitted the amount due, the Company shall inform the customer in writing of\nthe outstanding balance and shall issue a payment deadline of thirty (30) business days from\nthe date of that written notice.\nFor so long as the thirty (30) business-day payment deadline is running, the affected project shall\nremain on hold in the queue and shall NOT be deemed withdrawn. The construction-payment\ndefault procedure under this §12.5 is distinct from, and shall not be conflated with, the\ndeemed-withdrawal procedure under §12.4; the two mechanisms have different triggers, different\nconsequences, and different cure paths.\nUpon receipt of full payment prior to expiration of the deadline, the Company shall promptly\nrelease the hold and resume the affected work in queue order. If the deadline expires without\npayment, the Company may pursue such remedies as are available under the applicable\nInterconnection Service Agreement and this tariff, including, as a separate and subsequent step,\nissuance of a Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation under §12.4.\n\n\n---\n\nAlleniom Utilities — APUC-Filed Interconnection Tariff · §12 Interconnection\nVolume III · Sheet No. 214\nAPUC Tariff Filing — Alleniom Utilities Electric Tariff, Vol. III, §12\nPage 5 of 6\n§12.6 Interconnection Service Agreement\nInterconnection service shall commence only upon execution of an Interconnection Service\nAgreement (“ISA”) and the Company's written Permission to Operate. Material terms of an\nexecuted ISA, including but not limited to nameplate facility capacity and the designated point of\ninterconnection, may not be modified by amendment. Post-execution changes to such material\nterms require withdrawal of the affected application and re-application in accordance with the DER\nInterconnection Standard.\n§12.7 Nondiscriminatory Queue-Order Processing of Applications\nApplications shall be processed by the Company in queue order on a nondiscriminatory basis.\nNo applicant shall be advanced or disadvantaged in the processing sequence by reason of\nidentity, affiliation, customer class, geography, or any other factor not expressly provided for in this\ntariff or in the DER Interconnection Standard.\nScope note. This §12.7 applies to APPLICATION PROCESSING only — that is, to the\nsequencing of screening, technical review, study assignment, and issuance of interconnection\ndeterminations. Utility construction sequencing and the scheduling and allocation of constrained\nutility-owned equipment (including, without limitation, remote terminal units,\npoint-of-interconnection reclosers, and other supply-limited devices) are not governed by this\nsection. Such matters are addressed under the Company's operating guidelines, including OG-17\n(Equipment Deployment Prioritization Guideline), which sets forth the criticality factors and\nprioritization criteria applicable to constrained equipment. Nothing in this §12.7 shall be construed\nto require the Company to sequence construction or equipment deployment in queue order where\nOG-17 or another operating guideline provides a different criticality-based ordering.\n\n\n---\n\nAlleniom Utilities — APUC-Filed Interconnection Tariff · §12 Interconnection\nVolume III · Sheet No. 214\nAPUC Tariff Filing — Alleniom Utilities Electric Tariff, Vol. III, §12\nPage 6 of 6\n§12.8 Reservation\nThe Company reserves the right to seek modification of this section through filing with the Alleron\nPublic Utilities Commission. Nothing in this tariff limits any authority reserved to the Commission\nby statute, rule, or order.\nIssued by: Director, Regulatory Affairs, Alleniom Utilities\nFiled pursuant to: Order of the Alleron Public Utilities Commission\nDocket / Sheet Reference: APUC — Alleniom Utilities Electric Tariff, Volume III, §12 (Interconnection\nProvisions)	<h1 id="tariff-excerpt-s12-interconnection">Tariff Excerpt S12 Interconnection</h1>\n<p>APUC Tariff Filing — Alleniom Utilities Electric Tariff, Vol. III, §12 Page 1 of 6<br>\nALLENIOM UTILITIES<br>\nAPUC-Filed Interconnection Tariff<br>\n§12 Interconnection Provisions<br>\nFiled with the Alleron Public Utilities Commission<br>\nElectric Tariff — Volume III · Original Sheet No. 214 · Effective: March 1, 2024<br>\nSECTION 12 — INTERCONNECTION OF DISTRIBUTED ENERGY<br>\nRESOURCES<br>\n§12.1 Applicability<br>\nThis section governs the interconnection to Alleniom Utilities' (the “Company”) distribution system<br>\nof distributed energy resources (“DER”) within the Alleron service territory. Technical<br>\nrequirements, screens, study procedures, and equipment standards referenced herein are set out<br>\nin the Company's DER Interconnection Standard (DIS-01) and companion standards, each of<br>\nwhich is incorporated by reference. Nothing in this section shall be construed to modify or<br>\nsupersede any applicable safety, reliability, or protection requirement of the Company's<br>\nengineering standards.<br>\nFiled by: Alleniom Utilities · 4200 Veldmere Boulevard, Halston, Alleron 01HA</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Alleniom Utilities — APUC-Filed Interconnection Tariff · §12 Interconnection<br>\nVolume III · Sheet No. 214<br>\nAPUC Tariff Filing — Alleniom Utilities Electric Tariff, Vol. III, §12<br>\nPage 2 of 6<br>\n§12.2 Scope of the DER Interconnection Program<br>\nThe DER Interconnection Program administered under this section applies to applications for DER<br>\ninterconnection at or above five hundred kilowatts (≥500 kW). Within the Program, DER shall be<br>\nclassified according to the DER Interconnection Standard as follows:<br>\n(a)  Commercial small-scale DER — five hundred kilowatts to one megawatt (500 kW – 1<br>\nMW);<br>\n(b)  Medium-scale DER — one megawatt to five megawatts (1 MW – 5 MW).<br>\nThe Program scope threshold is ≥500 kW. DER below five hundred kilowatts (sub-500 kW),<br>\nwhether commercial or residential, are not administered under this section and shall instead be<br>\nprocessed under the Company's Simplified Small-Systems Process, which is filed separately<br>\nand is outside the DER Interconnection Program.<br>\nAggregation, controlled-capacity recognition, and shared point-of-interconnection eligibility are<br>\naddressed in the DER Interconnection Standard and shall control the classification of any facility<br>\nwhose nameplate rating, aggregate site rating, or point of common coupling brings it within<br>\nProgram scope.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Alleniom Utilities — APUC-Filed Interconnection Tariff · §12 Interconnection<br>\nVolume III · Sheet No. 214<br>\nAPUC Tariff Filing — Alleniom Utilities Electric Tariff, Vol. III, §12<br>\nPage 3 of 6<br>\n§12.3 Application Intake and Queue Position<br>\nA complete application shall establish the applicant's queue position as of the date the Company<br>\ndetermines the application to be complete. Applications determined to be incomplete shall be<br>\nreturned with a written deficiency notice identifying each item requiring cure and the date by which<br>\ncure is required. Queue position is not transferable between applications except as expressly<br>\nprovided in the DER Interconnection Standard.<br>\n§12.4 Deemed Withdrawal Following Notice of Impending Withdrawal or Cancellation<br>\nUpon issuance of a formal written Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation by the<br>\nCompany, the applicant shall have thirty (30) business days from the date of the notice to submit<br>\na substantive response curing the matter identified therein. If the applicant fails to respond within<br>\nthirty (30) business days, the application shall be deemed withdrawn and shall be closed from the<br>\nactive queue without further action of the Company.<br>\nFor purposes of this section, “business days” shall exclude Saturdays, Sundays, and federal<br>\nlegal public holidays observed within the response window. Without limitation, the following<br>\nfederal holidays falling within response windows relevant to the current filing period are recognized<br>\nas non-business days and shall not be counted:<br>\nHoliday<br>\nObserved Date<br>\nMemorial Day<br>\n05/26/2025<br>\nJuneteenth National Independence Day<br>\n06/19/2025<br>\nIndependence Day<br>\n07/04/2025<br>\nThe Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation shall be served in writing (certified mail or<br>\nverified electronic delivery) and shall state the date of issuance, the response deadline computed<br>\nunder this section, and the consequence of non-response. The Company shall maintain a log of all<br>\nnotices issued, dates of service, and dispositions.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Alleniom Utilities — APUC-Filed Interconnection Tariff · §12 Interconnection<br>\nVolume III · Sheet No. 214<br>\nAPUC Tariff Filing — Alleniom Utilities Electric Tariff, Vol. III, §12<br>\nPage 4 of 6<br>\n§12.5 Construction-Payment Defaults<br>\nWhere a construction work order or interconnection facilities invoice becomes payable and the<br>\ncustomer has not remitted the amount due, the Company shall inform the customer in writing of<br>\nthe outstanding balance and shall issue a payment deadline of thirty (30) business days from<br>\nthe date of that written notice.<br>\nFor so long as the thirty (30) business-day payment deadline is running, the affected project shall<br>\nremain on hold in the queue and shall NOT be deemed withdrawn. The construction-payment<br>\ndefault procedure under this §12.5 is distinct from, and shall not be conflated with, the<br>\ndeemed-withdrawal procedure under §12.4; the two mechanisms have different triggers, different<br>\nconsequences, and different cure paths.<br>\nUpon receipt of full payment prior to expiration of the deadline, the Company shall promptly<br>\nrelease the hold and resume the affected work in queue order. If the deadline expires without<br>\npayment, the Company may pursue such remedies as are available under the applicable<br>\nInterconnection Service Agreement and this tariff, including, as a separate and subsequent step,<br>\nissuance of a Notice of Impending Withdrawal/Cancellation under §12.4.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Alleniom Utilities — APUC-Filed Interconnection Tariff · §12 Interconnection<br>\nVolume III · Sheet No. 214<br>\nAPUC Tariff Filing — Alleniom Utilities Electric Tariff, Vol. III, §12<br>\nPage 5 of 6<br>\n§12.6 Interconnection Service Agreement<br>\nInterconnection service shall commence only upon execution of an Interconnection Service<br>\nAgreement (“ISA”) and the Company's written Permission to Operate. Material terms of an<br>\nexecuted ISA, including but not limited to nameplate facility capacity and the designated point of<br>\ninterconnection, may not be modified by amendment. Post-execution changes to such material<br>\nterms require withdrawal of the affected application and re-application in accordance with the DER<br>\nInterconnection Standard.<br>\n§12.7 Nondiscriminatory Queue-Order Processing of Applications<br>\nApplications shall be processed by the Company in queue order on a nondiscriminatory basis.<br>\nNo applicant shall be advanced or disadvantaged in the processing sequence by reason of<br>\nidentity, affiliation, customer class, geography, or any other factor not expressly provided for in this<br>\ntariff or in the DER Interconnection Standard.<br>\nScope note. This §12.7 applies to APPLICATION PROCESSING only — that is, to the<br>\nsequencing of screening, technical review, study assignment, and issuance of interconnection<br>\ndeterminations. Utility construction sequencing and the scheduling and allocation of constrained<br>\nutility-owned equipment (including, without limitation, remote terminal units,<br>\npoint-of-interconnection reclosers, and other supply-limited devices) are not governed by this<br>\nsection. Such matters are addressed under the Company's operating guidelines, including OG-17<br>\n(Equipment Deployment Prioritization Guideline), which sets forth the criticality factors and<br>\nprioritization criteria applicable to constrained equipment. Nothing in this §12.7 shall be construed<br>\nto require the Company to sequence construction or equipment deployment in queue order where<br>\nOG-17 or another operating guideline provides a different criticality-based ordering.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Alleniom Utilities — APUC-Filed Interconnection Tariff · §12 Interconnection<br>\nVolume III · Sheet No. 214<br>\nAPUC Tariff Filing — Alleniom Utilities Electric Tariff, Vol. III, §12<br>\nPage 6 of 6<br>\n§12.8 Reservation<br>\nThe Company reserves the right to seek modification of this section through filing with the Alleron<br>\nPublic Utilities Commission. Nothing in this tariff limits any authority reserved to the Commission<br>\nby statute, rule, or order.<br>\nIssued by: Director, Regulatory Affairs, Alleniom Utilities<br>\nFiled pursuant to: Order of the Alleron Public Utilities Commission<br>\nDocket / Sheet Reference: APUC — Alleniom Utilities Electric Tariff, Volume III, §12 (Interconnection<br>\nProvisions)</p>	[]	markdown	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	markdown	en	4	4	{}
50	der-program/standards/witness-test-waiver-procedure	939ee5e6a417f7a837d9	Witness Test Waiver Procedure	Procedure for witness-test waivers requiring equipment to be on current CEL edition	f	t				# Witness Test Waiver Procedure\n\nENG-PRC-014 Rev 3  ·  Alleniom Utilities DER Program Office Page 1\nALLENIOM UTILITIES — DER PROGRAM OFFICE\nDER WITNESS TEST & WAIVER PROCEDURE\nDoc no. ENG-PRC-014 · Rev 3 · 04/30/2025 · Owner: Standards Engineering (Evan Solberg)\n§1  PURPOSE\nThis procedure establishes when Alleniom Utilities personnel must witness commissioning tests of a\ncustomer’s Distributed Energy Resource (DER) prior to issuance of Permission to Operate (PTO), and\nwhen the on-site witness test may be waived on the basis of the equipment installed at the facility. It is the\ncontrolling document for the DER Program commissioning group and for Standards Engineering waiver\ndispositions.\nCompanion documents: Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard DIS-01 Rev 4; Telemetry &\nCommunications Standard TCS-02; the current edition of the Alleniom Certified Equipment List (CEL).\n§2  SCOPE\nApplies to every in-scope DER facility interconnecting to the Alleniom distribution system under the DER\nProgram (commercial small-scale 500 kW – 1 MW and medium-scale 1 MW – 5 MW). Domestic rooftop\nDER and sub-500 kW facilities routed through the Simplified Small-Systems Process are outside the\nscope of this procedure.\nApplies to the initial commissioning of a new interconnection, to re-commissioning following a capacity\nchange or protective-equipment replacement, and to the disposition of any request to waive the on-site\nwitness test in favor of an accepted commissioning-agent test record.\n§3  WAIVER ELIGIBILITY RULE\nWitness-test waivers apply ONLY to equipment listed on the CURRENT edition of the Certified Equipment\nList at the waiver date. Equipment not carried on the current CEL edition requires an on-site witness test\nbefore Permission to Operate (PTO) is issued. Listings appearing solely on a superseded CEL edition do\nNOT confer waiver eligibility on the current-edition date.\nThe following are corollaries of the rule and are provided for the avoidance of doubt:\n1. The “waiver date” is the date on which Standards Engineering signs the waiver disposition, not the\ndate on which the applicant filed the interconnection application, executed the ISA, or completed\nconstruction.\n2. Every protective device and every inverter (or power conversion system) installed at the facility must\nappear on the current CEL edition. A facility with any single unlisted component is ineligible.\n3. Withdrawal of an equipment listing at the time of a CEL republication is a substantive event. A listing\nthat was carried on a prior edition and was not carried forward into the successor edition confers no\nwaiver eligibility once the successor edition takes effect.\n4. Front-matter footnotes, cross-references, or historical annotations in the current CEL that describe a\nprior edition’s contents are informational only. They do not extend eligibility to models that are not\nthemselves listed in the body of the current edition.\nAlleniom Utilities · DER Program Office · 4200 Veldmere Boulevard, Halston, Alleron 01HA · (612) 555-0100 · alleniom.com\n\n---\n\nENG-PRC-014 Rev 3  ·  Alleniom Utilities DER Program Office\nPage 2\n5.\nThe CEL edition in force on the waiver date is the sole reference. A model that was listed at the time\nan application was filed, but is no longer listed on the waiver date, requires the on-site witness test.\n§4  PROCEDURE\n§4.1  Look up model against current CEL.\nThe commissioning coordinator retrieves the current edition of the Certified Equipment List from the\nStandards Engineering document repository. The coordinator confirms the edition identifier and the\n“supersedes” reference on the CEL cover page, and verifies that no more recent edition has been\npublished between the file date shown on the coordinator’s copy and the waiver disposition date. For each\ninverter and each protective device installed at the facility, the coordinator locates the exact make, model,\nand firmware family in the body of the current CEL and records the CEL row reference on the\ncommissioning checklist.\nIf any installed model is not present in the body of the current CEL — including any model that appears\nsolely on a superseded CEL edition — the facility is ineligible for a witness-test waiver and an on-site\nwitness test is required prior to PTO. Superseded editions of the CEL are archived and are not to be used\nto establish eligibility under this section. The coordinator marks the commissioning checklist “witness test\nrequired” and forwards to §4.2.\nWhere every installed model is confirmed on the current CEL, the coordinator marks the checklist “waiver\neligible” and forwards to Standards Engineering for written disposition. Waiver disposition remains at the\ndiscretion of Standards Engineering; a positive eligibility determination under this section is a necessary\nbut not sufficient condition for issuance of a waiver.\n§4.2  Schedule an on-site witness test.\nWhere a witness test is required, the commissioning coordinator schedules the test with the customer or\nthe customer’s commissioning agent, targeting the earliest mutually available date consistent with the\napplicant’s construction-complete status and the availability of an Alleniom Utilities witness engineer.\nTesting shall be performed per IEEE 1547.1 and shall include, at minimum, the protective-function trip\ntests, the anti-islanding test, and the ride-through verifications specified in DIS-01 Rev 4 §6. The customer\nor commissioning agent supplies the test plan, calibrated instrumentation, and the qualified personnel to\noperate the equipment; the Alleniom witness engineer observes, records, and dispositions the test in\naccordance with §4.3.\nWhere the required on-site witness test cannot be scheduled prior to the applicant’s targeted energization\ndate, the facility remains without PTO. On-site witness testing is not to be deferred to a post-PTO date\nunder any circumstance, and PTO is not to be issued conditionally against a future witness test.\n§4.3  Documentation.\nThe commissioning coordinator maintains a single commissioning record for each facility. The record shall\ninclude, at minimum:\n\nthe completed commissioning checklist, including the CEL edition identifier consulted, the CEL row\nreference for each listed model, and the “waiver eligible” or “witness test required” determination;\n\nfor waiver-eligible facilities, the written Standards Engineering waiver disposition, signed and dated\nby the Standards Engineer of record;\n\n\n---\n\nENG-PRC-014 Rev 3  ·  Alleniom Utilities DER Program Office\nPage 3\n\nfor witness-tested facilities, the witness engineer’s test record, including instrument identifiers,\nmeasured values, pass/fail dispositions per test point, and the witness engineer’s signature and\ndate;\n\nthe manufacturer’s certificate of conformance, factory test report, or equivalent evidence supporting\nthe CEL listing relied upon;\n\ncorrespondence with the customer or commissioning agent bearing on scheduling, deferrals, or\ntest-plan negotiation.\nThe commissioning record is retained in the facility interconnection file and is a prerequisite to PTO\nissuance by the System Operations Center. PTO shall not be issued in the absence of either (a) a signed\nwitness-test disposition or (b) a signed waiver disposition supported by a §4.1 eligibility determination\nagainst the current CEL edition.\nEnd of procedure. See DIS-01 Rev 4 for interconnection standards, TCS-02 for telemetry requirements, and the\ncurrent CEL edition for the list of qualifying equipment. Revisions to this procedure are issued by Standards\nEngineering and take effect on the date shown in the document-control block above.	<h1 id="witness-test-waiver-procedure">Witness Test Waiver Procedure</h1>\n<p>ENG-PRC-014 Rev 3  ·  Alleniom Utilities DER Program Office Page 1<br>\nALLENIOM UTILITIES — DER PROGRAM OFFICE<br>\nDER WITNESS TEST &amp; WAIVER PROCEDURE<br>\nDoc no. ENG-PRC-014 · Rev 3 · 04/30/2025 · Owner: Standards Engineering (Evan Solberg)<br>\n§1  PURPOSE<br>\nThis procedure establishes when Alleniom Utilities personnel must witness commissioning tests of a<br>\ncustomer’s Distributed Energy Resource (DER) prior to issuance of Permission to Operate (PTO), and<br>\nwhen the on-site witness test may be waived on the basis of the equipment installed at the facility. It is the<br>\ncontrolling document for the DER Program commissioning group and for Standards Engineering waiver<br>\ndispositions.<br>\nCompanion documents: Alleniom DER Interconnection Standard DIS-01 Rev 4; Telemetry &amp;<br>\nCommunications Standard TCS-02; the current edition of the Alleniom Certified Equipment List (CEL).<br>\n§2  SCOPE<br>\nApplies to every in-scope DER facility interconnecting to the Alleniom distribution system under the DER<br>\nProgram (commercial small-scale 500 kW – 1 MW and medium-scale 1 MW – 5 MW). Domestic rooftop<br>\nDER and sub-500 kW facilities routed through the Simplified Small-Systems Process are outside the<br>\nscope of this procedure.<br>\nApplies to the initial commissioning of a new interconnection, to re-commissioning following a capacity<br>\nchange or protective-equipment replacement, and to the disposition of any request to waive the on-site<br>\nwitness test in favor of an accepted commissioning-agent test record.<br>\n§3  WAIVER ELIGIBILITY RULE<br>\nWitness-test waivers apply ONLY to equipment listed on the CURRENT edition of the Certified Equipment<br>\nList at the waiver date. Equipment not carried on the current CEL edition requires an on-site witness test<br>\nbefore Permission to Operate (PTO) is issued. Listings appearing solely on a superseded CEL edition do<br>\nNOT confer waiver eligibility on the current-edition date.<br>\nThe following are corollaries of the rule and are provided for the avoidance of doubt:<br>\n1. The “waiver date” is the date on which Standards Engineering signs the waiver disposition, not the<br>\ndate on which the applicant filed the interconnection application, executed the ISA, or completed<br>\nconstruction.<br>\n2. Every protective device and every inverter (or power conversion system) installed at the facility must<br>\nappear on the current CEL edition. A facility with any single unlisted component is ineligible.<br>\n3. Withdrawal of an equipment listing at the time of a CEL republication is a substantive event. A listing<br>\nthat was carried on a prior edition and was not carried forward into the successor edition confers no<br>\nwaiver eligibility once the successor edition takes effect.<br>\n4. Front-matter footnotes, cross-references, or historical annotations in the current CEL that describe a<br>\nprior edition’s contents are informational only. They do not extend eligibility to models that are not<br>\nthemselves listed in the body of the current edition.<br>\nAlleniom Utilities · DER Program Office · 4200 Veldmere Boulevard, Halston, Alleron 01HA · (612) 555-0100 · alleniom.com</p>\n<hr>\n<p>ENG-PRC-014 Rev 3  ·  Alleniom Utilities DER Program Office<br>\nPage 2<br>\n5.<br>\nThe CEL edition in force on the waiver date is the sole reference. A model that was listed at the time<br>\nan application was filed, but is no longer listed on the waiver date, requires the on-site witness test.<br>\n§4  PROCEDURE<br>\n§4.1  Look up model against current CEL.<br>\nThe commissioning coordinator retrieves the current edition of the Certified Equipment List from the<br>\nStandards Engineering document repository. The coordinator confirms the edition identifier and the<br>\n“supersedes” reference on the CEL cover page, and verifies that no more recent edition has been<br>\npublished between the file date shown on the coordinator’s copy and the waiver disposition date. For each<br>\ninverter and each protective device installed at the facility, the coordinator locates the exact make, model,<br>\nand firmware family in the body of the current CEL and records the CEL row reference on the<br>\ncommissioning checklist.<br>\nIf any installed model is not present in the body of the current CEL — including any model that appears<br>\nsolely on a superseded CEL edition — the facility is ineligible for a witness-test waiver and an on-site<br>\nwitness test is required prior to PTO. Superseded editions of the CEL are archived and are not to be used<br>\nto establish eligibility under this section. The coordinator marks the commissioning checklist “witness test<br>\nrequired” and forwards to §4.2.<br>\nWhere every installed model is confirmed on the current CEL, the coordinator marks the checklist “waiver<br>\neligible” and forwards to Standards Engineering for written disposition. Waiver disposition remains at the<br>\ndiscretion of Standards Engineering; a positive eligibility determination under this section is a necessary<br>\nbut not sufficient condition for issuance of a waiver.<br>\n§4.2  Schedule an on-site witness test.<br>\nWhere a witness test is required, the commissioning coordinator schedules the test with the customer or<br>\nthe customer’s commissioning agent, targeting the earliest mutually available date consistent with the<br>\napplicant’s construction-complete status and the availability of an Alleniom Utilities witness engineer.<br>\nTesting shall be performed per IEEE 1547.1 and shall include, at minimum, the protective-function trip<br>\ntests, the anti-islanding test, and the ride-through verifications specified in DIS-01 Rev 4 §6. The customer<br>\nor commissioning agent supplies the test plan, calibrated instrumentation, and the qualified personnel to<br>\noperate the equipment; the Alleniom witness engineer observes, records, and dispositions the test in<br>\naccordance with §4.3.<br>\nWhere the required on-site witness test cannot be scheduled prior to the applicant’s targeted energization<br>\ndate, the facility remains without PTO. On-site witness testing is not to be deferred to a post-PTO date<br>\nunder any circumstance, and PTO is not to be issued conditionally against a future witness test.<br>\n§4.3  Documentation.<br>\nThe commissioning coordinator maintains a single commissioning record for each facility. The record shall<br>\ninclude, at minimum:<br>\n<br>\nthe completed commissioning checklist, including the CEL edition identifier consulted, the CEL row<br>\nreference for each listed model, and the “waiver eligible” or “witness test required” determination;<br>\n<br>\nfor waiver-eligible facilities, the written Standards Engineering waiver disposition, signed and dated<br>\nby the Standards Engineer of record;</p>\n<hr>\n<p>ENG-PRC-014 Rev 3  ·  Alleniom Utilities DER Program Office<br>\nPage 3<br>\n<br>\nfor witness-tested facilities, the witness engineer’s test record, including instrument identifiers,<br>\nmeasured values, pass/fail dispositions per test point, and the witness engineer’s signature and<br>\ndate;<br>\n<br>\nthe manufacturer’s certificate of conformance, factory test report, or equivalent evidence supporting<br>\nthe CEL listing relied upon;<br>\n<br>\ncorrespondence with the customer or commissioning agent bearing on scheduling, deferrals, or<br>\ntest-plan negotiation.<br>\nThe commissioning record is retained in the facility interconnection file and is a prerequisite to PTO<br>\nissuance by the System Operations Center. PTO shall not be issued in the absence of either (a) a signed<br>\nwitness-test disposition or (b) a signed waiver disposition supported by a §4.1 eligibility determination<br>\nagainst the current CEL edition.<br>\nEnd of procedure. See DIS-01 Rev 4 for interconnection standards, TCS-02 for telemetry requirements, and the<br>\ncurrent CEL edition for the list of qualifying equipment. Revisions to this procedure are issued by Standards<br>\nEngineering and take effect on the date shown in the document-control block above.</p>	[]	markdown	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	2026-11-20T11:40:53.219780+00:00	markdown	en	9	9	{}
